\par\par \b \uc2 = \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Curr Acct Data Highlight Funding Woes For Dlr   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 856 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Juni 2004\par 03:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--After the record U.S. trade gap announced earlier in the week, it wasn't a shock to see data Friday showing that the current account ballooned to its widest-level ever in the first quarter.   \par \par \uc2 Equally, it was hardly surprising to see the dollar weaken sharply in response to the numbers, which show the U.S. needs increasing amounts of foreign financing to cover these external deficits.   \par \par \uc2 The current account, the country's broadest measure of trade with the rest of the world, widened to a record $144.9 billion in the first quarter from $127.0 billion the previous quarter, the Commerce Department said Friday. This is roughly 5.1% of the U.S. gross domestic product.   \par \par \uc2 And Monday, the Commerce Department also revealed that the trade deficit in April widened to a record $48.33 billion. The trade account comprises the vast bulk of the current account.   \par \par \uc2 Many observers rightly point out that current account and trade data are backward looking and offer few clues to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's future direction. And although foreign capital inflows into the U.S. appear to be diminishing, they still more than adequately plug the gaps.   \par \par \uc2 But there are serious doubts as to how long this can continue, as the dollar's sharp slide to intraday lows against most counterparts Friday shows.   \par \par \uc2 As strong economic expansion means demand for imports will continue to easily outpace export growth, these gaps are likely to widen further still, making the question of how these deficits are financed more acute.   \par \par \uc2 'The U.S. current account deficit for the first quarter of the year shows the dollar in a much more vulnerable position than believed,' wrote UBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts in a research note. 'The numbers indicate that funding is becoming increasingly more precarious and short-term in maturity. There is no sign of a build up of long-term funding and an alarming proportion of the deficit is covered by official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'   \par \par \uc2 At 2:00 pm EDT Friday, the euro was up at $1.2131 from $1.2053 late Thursday in New York, and the pound was up at $1.8397 from $1.8359. The dollar was down at CHF1.2438 from CHF1.2507 late Thursday and Y108.71 compared with Y109.60 late Thursday in New York. The dollar hit a new six-week low against the yen of Y108.57 a short while ago Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Official Inflows To Dry Up   \par \par \uc2 Massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Asian central banks, most notably the record amount of yen-selling from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was a feature of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the first quarter. The monetary authorities sold their own \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars in order to boost exports and foster economic growth, and mostly recycled the proceeds back into U.S. Treasurys.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of U.S. assets rose a whopping $125.2 billion in the first quarter but that almost certainly won't be repeated. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has virtually ceased - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-March - and indeed U.S. Treasury flows data Tuesday showed that official purchases of U.S. assets almost halved in April from the previous month.   \par \par \uc2 This surge in \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying helped mask the deterioration in dollar-positive flows elsewhere. Net foreign purchases of non-Treasury U.S. securities fell to $62.3 billion from $83.8 billion in the fourth quarter, owing to sizable declines in equity and corporate bond purchases.   \par \par \uc2 And net foreign direct investment flows were dollar-negative for the third consecutive quarter. A net $39 billion flowed out of the U.S. in the three months to March, or an annualized $156 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Alan Ruskin, director at 4Cast, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research group in New York, says the combination of declining official inflows and faltering private sector inflows make funding the current account deficit a 'mammoth' task. 'Private flows have never covered such a large deficit. This does not bode well for the dollar longer-term,' Ruskin said.   \par \par \uc2 And with U.S. interest rates about to rise, attracting sufficient private investment into U.S. asset markets might prove difficult. The Dow Jones Industrials Average is virtually flat so far this year and could struggle to climb as higher interest rates put the brakes on corporate borrowing and investment and hold back earnings growth.   \par \par \uc2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York, doesn't see much short-term risk to the dollar but recognizes the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's structural deficiencies outlined by Friday's data.   \par \par \uc2 'The dollar's only hope of a long-term recovery is if: 1) the U.S. current account is reduced, 2) net FDI turns positive, and 3) foreign investors step up purchases of U.S. equities,' he wrote in a research note Friday.   \par \par \uc2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in New York, has also reported on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)   \par \par \uc2 -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020040621e06l001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
