\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Trade Data Highlight Dollar's Structural Drag  \b0\par\par\uc2 975 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Juni 2004\par 20:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar may be getting a lift from the prospect of U.S. interest rates finally going up from historical lows but a blast from the past Monday reminded investors just why the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is still in a multi-year downtrend.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Driven by a sizable decline in exports, the U.S. trade deficit with the rest of the world widened to a record $48.33 billion in April, data released by the Commerce Department showed. That equates to over 5% of gross domestic product on an annualized basis and much worse than the $45 billion deficit economists had expected. The trade account is the lion's share of the current account, a broader measure of the country's trade with the rest of the world.  \par \par \uc2 This structural imbalance - the huge gap means the U.S. must consistently attract massive amounts of foreign capital just to balance its books - was a major factor behind the dollar's depreciation over the last couple of years. And while the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's short-term prospects look bright on hopes that the Federal Reserve will tighten monetary policy as soon as June 30, the data show its medium-term horizon to be that little bit cloudier.  \par \par \uc2 "It's obviously a lot worse than expected and in that regard a reminder of the problems the dollar faces in the medium term," said Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The market will get the latest snapshot of foreigners' appetite for U.S. assets on Tuesday, when the U.S. Treasury will release securities flows data for April. A net $87.6 billion flowed into the country in March but the outlook for April may be difficult to accurately forecast. Private sector demand for U.S. fixed income assets appears strong but foreign central banks, most notably \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, are thought to have scaled back \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months and as a result, purchases of U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Noting the persistent U.S. deficit with the euro zone, Japan and China, John Vail, senior strategist at Mizuho Securities in Chicago, thinks the dollar has an underlying drag. "(Downward) Pressure on the dollar still prevails despite recent moves in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets," Vail wrote in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The moves he's referring to have been the dollar's appreciation in recent weeks as tough talk from several Fed officials, including Chairman Alan Greenspan, prompted traders to price in more aggressive rate hikes than had been expected. Higher official interest rates - the expectation of rate hikes has already pushed up bond market yields - would make it more attractive to buy the dollar and hold U.S. assets.  \par \par \uc2 Keen to show the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to keeping inflation under control, Greenspan and others have indicated the Fed will do "what is required" to maintain price stability. This indicates their pledge to move at a "measured" pace isn't necessarily a given.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    China Gap Widens \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar traded at multiweek highs against the euro, and Australian dollar, amongst others, earlier Monday as those interest rate expectations continued to buoy the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the trade figures halted it in its tracks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro shot up to an intraday high of $1.2085 from around $1.2000 before the numbers were released, sterling bounced up to $1.8175 from around $1.8080 (but is still weaker on the day) and the dollar fell against the Swiss franc to CHF1.2565 from around CHF1.2630.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York, said the figures were bad for the dollar not only for the obvious reasons, but also because the trade report suggests inflation is well under control.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Inflation, in particular (high) oil prices, haven't factored themselves into the figures," which may remove some of the expectations of the Fed needing to raise rates in a more aggressive manner, Woolfolk said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Energy imports in April fell to $12.67 billion from $13.72 billion the previous month, even though the price of crude oil rose. Instead, "China is the culprit...and this could be the first credible piece of evidence of a Chinese (economic) slowdown," Woolfolk said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data show that the U.S. trade deficit with China widened to $12.0 billion in April from $10.4 billion in March, as Chinese demand for U.S. goods fell nearly 20%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If China's overheating economy is finally beginning to cool, one of the biggest losers over the longer term in terms of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could be the yen, as Japan is China's largest trading partner. That could partly explain why the yen is weaker across the board on Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Lynch at BNP Paribas and BoNY's Woolfolk both expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers will have forgotten the trade data within a day or two as they turn their attention to the U.S. consumer price index Tuesday and other data scheduled before the Fed's interest rate decision on June 30.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Andreas Mann, senior dealer at Commerzbank in New York, agrees there are other issues facing the dollar but says the trade report could still hold some sway over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if its near-term outlook bleakens.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A break of $1.1750 (support for the euro) is needed soon or else the market will revert back to what we had last year," he said, referring to concerns about the U.S. external deficits. The euro is currently trading at $1.2065.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 06-14-04 1406ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040614e06e0002s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
