\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Turns, Weakens Vs Europe As Long Weekend Looms  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 741 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Juni 2004\par 18:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is weakening sharply versus the major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sterling in particular, midsession Thursday in New York, giving back some of the previous day's gains as dealers square positions ahead of a de facto long weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of long-weekend squaring because tomorrow (Friday) is virtually a non-trading day," said Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp. in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "No one's too keen to hold dollars over the long weekend," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales trader at a European bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 After posting huge gains Wednesday in a delayed reaction to relatively hawkish comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan the previous day, there was a lot of ground to give back.  \par \par \uc2 With the pound showing a belated reaction to the Bank of England's rate hike and the New Zealand and Australian dollars already soaring from a similar move by the Reserve Bank of New Zealand earlier Thursday, the greenback is now under pressure across the board.  \par \par \uc2 At 12:50 a.m. EDT (1650 GMT), the euro was at $1.2105, up from $1.2043 late Wednesday in New York and almost a cent up from the $1.2024 two-week low plumbed earlier Thursday. The euro was down against the yen but paring its losses, trading at Y132.35 compared with Y133.10 late Wednesday and Y131.56 earlier Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker against the yen at Y109.35 compared with Y110.49 late Wednesday and off against the Swiss franc at CHF1.2436 from CHF1.2552. Sterling was back up at $1.8403 from $1.8295 late Wednesday in New York and a low Thursday of $1.8190.  \par \par \uc2 Several layers of stop-loss selling of dollars against the euro, pound and Swiss franc in particular may have been exaggerated by thinning liquidity. Most U.S. financial markets are closed Friday in honor of former President Ronald Reagan's funeral.  \par \par \uc2 The euro is now approaching $1.2135, considered a key technical level. A close Friday above that level will keep its bullish trend intact, according to Robert Sinche, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar had gotten a boost in U.S. trading earlier Thursday from U.S. economic data hinting at building inflationary pressures, which could spur more aggressive action from the Fed. Against a backdrop of rampant oil prices, import prices in May shot up by 1.6%, twice as fast as economists were expecting. Following Grenspan's pledge Tuesday that the Fed is "prepared to do what is required" to maintain price stability, this was enough to lift the dollar further.  \par \par \uc2 But reports of official European bids for euros around $1.2020 as well as an official European name checking euro rates against the Swiss franc at its fresh 14-month lows of CHF1.5060 helped the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rebound.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was already stronger on the back data showing a massive leap in Japanese core machinery orders in April, and a subsequent rise in the Nikkei of over 1%. Talk of an influential insider report circulating in the market, on Japanese monetary authorities' preferred levels for the dollar against the yen, isn't having too much impact.  \par \par \uc2 "I wouldn't put too much credence on it," said a Japanese bank dealer. "Price action has been fairly muted this session, with euro/yen taking the brunt of the moves."  \par \par \uc2 According to dealers who have seen the report, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  are said to be expected to crank up verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the strength of the yen if the dollar trades as low as Y108, before actually \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell yen at Y105. Their preferred range for dollar/yen is Y110-Y120, said the dealers, citing the report.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling is bouncing back from the selloff triggered by the BoE's decision to raise its benchmark repo rate by 25 basis points to 4.50%. Although more hikes are in the pipeline, the pound suffered from a "buy the rumor, sell the fact" move.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a cliche, but it's true," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at ING Financial Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096 jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com [ 06-10-04 1258ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040610e06a0002a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
