\par\par \b \uc2 Asian  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  reserves level off in April and May.  \b0\par\par\uc2 717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2004\par 10:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     By John Mair\par     SINGAPORE, June 8 (Reuters) - The surge in Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves stalled in April and May, latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par figures show, as a rebounding U.S. dollar lessened the need for\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening.\par     Analysts say an outflow of capital from Asia as investors\par position for expected U.S. rate rises has also eased the need\par for central banks to build up reserves. As a region, Asia holds\par the vast majority of the world's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves.\par     "I think there is an element of a pause because the market\par has gone the right way for them," said Robert Rennie, senior\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Westpac Bank in Sydney.\par     Excluding China, Asian reserves totalled $1.718 trillion in\par May, compared with $1.711 trillion in April and $1.716 trillion\par in March, the latest figures from central banks show.\par  \par     The basically steady holdings were in stark contrast to the\par rise of $210.7 billion over the first three months of 2004.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The figures do not include China because it has not\par released its reserve holdings for April or May.\par     In the first three months of the year, China's reserve\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 holdings rose over $36 billion to $439.8 billion. China is the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 world's second-largest holder of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 after Japan.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the 18 months to March 2004, Asian reserve holdings  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 including China rose by $780 billion to $2.156 trillion - a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 rise of almost 57 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rapid accumulation largely reflected the region's  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 determination to stem gains in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against a weakening  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. dollar and protect export competitiveness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pause during April and May matched a period of strength  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 for the dollar, which reached nine-month highs against the yen  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and multi-month highs against other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     "I suspect that it is that factor more than anything that\par has meant the pace has slowed," Rennie said.\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , for example, did not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par markets in April or May after spending a record 14.8 trillion\par yen ($135 billion) in the first quarter of 2004 to cap the yen.\par     The end of a sharp rally in Asian stock markets has eased,\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 and in some cases reversed, the investment inflows which were  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 adding to the pressure for regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to rise. This  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 has also lessened the need for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, central banks remained vigilant. Rennie pointed to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 comments from Korean officials trying to stem gains in the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korean won since the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  started to strengthen against the  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar in mid-May.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The rhetoric has picked up noticeably in the last couple  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 of weeks, something that had been fairly absent for a  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 reasonable period of time," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The won fell against the dollar from early April to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 mid-May. In that period, Korean reserves rose by less than $200  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 million. In the second half of May, as the dollar weakened and  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 the won rallied, reserves rose by $2.8 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Korean \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said the rise was due to the weaker  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 dollar increasing the conversion value of assets held in other  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and interest income.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserve holdings:\par                Reserves       As at      end-2003   percent\par                   $ bln                     $ bln    change\par  Japan            816.8      May 31         673.5     +21.3\par  China*           439.8      Mar 31         403.3      +9.1\par  Taiwan           229.0      May 31         206.6     +10.8\par  South Korea      166.5      May 31         155.4      +7.1\par  Hong Kong        120.1      May 31         118.4      +1.4\par  India            119.8      May 28         100.6     +19.1\par  Singapore        101.1      May 31          96.3      +5.0\par  Malaysia          53.9      May 14          44.9     +20.0\par  Thailand          42.9      May 28          42.1      +1.9\par  Indonesia         36.5      May 31          36.3      +0.6\par  Philippines       16.5      May 31          16.8      -1.8\par  Pakistan          12.5      May 15          12.0      +4.2\par  Bangladesh         2.6      May 31           2.6       0.0\par  Total          2,158.0                   1,908.8     +13.1\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 *In late December, China used $45 billion of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 reserves - about 10 percent of its holdings at that time - to  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 bail out two banks. Those funds are not included in this  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 figure.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sources: central banks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040608e0680014d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
