\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says didn't  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  market in May.  \b0\par\par\uc2 436 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2004\par 12:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 31 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Monday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in May, the second consecutive month it stayed out of the market after spending a record amount last year to stem the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The last time the MOF stepped into the market was on March 16, when the dollar fell close to two yen on the day to around 108.60 yen  \par \par \uc2 The MOF sold 14.83 trillion yen ($134.5 billion) in the first three months of this year after spending an annual record of 20 trillion yen during 2003 to curb the yen's strength and help ensure the economic recovery was maintained.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a four-year low of 103.40 yen on March 31 then rebounded, touching an eight-month of 114.90 yen on May 14 on anticipation that the U.S. Federal Reserve would raise interest rates sooner than the market had anticipated.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has slipped in the past few weeks on concerns that high oil prices could hurt the U.S. economy - the world's largest consumer of oil - and delay any action by the Fed.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo analysts said an attack by suspected al Qaeda militants in Saudi Arabia at the weekend, in which 22 civilians were killed, served as a reminder of Middle East instability and potential disruptions to oil supply.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped under 110 yen for the first time in three weeks on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Although MOF officials have repeatedly said their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy remained unchanged and that excessive movements were undesirable for the economy, most analysts think they will hold off from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  for now.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts said that since speculative moves are not behind the current strength of the yen, unlike the rally earlier this year, the MOF could be unable to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if the yen rose further.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably a decline down to 105 levels right now is going to be very different to the decline we saw in January, which was very much speculative based," said Naomi Fink, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF has said its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was aimed countering speculative moves in the market.  \par \par \uc2 Data from the U.S. Commodity Futures Trading Commission showed on Friday that speculators had not increased their yen-long positions and still held net dollar-long positions during the week that ended May 25.  \par \par \uc2 During that period the yen rose to 111.80 yen per dollar from around 114.32. ($1=110.25 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040531e05v000vq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
