\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls vs yen as oil worries weigh.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2004\par 09:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 31 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a three-week low against the yen in thin pre-holiday trading on Monday as an attack in Saudi Arabia fuelled worries about higher oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The attack by suspected al Qaeda militants, in which 22 civilians were killed, was the second in a month thought to be aimed at destabilising the world's top oil exporter.  \par \par \uc2 "A continued speculative bout of oil price strength would probably hurt the dollar more than anything, just because it is going to hurt the U.S. consumer who has been fuelling U.S growth," said Naomi Fink, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 Fink said high oil prices suggested to some in the market that the Federal Reserve might either delay increasing interest rates, or increase at a slower rate than previously assumed - or even not hike at all this year.  \par \par \uc2 In recent months, the dollar has been supported by speculation that the Fed would raise interest rates in June from the current 46-year low of one percent.  \par \par \uc2 At 0700 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 109.47/52 yen after dipping to around 109.43, the lowest since May 6 and around five percent below an eight-month high of 114.90 on May 14.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a turnaround of the dollar-buying trend of the past two months. Recent buying happened too fast," said Yoshi Yanagisawa, treasury manager at State Street Bank in Tokyo. He said the dollar may fall to around 108 yen in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said price movements were exaggerated by thin volumes ahead of holidays on Monday in the United States and some European countries, including Britain.  \par \par \uc2 Oil futures prices rose across the board in Tokyo on Monday in response to the attack in Saudi Arabia.  \par \par \uc2 JOBS DATA AHEAD  \par \par \uc2 The market is looking to Friday's U.S. jobs data for possible clues on the outlook for interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was slightly weaker against both the dollar and the yen. By 0700 GMT, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fetching around $1.2200 compared with $1.2225 in late U.S. trade on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 By late in the Asian day, the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had dropped around 1-= yen from its day high to 133.54 yen .  \par \par \uc2 Some traders said the yen had benefited from an announcement by Morgan Stanley Capital Investment that its latest review would result in a one percentage point rise in Japan's weighting for standard equities portfolios held by U.S. investors.  \par \par \uc2 Later on Monday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will release \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data for the month of May.  \par \par \uc2 The data are expected to show that for the second straight month MOF did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold some 35 trillion yen ($318 billion) in the 15 months to March to help halt the rise of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and keep its export-led recovery intact.  \par \par \uc2 Since then a rally in the dollar and strong Japanese economic data have made \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnecessary, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Highlighting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's broadening economic recovery, a Reuters survey showed on Monday that factories pushed out goods at a faster pace in May than the month before.  \par \par \uc2 The Reuters/Nomura/JMMA Purchasing Managers Index (PMI) rose to 56.2 from 55.9 in April, just shy of the survey high of 56.4 set in November.  \par \par \uc2 A reading above 50 in the PMI, which gives an early snapshot of manufacturing activity, suggests a business expansion. A figure below it indicates a contraction.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040531e05v000p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
