\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For May 24  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,264 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2004\par 11:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Palaniappan Chidambaram, who returns to head India's finance ministry, is a favorite of financial markets and famous for his "dream budget" of 1997, when he cut income tax rates and later took the first steps toward liberalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  controls. ("=PROFILE: India Fin Min Chidambaram A Winner For The Mkts," ran at 0759 GMT; UPDATE with comments from Chidambaram to run by around 0930 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Expectations that the Federal Reserve will end its three-year easing cycle and begin raising interest rates as early as next month have roiled financial markets in Asia and elsewhere. But some analysts say the rate-hike fears are somewhat exaggerated and that many Asian central banks may not feel obligated to match the Fed's tightening as fast or by as much as they would have in the past. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Asia May Not Be Keen To Follow Fed" ran at 0004 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Indonesia's economy grew 4.46% on year in the first quarter of the year, slightly above expectations due to strong consumer spending. ("DATA SNAP: Indonesia 1Q GDP +4.46% On Yr On Consumption," ran at 0710 GMT; DATA VIEW to follow by 1030 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: In its first official comments on Taiwan President Chen Shui-bian's May 20 inauguration speech, Beijing said Monday he is still leaving the door open to independence, endangering relations across the Taiwan Strait. ("China Says Taiwan Pres Leaves Door Open For Independence," ran at 0229 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A slower pace of growth in China will curtail the ability of state banks to reduce their nonperforming loan levels, analysts at Standard & Poor's said Monday. ("S&P: China Slowdown To Ease Pace Of NPL Reductions," first take ran at 0705 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Monday that it increased to 36.75 billion yuan an offering of seven-year bonds that it auctioned to yield 4.89%, as the sale was strongly oversubscribed. The bond is set to become the most important on China's markets since it is tradable on all of the country's debt exchanges and because the government has designated the seven year maturity as the key one. ("China MOF Ups 7-Yr 4.89% Bond To CNY36.75B From CNY33B," first take ran at 0736 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Poland plans to return to Japan's Samurai market this week with a Y50 billion issue of five-year bonds almost a year after its debut last June, according to documents filed at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (Update to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Options prices for major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  except the yen continue to fall, suggesting value in selling short-term euro-dollar options and buying the long end of the dollar-yen curve. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: Short-Date Euro Vols Rich, Yen Favored," ran at 0413 and 0530 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Conventional wisdom among alcohol lovers in Japan is that "cold sake" - no matter how deceptively light, fragrant or delicate the taste - comes with an unpleasant delayed reaction. This "cold-sake effect" explains in part why the dollar has remained firm despite the abrupt end of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's multi-trillion yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign more than two months ago; Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still causing a throbbing headache for the yen. (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA to run by around 1000 GMT.)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's export orders rose to a fresh record high in April, mainly due to the continuing global recovery and higher foreign trade, the government said Monday. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan April Export Orders Hit New Record High," ran at 0818 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Bank of Japan data showed Monday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought no shares from commercial banks in the 10 days through May 20. The lack of share purchases indicates banks aren't pressed to unload shares, even though the Nikkei 225 fell below 10600 in the period for the first time since February. And banks have likely almost finished unwinding their cross-holdings. ("BOJ Shareholdings Y1.98T May 20, Steady From May 10," ran at 0156 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippine economy likely grew 5% on year in the first quarter on the back of record growth in farm output, Economic Planning Secretary Romulo Neri said Monday. Agriculture, which accounts for one fifth of the gross domestic product and employs 40% of the Philippines' work force, grew at its fastest pace in 15 years in the first quarter. ("Philippines Econ Chief Says 1Q GDP Boosted By Farm Output," ran at 0415 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Counties in China will be allowed to set up companies to independently handle asset management, the head of China's restructuring agency said, state media reported. ("China Counties Can Set Up Asset Management Firms - Xinhua," ran at 0357 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's consumer prices rose 0.2% in April from March due to the higher cost of clothing and health care, the Department of Statistics said Monday. ("Singapore Apr CPI +1.3% On Year, +0.2% On Month," first take ran at 0500 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: T.S. Eliot once wrote that April is the cruelest month and Japanese supermarket operators might tend to agree this year after a tax rule change battered their sales last month. ("=UPDATE: Tax Change Hurts Japan Supermarket Sales In April," which also includes department store sales, ran at 0656 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Many major Japanese banks delivered good news for investors Monday, reporting stronger earnings for the fiscal year ended March 31 and solid progress in cleaning up their balance sheets. (UPDATE to run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian property and construction group Lend Lease Corp. Monday unveiled a merger plan with General Property Trust that will form a top 20 local company with a market value of about A$10 billion. (UPDATE to run by 1000 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Creditors of South Korea's Hynix Semiconductor Inc. (000660.SEA) are reviewing a sweetened offer by the venture capital arm of Citigroup Inc. (C) to buy the chipmaker's nonmemory \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  for 954.3 billion won ($1=KRW1,177), creditor officials told Dow Jones Newswires on Monday. ("=UPDATE:Citigroup Ups Bid For S Korea Hynix Nonmemory Ops," ran at 0724 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: SABMiller formally launched its offer buy all the shares of Harbin Brewery that it doesn't already own, officially staking its claim in what could become a takeover battle for the China beer maker. (UPDATE to run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toyota Motor Corp. said Monday it's considering launching a dealership network in China specializing in sales of its Lexus luxury cars. ("Toyota Motor Mulls Launching Lexus Sales Channel In China," ran at 0309 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Shares of Mitsubishi Motors Corp. fell 6.3% Monday, suggesting some investors are concerned the troubled Japanese automaker's restructuring plan won't put it back on its feet. (Story wrapping in Goldman Sachs rating cut to run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DRAM REPORT: Spot prices of the dynamic random access memory chips most widely used in personal computers fell below $5 last week, and analysts said there is more downside ahead as DRAM makers enter the peak of the weak selling season.("=Asia DRAM Report: DRAM Prices Dip Below $5;More Downside," ran at 0602 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Ping An Insurance Co. started pre-marketing its initial public offering worth about US$1.5 billion Monday, after formally securing approval from Hong Kong stock exchange regulators late Friday, people familiar with the deal said. ("China Ping An Starts Pre-Marketing US$1.5B IPO - Sources," ran at 0422 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Unlike some of its competitors who are snapping up Chinese homegrown make-up brands, Revlon Inc. is looking to grow its share of China's booming market mainly by pushing the Revlon brand, a senior executive said. (INTERVIEW to run by 1200 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: A trend toward outsourcing the management of Asian insurance assets is opening up big business opportunities for international fund managers, according to Deutsche Asset Management. ("=Asia Fund View: Big Opportunity In Insurance Outsourcing," ran at 0602 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =TECHNIAL TALK: Shares in debt-laden Thai Petrochemical Industry PCL (TPI.TH) surged on Friday after its major creditor, Bangkok Bank, reportedly agreed in principle to a plan to bring in new partners. The charts say there's a good chance of large further gains in coming days and weeks. ("=TECHNICAL TALK: Thailand's TPI May Be Starting Bull Run," ran at 0245 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Temasek Holdings Pte. Ltd., the Singapore state-owned investment giant, said Monday it may try to create a pan-Asian banking network by unifying its disparate set of bank holdings. ("=UPDATE:Singapore Temasek Mulls Pan-Asian Banking Network," ran at 0858 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Asahi Glass Co. (5201.TO) said Monday it will invest Y9 billion to launch a new display-glass substrates processing facility in South Korea to meet ever-growing demand for substrates for use in flat TVs and personal computers. ("Asahi Glass To Invest Y9B On S.Korea TFT LCD Glass Plant," first take ran at 0914 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian newspaper group John Fairfax Holdings Ltd. has Monday upgraded its earnings guidance for fiscal 2004, forecasting that earnings before interest and tax will slightly exceed A$360 million. ("John Fairfax Tips FY04 EBIT Slightly Above A$360M," first take ran at 0202 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's Ramsay Health Care Ltd. said Monday it will acquire Benchmark Healthcare Group, the operator of 10 hospitals in Victoria and South Australia states, for A$125 million. ("Australia's Ramsay Healthcare To Buy Benchmark," first take ran at 2348 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Storefund Ltd., which launched its NZ$30 million initial public offering Monday, plans to acquire several specialty retail businesses with annual sales of between NZ$20 million and NZ$100 million over the next few years, said Wayne Walden, one of the company's founders. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ's StoreFund Keen On Specialty Retailers," ran at 0542 GMT) [ 24-05-04 0935GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Powerco Ltd., a gas and electricity networks company, said Monday it has breached the New Zealand Commerce Commission's price and reliability thresholds for electricity lines companies. ("NZ Powerco: Has Breached Price, Reliability Thresholds" ran at 2150 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: PT Semen Gresik, an Indonesian cement maker, Monday said its total cement sales in the first four months of this year rose 16% on year to 4.61 million metric tons from 3.97 million metric tons, due to an increase in domestic sales and exports. ("Indonesia Semen Gresik 4-Mo Cement Sales Up 16% On Year," ran at 0150 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's China Motor Corp. said it bought Mitsubishi Motors Corp. preferred shares worth Y10 billion as part of the Y450 billion bailout for the Japanese auto maker. ("Taiwan's China Motor Buys Mitsubishi Motors Shrs For Y10B," ran at 0303 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Carpet manufacturer Feltex Carpets Ltd. said Monday that shares in its initial public offering will be sold at NZ$1.70 each, which is at the bottom of the NZ$1.70 to NZ$1.95 indicative range. ("NZ Feltex To Sell IPO Shares At NZ$1.70 Each" ran at 2123 GMT"  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Wah Sang Gas Holdings Ltd. said Monday that its chairman and chief executive, Charlie Shum, resigned May 4 as the piped-gas distributor faced fines and an investigation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in China. The company said it defaulted on syndicated loan arranged by CITIC Ka Wah Bank, and the Hong Kong branch of China Merchants Bank Co. has also declared Wah Sang in default of a bank facility. ("HK Wah Sang Gas: Chairman, CEO Shum Resigned Posts May 4," first take ran at 0222 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =CHARTING OIL: July Nymex crude oil futures are set to test key support in the next day or two, and any break of that support would signal their spectacular bull run had ended for the short term at least. ("=CHARTING OIL: Nymex Crude Risks Fall Of Around 10%," ran at 0515 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Crude oil futures trade is expected to stay volatile Monday despite weekend calls from G-8 finance ministers and leading producer Saudi Arabia for more supply, which helped bring prices below the psychologically important $40 a barrel mark. ("=OIL FUTURES: Crude To Stay Fickle, OPEC Hike Still Seen," ran at 0452 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China bans two trading houses from selling Brazil soybeans. (First take to run shortly).  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Kashima Oil Co. will keep crude runs at its refinery in Ibaraki Prefecture, near Tokyo, around 80% of capacity through October until it completes repair work and restarts a secondary refinery unit that was damaged by fire last month. ("Kashima Oil's Ops At Fire-Hit Refinery At 80% Until Oct," ran at 0252 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: The latest Australian energy auction kicked off Monday with the receivers of Epic Energy Inc.'s Dampier-to-Bunbury gas pipeline targeting a near-A$2 billion sale inside nine weeks. ("=INTERVIEW: Epic Pipeline Receiver Resumes A$2B Sale," ran at xxxx GMT)0705  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Oil & Natural Gas Corp., India's largest oil explorer, plans investments totaling $7 billion in natural gas, power and petrochemical projects over the next four years. (UPDATE due 1230 GMT). [ 24-05-04 0935GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040524e05o000ho\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
