\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-IMF may upgrade Japan growth forecast.  \b0\par\par\uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2004\par 06:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 21 (Reuters) - The International Monetary Fund (IMF) said on Friday it may raise its forecast for Japan's economic growth for this year to nearly four percent from 3.4 percent when it issues its next global economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Daniel Citrin, deputy director of the IMF's Asia-Pacific department, told reporters after an annual consultation with Japanese officials on economic policy that the country's recovery from a decade of stagnation was becoming more solid.  \par \par \uc2 "Looking at the current conditions relative to our previous forecast, we're more likely to revise our forecast for Japan up, rather significantly," Citrin said.  \par \par \uc2 He said work on a revised forecast for the Washington-based institution's next semi-annual World Economic Outlook, due out in September, would start in June.  \par \par \uc2 The comments come on the heels of gross domestic product (GDP) figures for the first three months of the year, which showed the economy grew at a stronger-than-expected 1.4 percent from the previous quarter, or an annualised rate of 5.6 percent.  \par \par \uc2 It was the eighth straight quarter of expansion, the longest stretch since the mid-1990s.  \par \par \uc2 Citrin cited higher world oil prices and a potential slowdown in the Chinese and U.S. economies due to possible monetary tightening in those countries as potential risks going forward. But he added that the domestic sectors were picking up as unemployment bottomed and business investment grew.  \par \par \uc2 To sustain the forward momentum, Citrin said the Bank of Japan (BOJ) should continue its accommodative monetary policy, despite easing deflationary pressures.  \par \par \uc2 An IMF statement on the meeting with Japanese officials - known as the Article 4 consultations, which it conducts with every member country - said that as Japan neared the end of deflation, the IMF encouraged the BOJ to stabilise inflation expectations by clarifying medium-term inflation objectives.  \par \par \uc2 Citrin said this did not necessarily mean the IMF was urging the BOJ to adopt a so-called inflation-targeting policy.  \par \par \uc2 He noted that while some central banks around the world had more formal target ranges for prices, others clarified their intentions by defining what they thought could be regarded as price stability.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which began at the start of last year and came to a screeching halt last month, Citrin said he saw it as part of a broader economic policy package.  \par \par \uc2 In a bid to prevent a higher yen from derailing the country's export-led recovery and to contain deflation, Japan spent a record 20 trillion yen ($177.5 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of this year.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the last year was what one might call an extraordinary measure under extraordinary circumstances, regarded as part of the overall package of measures that were taken to ensure that monetary stance was adequately and appropriately expansionary," Citrin said.  \par \par \uc2 While the dollar's reversal higher over the last several weeks - ironically, after \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  - has moved the focus away from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's market actions, Citrin said what steps the Japanese authorities might take when the yen strengthens again depended on overall economic conditions.  \par \par \uc2 "What steps the MOF (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and the BOJ take if that happens will be judged in the context of the overall cyclical situation," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "With a stronger recovery, clearly one can handle a somewhat stronger yen. But at the same time, a very sharp and sudden appreciation of the yen would obviously have some negative impact on growth." ($1=112.64 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040521e05l000gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
