\par\par \b \uc2 S&P Analyst: Japan Deflation Weaker Than A Year Ago  \b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Mai 2004\par 09:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Deflationary pressure on the Japanese economy is easing compared with a year ago, Standard & Poor's Ratings Services' sovereign analyst for Japan said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "While it may be difficult for Japan to escape deflation this year, it may happen next year," Takahira Ogawa said at a meeting with reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Deflation isn't a pressing problem for Japan's economy now, Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 On March 24, Standard & Poor's upgraded its outlook on Japan's AA- sovereign rating to stable from negative.  \par \par \uc2 Still, Ogawa reiterated that it isn't likely that Japan will see its ratings upgraded in the next year or two, because the country isn't expected to solve its large fiscal-deficit problem anytime soon.  \par \par \uc2 "The cyclical recovery of the economy would contribute to easing Japan's fiscal burden, but such a positive impact would be limited if Japan cannot solve the structural problem of its fiscal condition," Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 Unless Japan balances its revenue and expenditure, the economy will start to decline and cause the deficit to deepen further, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Asked about the potential economic impact of the recent rise in crude oil prices, Ogawa said he doesn't expect it to have a significant impact on Japan for now.  \par \par \uc2 "Since deflationary pressure still exists in Japan, companies are yet to be able to transfer the higher oil prices on to their goods prices," Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 So far, companies have absorbed higher costs on materials by cutting costs for other parts, he said.  \par \par \uc2 But higher raw materials prices would have a negative impact if a time comes when companies can no longer absorb the higher costs and they start raising their product prices, Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 Another potential risk for the nation's economy, Ogawa said, would be large-scale yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  like that undertaken by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in the fiscal year that ended in March. That's because there's interest-rate risk involved in such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 Ogawa pointed out, however, that there's interest-rate risk in the government's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises the funds for its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by issuing financial bills, and it invests part of the dollars it buys in the market in longer-term Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 Rises in Treasury yields would depress the value of the securities the Japanese government holds, while funding the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  via short-term bills results in higher funding costs in the medium- to long-term if interest rates in Japan start to rise, Ogawa said.  \par \par \uc2 "The large issuance of financing bills could also crimp the flexibility of the Bank of Japan's monetary policy" by reducing the bank's policy options, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Ogawa's view of large-scale yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a potential risk for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy is in contrast to the stance of Moody's Investors Service, which upgraded its ratings for foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euroyen debt issued by Japan's government to Aaa from Aa1 in April, partly prompted by a sharp rise in Japan's official reserve assets to more than $770 billion. The reserve assets ballooned as a result of Japan's selling of the yen, mainly against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent a record Y35 trillion in the 15 months from last January to mid-March this year \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support by economy by selling yen to keep the Japanese unit from rising against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 A strong yen hurts Japan's exporters by making their products less competitive overseas and by reducing the yen value of their overseas earnings.  \par \par \uc2 -By Michiyo Seki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; michiyo.seki@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie and John Gibson [ 21-05-04 0737GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040521e05l000h9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
