\par\par \b \uc2 Japan seen less worried at G7, focus on oil, reforms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya  \par \uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Mai 2004\par 07:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 19 (Reuters) - Japan will go into a Group of Seven meeting this weekend bolstered by a broadening economic recovery but, like its counterparts, will be concerned about soaring oil prices and the prospect of rising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Finance ministers of the G7 leading nations and Russia will gather in New York over the weekend to discuss the global economy and also work out proposals for a leaders' summit scheduled next month in Sea Island, Georgia.  \par \par \uc2 "There won't be any major changes to our views expressed in the April statement. The big trends are unchanged," a senior Japanese Finance Ministry official told reporters on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 A statement after an April meeting of G7 finance chiefs and central bankers said the global economic recovery continued to strengthen and that the overall balance of risks for the outlook had improved.  \par \par \uc2 Energy prices, cited as a risk to economic prospects at the April meeting, would be discussed at the coming gathering in the context of the global economy, but not necessarily as a specific topic on the agenda, the MOF official said.  \par \par \uc2 Oil prices have reached their highest in 21 years in recent days, threatening to dent corporate earnings in Japan, which is almost entirely dependent on imported oil.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen has also fallen against the dollar recently, adding to the cost of buying imported oil.  \par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Saturday that the rise in oil prices would need to be monitored for its impact on Japan and the rest of Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Economists said however that the overall impact on Japanese company profits and on prices has been subdued so far.  \par \par \uc2 "Even if oil import prices were to average $45/barrel, chances are small that this would cause companies to fall into the red," said Mamoru Yamazaki, chief economist at Barclays Capital in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 INTEREST RATES UP  \par \par \uc2 Brighter U.S. economic data has also fanned speculation the Federal Reserve will raise interest rates as early as next month.  \par \par \uc2 Any such move, plus any efforts by China to dampen its economy by tightening credit, could crimp demand for Japan's exports in its two largest markets.  \par \par \uc2 G7 sources say however that interest rates are unlikely to be on the agenda, given that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors are not participating in the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF official said that the main message of the meeting would be for the G7 countries to press ahead with reforms.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese policymakers have expressed growing confidence about the domestic economy and in tackling deflation, which is showing signs of easing after about six years of persistent price falls.  \par \par \uc2 Government data on Tuesday showed Japan's economy grew a real 1.4 percent in the January-March quarter over the previous three months. Annualised, growth was 5.6 percent, outstripping U.S. growth of 4.2 percent in the same period.  \par \par \uc2 Nominal GDP, which does not take into account price changes, expanded 0.8 percent in the quarter, the fastest pace since the October-December quarter of 1996.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely to be discussed at the meeting, the G7 sources said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stayed away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent weeks as the yen has weakened.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo's G7 counterparts have been openly critical about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's record yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past year, which came to a halt on March 16, according to MOF data.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040519e05j000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
