\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For May 12  \b0\par\par\uc2 3,176 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2004\par 11:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's billionaire prime minister Thaksin Shinawatra may have thought his plan to make Liverpool FC a "Thai asset" would boost his popularity at home, but the bid to buy a 30% stake in the English Premier League soccer club has resulted in a serious backlash both at home and abroad. (story will run by around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The Australian government Wednesday began selling its 2004-05 budget, fending off claims its pitch to families could force rates higher as it tries to win this year's election. ("=3rd UPDATE: Australia Govt Vows Budget Won't Spur Rates," ran at 0651 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Aware that a pre-election rate hike will be politically devastating, the Australian government has tried to package its budget sweeteners to avoid over-stimulating the economy. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Budget Stimulus A Mild Upside For Rates," ran at 0808 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's housing market remains strong enough to raise concerns about whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prematurely put interest rates on hold, economists said Wednesday. ("=UPDATE:Housing Market Challenges Australia Policy-makers," ran at 0753 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: When it comes to the yen's exchange rate, it seems there's no satisfying some Japanese officials. Having spent an unprecedented Y20 trillion in 2003 and three-quarters that much again in the first three months of this year fighting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's surge, one might expect Tokyo policy makers to be uniformly thrilled at the yen's retreat this week to its lowest levels against the dollar since September. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Weaker Yen Doesn't Thrill All Japan Officials," ran at 0811 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  abruptly ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign on March 16 after a record-breaking stretch of dollar-buying, allowing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall to a four-year low in the following two weeks, according to data issued Wednesday. ("=UPDATE: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ended \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  In Mar After Record Sum," ran at 0518 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan would have been proud. The quarterly report by the monetary-policy committee of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sufficiently vague to allow analysts to quibble over the next move in the country's tightening policy. ("=Opinions Split Over China Rates Following PBOC Report," ran at 0547 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday it will add a second money market \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  each week, redoubling its efforts to keep money supply from expanding too rapidly. The People's Bank of China said it will conduct repurchase agreements with commercial banks each Thursday, in addition to its regular Tuesday sales of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills. ("China PBOC Adds 2nd Weekly Money Market Op On Thursdays," ran at 0627 GMT; UPDATE will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: A Chinese Academy of Social Sciences researcher has called for strong measures to curb China's ongoing real- estate boom as a means to take the steam out of the country's overheating economy. (story will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be seen as a neutral regulator and not as an interested party, the impression that was created after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore announced expanded changes in limits on cross-shareholdings, the day after Temasek, also controlled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , made an unsolicited bid for United Overseas Bank's property unit. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: MAS Needs To Emphasize Independence," ran at 0311 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =PHILIPPINE VOTE: President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo's apparent victory of six more years in office will be good for the Philippines, but she will still have her work cut out for her in such tough areas as paring the giant budget deficit, ratings agencies say. Officials say it's too early to comment on the outlook for the country's battered sovereign credit rating from Monday's election, in which Arroyo appears to have a decisive lead. Assuming the incumbent wins, agencies say they'll be keenly watching whether she gets a strong congressional majority and what kind of economic team she assembles. (=PHILIPPINE VOTE story will run around 1000 GMT; see also "=INTERVIEW: Philippines Budget Gap To Fall - Budget Secy," which ran at 0617 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's unemployment rate fell to a 16-year low of 4.3% in the first quarter of 2004 as growth in jobs accelerated, increasing the likelihood of a rise in official interest rates next month, data released Wednesday showed. ("=UPDATE: NZ's 1Q Jobless Rate Falls To 16-Yr Low Of 4.3%" ran at 0001 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's constitutional court is widely expected to overturn parliament's unprecedented vote to impeach President Roh Moo-Hyun when the nine-member panel announces its verdict Friday. Such a decision would allow Roh to resume office after two months of uncertainty, and the newly empowered president is expected to launch long-awaited measures to invigorate the economy. This verdict is unlikely to move financial markets which have long assumed such an outcome, but they could be thrown into a panic should Roh, on a very slim chance, be thrown out of office, analysts said. ("=S Korea Court Likely To Overturn Roh Impeachment Vote," ran at 0520 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Financing conditions of South Korea's credit card companies have improved this year amid eased worries over their liquidity shortage problems, said the Financial Supervisory Service Wednesday. The country's six card issuers secured 908.4 billion won in new funds via loans, bonds and commercial paper in March, almost double the KRW456.6 billion a month earlier, the FSS said. ("S Korea Card Cos In Better Financial Health This Yr - FSS," ran at 0300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean consumer sentiment improved in April to its highest level in 19 months at 99.9 from 94.4 in March, but remained below the key 100 level, according to survey conducted by the National Statistics Office on Wednesday. ("S Korea Consumer Sentiment Index At 99.9 Apr Vs 94.4 Mar," ran at 2253 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The South Korean government and the ruling Uri Party agreed on the need for a supplementary budget and the National Assembly will likely review the budget proposal when it convenes in June, a Uri Party official said Wednesday. ("S Korea Govt, Uri Party To Discuss Extra Budget In June," ran at 0129 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: Standard & Poor's Wednesday put Indonesia's long-term sovereign ratings on positive outlook, indicating an upgrade is in the cards, but the agency says it will assess the performance of the country's new government before acting. ("=2nd UPDATE:S&P Puts Indonesia's Rtgs On Positive Outlook," ran at 0857 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =RBI WATCH: Inflation is threatening the Indian economy which looks set to expand robustly again this year - but don't expect the Reserve Bank of India to act when it meets next week on policy. After a long easing spell, the Indian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to keep to neutral gear, waiting to see how the economy and inflation play out amid higher oil prices, and fears of higher U.S. interest rates, economists said. ("=RBI WATCH: India Likely To Stress Neutral Monetary Bias," will run by around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The yen rose against the dollar and the euro in Asia Wednesday, buoyed by Japanese exporters' bids and solid gains in Japanese stocks. The dollar-buying hype triggered by expectations of higher U.S. interest rates in early summer cooled after the greenback's overnight failure to climb past Y114 discouraged dollar bulls, traders said. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Japan Stk Rise Drives Yen Higher Vs Dlr, Euro," ran at 0547 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: One-time gains boosted National Australia Bank's fiscal first-half net profit by 19% on year, but the nation's biggest lender Wednesday warned investors of a long wait before it delivers sustainable earnings growth. New Chief Executive John Stewart has no illusions about the reforms needed to lift performance as the bank attempts to put a damaging \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading scandal and bitter boardroom dispute behind it. ("=UPDATE: Natl Australia Bk Warns Of Earnings Boost Delay," ran at 0150 GMT; 2nd UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: United Overseas Bank said Wednesday it will pay S$935 million for a majority stake in Bank of Asia, a move that will expand the Singapore lender's position in Thailand's fast-shrinking banking sector. Singapore's No. 2 lender by assets has agreed to buy ABN Amro Bank's 80.77% stake in Thailand's ninth-largest lender for THB5.35 a share, or THB22.02 billion in total. ("=UPDATE: UOB Expands Thai Presence With Bank Of Asia Buy," ran at 0518 GMT; 2nd UPDATE will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Even as Singapore billionaire Wee Cho Yaw expands his United Overseas Bank in Asia by buying a second bank in Thailand, his control over a vast banking and property empire remains under a cloud at home. The fiercely independent 75-year-old tycoon has avoided a battle with state investment agency Temasek Holdings by rejecting its offer to buy UOB's property arm, but now has to come up with a better alternative. ("=As UOB Grows Overseas, Its Chmn Faces Problems At Home," ran at 0854 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: In a widely anticipated move, Hyundai Motor and DaimlerChrysler have agreed to redefine a comprehensive strategic alliance, with the German-U.S. car maker agreeing to sell its entire stake in South Korea's largest auto maker, Hyundai Motor said Wednesday. ("Hyundai Motor:DaimlerChrysler To Sell 10.5% Stake In Co" ran at 0638 GMT; UPDATE will run by around 1100 GMT) [ 12-05-04 0942GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Shares of Nissan Motor have tumbled since the Japanese auto maker disappointed investors by making modest earnings forecasts two weeks ago. But many analysts say now is the time to buy. The reason is simple: the Nissan Motor projections were probably too modest. Given a solid outlook for its business in North America, and a tail wind from a yen weakening against the dollar, Nissan will likely bump up its forecasts later this year, these analysts say. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Nissan Motor Stock May Have Fuel," ran at 0233 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Woori Finance said Wednesday it swung to a hefty first-quarter net loss of KRW156 billion mainly due to the poor performance of its credit card unit, from a net profit of KRW181 billion in the year-ago period. ("S Korea Woori Finance 1Q Loss KRW156B Vs KRW181B Pft," ran at 0554 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: The latest index review by Morgan Stanley Capital International isn't likely to be the harbinger of a tangible shift of foreign funds into Asian stocks. (=ASIA FUND VIEW will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Transport Ministry says it won't allow Mitsubishi Fuso Truck to sell new vehicles with its latest wheel hub model until its safety is verified, another setback for the struggling truck and bus maker following a massive recall in March and the arrests of former executives at the company last week. ("Japan Govt Halts Certifying Of Mitsubishi Fuso Vehicles," ran at 0351 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: BOC Hong Kong (Holdings) Ltd. said the stock exchange's listing committee told it to reveal that its loans to a family of eight listed companies comprised more than a quarter of the bank's net asset value last year. ("BOC HK Told To Reveal Scope Of Lending To Big Borrowers," ran at 0347 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: The recent violence in Thailand's southernmost provinces has shaken investor confidence in the local bourse and dented share prices of transport-sector stocks amid fears that the ongoing unrest may drive tourists away. While the near-term outlook is uncertain, analysts still see value in national carrier Thai Airways, which has fallen to an attractive level. But they are less keen on the sector's other airline stock, Airports of Thailand, saying the airport operator's shares are too pricey despite recent declines. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Thai Airways Worth A Look, AOT Pricey," ran at 0408 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Fuyao Group Glass Industries, China's largest automotive glassmaker, said Wednesday the U.S. Commerce Department has issued a preliminary ruling imposing a close to zero percent anti-dumping tariff on its exports. ("China Fuyao Glass Gets Near Zero Dumping Tax On US Export," ran at 0913 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Foreign investors were net buyers of 3.51 trillion won of shares in the Korea Stock Exchange in April, remaining net buyers for 12 straight months, said the Financial Supervisory Service Wednesday. Overseas investors purchased a net KRW2.54 trillion worth of shares in the main bourse in March. ("Foreigners Net Buyers Of KRW3.51T S Korea Shares In April," ran at 2100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China United Telecommunications Corp. said Wednesday shareholders have approved its plan to issue share-purchase rights and the offering will likely go ahead in the first half of 2004. ("China Utd Telecommunications Hldrs OK Share Purchase Rts," ran at 0020 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Pacific Retail Group is selling its mainstay New Zealand businesses through an initial public offering because it can get better returns elsewhere, but shares in the appliance and electronic retailers will still be attractive to retail investors who want to buy their "icon" status, Chairman Maurice Kidd said Wednesday. ("=INTERVIEW: PRG Sees Better Returns Outside NZ Retailing," ran at 0303 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand-based forestry and biotechnology investor Rubicon is unlikely to raise its takeover offer for Tenon, and would trim or cancel Tenon's proposed second capital repayment if it gains control, an independent advisor's report said. ("=UPDATE: Rubicon Unlikely To Up Bid For NZ Tenon -Advisor," ran at 0224 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese heavy-machinery maker Kawasaki Heavy Industries said Wednesday its group sales and profits fell for the year ended March, weighed down by strength in the yen. ("Kawasaki Heavy FY Group Net Pft Y6.33B Vs Y13.02B Pft," ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Suzuki Motor, Japan's largest maker of mini-vehicles in terms of sales volume, said Wednesday its group net profit jumped 41% in the fiscal year that ended March 31 on cost cuts and robust overseas sales. ("Suzuki Motor FY Group Net Pft Y43.84B Vs Y31.02B Pft," ran at 0555 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: East Japan Railway said that profits for the business year ended March rose as the railway operator reaped the benefits of cost cuts and a lower interest burden. ("JR East FY Group Net Profit Y119.87B Vs Y97.99B Profit," ran at 0736 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nippon Mining said Wednesday its group net profit for last fiscal year quadrupled to Y14.85 billion from Y3.65 billion, thanks to a rise in sales volume and price increases in its petroleum business \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Nippon Mining Last FY Group Net Pft Y14.85B Vs Y3.65B Pft," ran at 0730 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's Datacraft Asia turned in a small second-quarter net profit from a loss a year earlier, coming a week after a damaging probe that has dogged the company for the past 2 years was finally lifted. The company, which mainly installs computer networks and equipment from suppliers like Cisco Systems, posted a net profit of $158,000 from a $12.9 million net loss a year earlier, helped by growing demand for information technology in the 13 Asian markets it operates in. ("=2nd UPDATE: Singapore's Datacraft Makes Small 2Q Profit," ran at 0608 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 INTERVIEW: Taiwan's Simplo Technology Co. aims to replace rival Sony Corp. as the world's No.2 notebook computer battery pack maker this year as it expands production in China and wins new orders from PC multinationals, said Chairman Raymond Sung. (INTERVIEW may run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's LG Corp. says it plans to increase research and development spending for its electronic businesses to 4 trillion won by 2007, from this year's budget of KRW2.3 trillion. ("S Korea LG Corp To Boost R&D Spending, Staff For Elec Ops," ran at 0358 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong Exchanges & Clearing Ltd., or HKEx, reported strong results Wednesday, saying its first-quarter net profit more than tripled to HK$313.5 million from HK$88.7 million a year earlier. ("Hong Kong Exchanges 1Q Net HK$313.5 Mln Vs HK$88.7 Mln," ran at 0517 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Cosco Corp. (Singapore) Ltd., the Singapore-listed unit of China's largest shipping conglomerate COSCO Shipping Co., said its first-quarter net profit rose nearly four-fold to S$11.39 million, from S$2.97 million a year ago. ("Cosco Corp (Singapore) 1Q Net S$11.4M Vs S$2.97M," ran at 0535 GMT; INTERVIEW with company president will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Australian shipbuilder Austal Ltd. having stemmed recent losses, is just weeks away from learning whether its four-year effort to crack the U.S. military market has succeeded. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia's Austal Set For US Navy Decision," ran at 0332 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's Island Power Supply Pte Ltd. is set to make a foray into the country's highly competitive electricity retail sector after receiving a license from the Energy Marketing Authority. ("=Singapore's Island Power Set To Enter Elec Retail Market," ran at 0131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN POWER: The Japanese government is under pressure to abandon its long-term nuclear power strategy of developing and building fast breeder reactors, partially as a consequence of its electricity deregulation program. ("=JAPAN POWER: Change In Civil Nuclear Pwr Strategy Mulled," ran at 0128 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: The Chinese government wants to slow the pace of new power station construction, despite the country's shortage of electricity. That's because there are already more than enough projects in the works to meet demand within two years, and the authorities need to act now to prevent overcapacity. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH: Power Sector - From Famine To Feast," ran at 0909 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's Cementhai Chemicals Co., the petrochemical arm of Siam Cement, is considering a plan to build an olefins cracker and downstream petrochemical plants to meet increasing demand in Asia. ("=INTERVIEW: Siam Cement Unit Mulls Olefins Cracker Invest," ran at 0443 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Papua New Guinea's growing cocoa industry is facing a new risk: the cocoa pod borer pest, hitherto known only in bigger producing countries of the region such as Indonesia, Malaysia and Thailand. ("=INTERVIEW: Pod Borer Could Threaten PNG's Cocoa Industry," ran at 0857 GMT) [ 12-05-04 0942GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040512e05c000kn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
