\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ INTERVIEW:Tsy Taylor:Flexibility Is Guiding  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Principle  \b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 929 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2004\par 17:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Senior U.S. Treasury official John Taylor said Wednesday \par that he has discussed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  flexibility with various nations, \par including Japan and China, and that he is beginning to see some changes in \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policies. \par  \par Partly because of a robust economic rebound, Tokyo also appears to have \par abandoned its massive campaign to weaken the yen against the dollar for now. \par And Beijing's long-stated goal is to eventually establish a more market- \par oriented \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  system and is continuing talks with Washington \par toward this end. \par  \par "I just visited China as well. There our focus, along with China, is finding \par ways for them to move to a more flexible exchange rate, and so that's been \par basically our principle," the Treasury undersecretary for international \par affairs said. \par  \par "And we discussed this with Japan and other countries and I think right now \par you're seeing some changes in policies and the good performance that results \par from that," he told Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC Asia Pacific in an interview. \par  \par Indeed, data released Wednesday by the Japanese Finance Ministry underlined \par two points: record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the three months through March and a sudden \par stop in action after March 16 and into April. \par  \par That halt allowed the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to fall to a four-year low in the \par following two weeks. The MOF took action on 47 out of 64 trading days during \par the first two-and-a-half months of the year, before stopping its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  \par and apparently altering its strategy. \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold Y14.83 trillion in the January-March quarter to stem a fall in the \par dollar and to weaken the yen when the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eventually rebounded from \par mid-February onward. \par  \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push the dollar higher when it was already rising in the \par second half of February and into March generated concern and, at times, \par criticism inside and outside Japan. Both Federal Reserve Chairman Alan \par Greenspan and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow directly commented on the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in early March. \par  \par But a Japanese MOF official said Tokyo's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  actions were in line with a \par Group of Seven statement made by finance ministers and central bankers in Boca \par Raton, Fla., in February. \par  \par For his part, Taylor, asked about the MOF's herculean effort, as evidenced by \par the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  data, said that the U.S. guiding principle was flexibility, \par as seen in G7 statements. \par  \par "The message that I would emphasize is the message that has been put in the G7 \par communique the last couple of times calls for more flexibility in exchange \par rates and that is our guiding principle," he said. "How that actually gets \par operated depends on the circumstances." \par  \par But he also said that Tokyo's overall \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy was "exactly consistent \par with the G7." \par  \par The U.S. official is visiting Japan Wednesday to Friday as part of a one-week \par tour of Asia, which included a stopover in China on Monday and Tuesday and \par will be concluded with a visit to South Korea, starting Friday, to attend the \par annual meetings of the Asian Development Bank. \par  \par In China Tuesday, Taylor said no agreement has been reached within the nation \par on the type of flexible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime to be pursued. China has a managed \par float that keeps the yuan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate effectively pegged to the \par dollar. \par  \par The yuan, or renminbi, is fully convertible only on the trade account and even \par then is kept in a tight band of around 8.2777 to the dollar. \par  \par CNBC will broadcast portions of the interview Thursday. \par  \par  \par With economic indicators showing signs of improvement, Tokyo's mandarins \par apparently believe the nation's exports and corporate balance sheets can \par weather a stronger yen. \par  \par Indeed, the economy grew at a blistering real annualized pace of 6.4% in the \par three months through December, and economists' expect January-March gross \par domestic product, due out May 18, will top 3.0%. \par  \par Taylor was also optimistic on Japan's economic outlook, but said that the \par nation needed to ensure there is balance among all sectors. Growth isn't just \par dependent on exports and includes "considerable" investment, improving \par consumption and the right monetary and bank reform policies. \par  \par "For all reasons, this is a more sustainable situation than we've seen in a \par long time," he said. "If it's going to be sustainable you have to have balance \par in all the compositions of spending." \par  \par Taylor also said that the deflation situation is also starting to improve \par because of the more aggressive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , saying that the policies \par are "just right." \par  \par "The policies that underlie the growth are different from the past: the good \par focus on monetary policy to try to end the deflation, which has been a drag on \par the Japanese economy. It's beginning to end. It reached its bottom and is \par coming back up again," he said. \par  \par Recovering domestic activity has eased deflationary pressure, but consumer \par prices continue to fall slightly, reinforcing expectations the Bank of Japan \par will keep its zero-rate policy in place for some time. \par  \par Japan's nationwide core consumer prices fell 0.1% in March from a year \par earlier. The BOJ uses nationwide core consumer prices as its chief price \par indicator and has pledged to keep its quantitative easing until year-to-year \par changes in nationwide core consumer prices stabilize at or above zero. \par  \par  \par   -By James Simms, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; \par james.simms@dowjones.com \par Copyright 2004 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 05-12-04 1110ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020040512e05c000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
