\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar falls on wider March US trade deficit.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 629 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2004\par 22:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, May 12 (Reuters) - The dollar fell against the euro on Wednesday on news the U.S. trade deficit widened more than expected in March, due largely to a spike in oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 The March trade gap was $45.96 billion, easily overshooting economists' forecasts of a $42.60 billion deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Although the report indicated stronger U.S. growth than in other major economies, the swelling deficit weighed on the dollar. Americans' purchases of foreign goods like German cars, for instance, require selling dollars to buy euros.  \par \par \uc2 "The trade report was horrendous," said Bob Priore, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk manager at Calyon Financial in Chicago. "In a way, I think it shows that the money in the U.S. is a little bit looser right now and is buying more foreign goods. I don't think this is a number that's going to go down any time soon."  \par \par \uc2 Worries about rising inflation emanating from surging oil prices also threatened the dollar, traders said. That is because higher inflation could hurt the U.S. economy, and by extension, diminish the allure of dollar-denominated assets.  \par \par \uc2 "A lot of these (recent economic) numbers coming out, while positive for the U.S. economy, aren't necessarily positive for the U.S. dollar (especially if there are) any signs that inflation is getting out of the grip of the Fed," said Thomas Molloy, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Bank Leumi in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "The specter of inflation can't be good for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 " and that scenario could be a concern for the dollar, he added.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of Friday's U.S. consumer price report, analysts looking to gauge price pressures will be checking producer price data, due out on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Other key reports scheduled for Thursday include data on April U.S. retail sales and weekly first-time jobless claims.  \par \par \uc2 In late New York trading, the euro was at $1.1900, up about 0.21 percent. Against the yen, the dollar was trading at 112.89 yen, down about 0.12 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was down about 0.25 percent at 1.2937 francs.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was up 0.81 percent at $1.7724, partly on signals of future U.K. interest rate hikes.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the main focus of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains interest rates and Federal Reserve policy more than the trade balance," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of America in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar had held steady against the euro and yen but stayed off recent peaks as rising Tokyo stocks lifted the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a Fed official's comments tempered U.S. rate hike enthusiasm.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday morning, Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow said the pace of monetary tightening is likely to be gradual and will depend on employment growth and inflation, just as Philadelphia Fed President Anthony Santomero said on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Santomero's comments also kept the upcoming U.S. inflation data in focus, while the yen found support from the Nikkei stock average's biggest one-day gain this year.  \par \par \uc2 New data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Ministry had refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since mid-March.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, Bank of Japan policy board member Shin Nakahara said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep rates at zero even after ending its quantitative easing.  \par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar rose more than 1 percent against the greenback, receiving help from strong employment data that painted a robust picture of the domestic economy.  \par \par \uc2 Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to recover from recent falls against the dollar that had come on worries that attempts to cool China's economy could hurt the wider Asia-Pacific region. (Additional reporting by John Parry in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040512e05c0037n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
