\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-U.S.'s Taylor says Japan now in sustained recovery.  \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Mai 2004\par 07:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 12 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Under-Secretary John Taylor said on Wednesday there was mounting evidence that Japan's long-suffering economy had broken out of a decade-long slump and begun a period of steady growth.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor, who arrived in Tokyo on Tuesday night for a day and a half of talks with officials from the Bank of Japan, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other agencies as well as with Japanese and foreign banking and business officials, said many needed measures to induce growth were taking root.  \par \par \uc2 "The economy has turned the corner and what we're seeing now is some sustainability," Taylor told reporters traveling with him.  \par \par \uc2 "It's turned corners before, but this is turning the corner and moving down the road because there's a number of quarters of positive growth, it's not simply exports."  \par \par \uc2 Taylor said Tokyo was making progress not only in restoring economic growth, but in whittling down a mountain of bad loans left from the 1990s slump and in quelling deflation, or a downward spiral of prices.  \par \par \uc2 All of this was contributing to Japan's brightened economic prospects, he said. "On deflation, it's also turned the corner and looks like it's moving down the road but that will require continued effort on monetary policy," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has been following a policy of quantitative easing - flooding financial markets with liquidity through easy credit - to try to stop deflation. Taylor said it was impossible to say when that policy or the price drops would end.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no way to determine it precisely, it depends upon events," Taylor said, noting the Bank of Japan had clearly stated it would maintain the policy until inflation was greater than zero and that it was not yet there.  \par \par \uc2 "We're getting closer because you can see deflation becoming less of a problem but it's not possible to say when," Taylor said.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor declined a direct answer when asked whether the United States was pleased that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had cut back on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb rises in the yen, selling yen for dollars in order to prevent exports from suffering.  \par \par \uc2 "We're pleased that the economy is picking up and that the monetary policy, a policy that has been dealing with purchasing \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and domestic securities, is responding as the economy is picking up," Taylor said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040512e05c000tx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
