\par\par \b \uc2 OECD: Japan Consumer Prices To Rise Slightly In 2005  \b0\par\par\uc2 952 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Mai 2004\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese consumer prices are set to rise slightly next year, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Tuesday, as faster-than-expected economic growth helps the world's second-biggest economy escape a years-long deflationary slide.  \par \par \uc2 However, the Paris-based group warned that Tokyo must forge ahead with vital structural changes - including a cleanup of banks' huge pile of bad loans - if it hopes to beat deflation for good and ensure long-term economic growth.  \par \par \uc2 But it was optimistic that recent export-led gains have begun to translate into a broad-based recovery that will lift consumer prices, which have fallen since 1998.  \par \par \uc2 "The pick-up in world trade and steady increase in domestic demand, buoyed by some improvement in the labor market, are expected to help sustain growth near 3% through 2005," the OECD said in its semi-annual economic outlook.  \par \par \uc2 "Such growth would make this Japan's longest upturn since the 1980s and help bring deflation to an end," the OECD said.  \par \par \uc2 The group projected that Japan's gross domestic product would grow 3.0% in 2004 and 2.8% in 2005 in price-adjusted terms. In its previous report in November, it forecast more modest 1.8% expansions in both years.  \par \par \uc2 Consumer prices are expected to edge up 0.1% in 2005 after declining 0.2% this year, the OECD said. In November it forecast annual falls of 0.2% through 2005.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, we see deflation coming to an end next year, in 2005, based on CPI," said Randall Jones, a senior OECD economist.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Recovery Lifting All Sectors \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tuesday's report marked a sharp change in tone from the OECD's gloomier November outlook, which highlighted the wide gap between booming export firms and the struggling domestic sector, and foresaw no end to falling prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest report suggests Japan's recovery has gathered enough pace to put the end of deflation in sight - a brighter view that reflects the economy's 6.4% annualized surge in the quarter through December. Analysts expect growth in the January-March quarter, due to be announced May 18, to top 3.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crucially, Japan's current growth spurt has begun to lift all sectors of the economy, the OECD said, and doesn't depend on massive increases in government spending - a feature of the failed recoveries of the 1990s.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Although this expansion has been concentrated in key exporting industries, the most recent profit trends suggest that the recovery may be spreading to the service sector and to smaller companies," the OECD said. "In contrast to the past two upturns, the current expansion is being driven by private demand, without a large contribution from fiscal stimulus."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To be sure, the "deflation picture remains mixed," the OECD said, with another measure of prices, the GDP deflator, set to fall 1.8% this year and 1.1% in 2005. Continued declines in land prices, as well as falling bank lending, present "significant headwinds" for the economy, it said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the group welcomed stepped-up efforts by the Bank of Japan to fight deflation, noting the BOJ's decision in January to raise its target for liquidity, measured by commercial banks' account balances at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , by Y3 trillion to a range of Y30 trillion-Y35 trillion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 To ensure deflation doesn't return, the BOJ should maintain its ultra-loose credit policy even if prices begin to rise slightly, the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Monetary policy should continue the quantitative easing strategy until positive inflation is achieved on a sustained basis and the risk of deflation becomes negligible," the OECD said, echoing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's own policy target.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It's unclear when price rises will be sufficiently sharp or long-running to merit a tightening of BOJ policy, the OECD's Jones said, but "we don't see it happening next year."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The group also said major banks have made "significant progress" in reducing their mountain of bad loans - a major obstacle to the BOJ's stimulus effort - and are on track to reach the government's target of cutting dud loans to 4% of all lending by March 2005.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Support For \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan Forex Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the economy's stronger footing, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  remains vulnerable to economic shocks, the OECD said. One perennial threat is the country's sky-high public debt, which is expected to hit 160% of GDP this year despite a government austerity drive.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A slowdown in world trade - still the most important engine of Japanese growth - could also threaten the recovery, the OECD said. It noted that demand from China, where it expects growth to slow to around 8.0% this year and next from a runaway 9.2% rate in 2003, accounted for two thirds of Japan's export growth last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, Jones said Japanese exports "should still be able to grow" even if China's economy slows, as increased demand from the U.S., South Korea and other countries fills the gap.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The OECD reiterated its support for Japan's aggressive campaign since early last year to stem the yen's rise against the dollar - an effort it said has complimented the BOJ's monetary easing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on an unprecedented scale, to slow upward pressure on the exchange rate, has also helped to keep monetary conditions relaxed," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo sold a record Y20 trillion in 2003 and three-quarters that much again in the first three months of this year fighting the dollar's slide to three-year lows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale [ 11-05-04 0900GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040511e05b000hr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
