\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's MOF denies change in  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  policy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2004\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, May 6 (Reuters) - A senior Japanese Finance Ministry official denied on Thursday the government had dropped its policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed, after data last week showed it had held back from selling the yen in April.  \par \par \uc2 "There has been no change in our stance," Hiroshi Watanabe, head of the ministry's international bureau, told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 "We will act if needed, and won't if not needed," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in April, the first month of inaction since April 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Authorities sold 15.1991 trillion yen ($139.7 billion) from January to March in an effort to ease pressure on Japanese exporters from a strong yen. That followed an annual record of 20 trillion yen worth of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in calendar 2003.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe also denied the halt in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was triggered by pressure from overseas governments, which have in the past criticised Tokyo for giving its exporters an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 "Our decisions aren't affected by other countries," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has often been a controversial subject among the world's top financial authorities, but it was barely mentioned at a recent meeting of Group of Seven (G7) officials amid a perceived halt in Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar's rise.  \par \par \uc2 G7 finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs met in Washington late last month and the ministers meet again on their own in New York around May 22 and 23.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said little had changed since the April meeting, suggesting little attention to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at the next meeting.  \par \par \uc2 However, he cited a rise in oil prices as a concern.  \par \par \uc2 "Oil prices have extended their rise since the last time (the G7) met, so that's one worry," he said.  \par \par \uc2 He added the ministers would discuss fighting "terrorism funding" and lay the groundwork for a later meeting of G7 leaders. ($1=108.73 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040506e056001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
