\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  in April-MOF.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2004\par 12:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in April, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday, underscoring market speculation that Japan's 15-month campaign to keep yen strength in check may be near an end.  \par \par \uc2 The absence of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the period from March 30 to April 27 compared with 4.7026 trillion yen ($42.93 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March, the third-largest monthly total on record.  \par \par \uc2 It was the first month since April 2003 that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The data confirms recent market speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has taken its foot off the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pedal, partly encouraged by a ream of upbeat data on the Japanese economy that suggests Japan's export-led recovery is taking hold.  \par \par \uc2 "It's no real surprise," said Tomoko Fujii, economic and market analysis director at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no reason to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the current levels, but I think they'll think about it again if the dollar challenges the 100 yen mark."  \par \par \uc2 Within the period covered by the data, the dollar fell to a four-year low of 103.40 yen on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 That dip was prompted partly by speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was stepping back from its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which the market saw as one of the only supports for a dollar under pressure from a gaping U.S. current account deficit that hit record levels in 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has received a boost from a recent crop of upbeat data in the U.S. jobs market that raised expectations the Federal Reserve will move by August to lift its key overnight funds rate from a 46-year low of one percent.  \par \par \uc2 By April 27, the end of the period covered by Friday's data, the greenback had rebounded 4.5 percent to around 108 yen from its four-year low against the yen, supported by hopes of an imminent U.S. rate rise.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly after the April \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figure was announced it was at around 110.20 yen  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities sold 15.1991 trillion yen from January to March in an effort to ease pressure on Japanese exporters from a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 That followed an annual record of 20 trillion yen in calendar 2003.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  OVER?  \par \par \uc2 Despite repeated, aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past year, the yen continued to rise because of a recovering economy and a rising Tokyo stock market, as well as concern about geopolitical risks that undermined the dollar against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been criticised by its trade partners, who say that keeping the yen artificially low gives Japan an unfair trade advantage.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say that given improving economic fundamentals, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are becoming harder to justify.  \par \par \uc2 "If the current Japanese economic expansion continues, the stronger yen will likely be accompanied by an improvement in domestic demand," said Koji Fukaya, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "A fall in the dollar to 100 yen would be inevitable, but the sort of large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we've seen in the past would become more difficult, if not unnecessary."  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's closely watched "tankan" survey of business sentiment released early in April showed that Japanese companies thought business was better in March than at any time in almost seven years.  \par \par \uc2 That came after data showing Japan's gross domestic product expanded at its fastest pace in 13 years in the final three months of last year, clocking growth of 6.4 percent on an annualised basis in real terms. (Additional reporting by Satomi Noguchi and Ritsuko Ando) ($1=109.52 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040430e04u001aw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
