\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  did not  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  in April-MOF.  \b0\par\par\uc2 182 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2004\par 12:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 30 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in April, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday, marking the first month in a year that the authorities have not bought dollars in an attempt to curb the strength of the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's announcement was for the period from March 30 to April 27. By comparison, Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March amounted to 4.7026 trillion yen ($42.93 billion), the third-largest monthly total on record.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities sold 15.1991 trillion yen from January to March in their effort to ease pressure on Japanese exporters from a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 That followed an annual record of 20 trillion yen in calendar 2003. The last month in which the Japanese authorities refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  was April 2003.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dipped to a four-year low of 103.40 yen on March 31, and then rebounded 4.5 percent to around 108 yen by April 27.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly after the April \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figure was announced it was at around 110.18 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040430e04u00189\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
