\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -China worries hit yen, dollar claws back.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 626 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2004\par 10:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 30 (Reuters) - The yen hit five-week lows against the euro and lost half a percent against the dollar on Friday as Japanese share prices fell on worries about a possible slowdown in China's economy.  \par \par \uc2 After a Japanese market holiday on Thursday, the Tokyo stock market's benchmark Nikkei average fell 2.02 percent on Friday, in part a late reaction to comments to Reuters on Wednesday by Chinese Prime Minster Wen Jiabao that China needed to take forceful measures to cool down its economy.  \par \par \uc2 The euro scaled back from nine-day highs against the dollar hit on Thursday after data showing U.S. first-quarter growth was 4.2 percent against 5.0 percent forecast.  \par \par \uc2 "The Asian region has been undermined by the potential threat in China of more forceful action to cool the economy," said Derek Halpenny, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations for a U.S. rate rise have not changed after the GDP data and we expect a move in August."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading around 110.50 yen at 0735 GMT, up nearly half a percent from late New York levels amid Japan's "Golden Week" holidays, but below six-week highs just above 111 yen hit on Thursday. Japanese financial markets will close again for the first three days of next week.  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit its highest since March 22 against the yen at 132.06 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The yen came under further pressure on Friday as Chinese financial markets steeled themselves for the first interest rate hike in nine years to cool the economy, after press reports saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  planned to raise commercial lending rates by half a percentage point in May.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped a third of a percent to $1.1930 from late New York levels compared with a nine-day high of $1.1982 hit after Thursday's U.S. GDP data.  \par \par \uc2 INFLATION UP  \par \par \uc2 Expectations the United States will soon raise interest rates from the current 46-year low of 1.0 percent remained on the boil after the GDP report also showed the Fed's favoured inflation measure, the core personal consumption expenditure index, rose to 2.0 percent from 1.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan's consumer sentiment survey at 1345 GMT is expected to show that U.S. consumers regained some of their optimism over the course of April.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts are looking for a drop in the consumer sentiment index to 94.0 from a final reading in March of 95.8 but this would mark an increase from the beginning of April when the index was as low as 93.2.  \par \par \uc2 German retail sales for March showed a nominal decline of 0.5 percent month-on-month, compared with a consensus forecast of a 0.25 percent decline.  \par \par \uc2 The euro zone business sentiment index for April, due at 0900 GMT, is forecast at minus 7.0, unchanged from March. Euro zone flash consumer price data for April, also due at 0900 GMT, is forecast to show a rise of 1.9 percent after rising 1.7 percent in March.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH  \par \par \uc2 Traders were also awaiting data from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1000 GMT on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in April.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 4.7026 trillion yen ($42.93 billion) in March to help support the dollar and protect the country's export-led recovery, but analysts say the figure for April will likely be close to zero.  \par \par \uc2 "Most people think they have completely stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yoshi Yanagisawa, vice president at State Street in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "If there is a positive number it may surprise the market and lead some to buy dollars."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040430e04u000zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
