\par\par \b \uc2 Yen down as Tokyo shares fall on U.S., China worry.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By David McMahon  \par \uc2 697 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 April 2004\par 08:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 30 (Reuters) - The yen weakened slightly on Friday as Japanese share prices fell on worries about rising U.S. interest rates and a possible slowdown in China's economy.   \par \par \uc2  After a Japanese market holiday on Thursday, the Tokyo stock market's benchmark Nikkei average fell 2.02 percent on Friday, in part a late reaction to comments on Wednesday by Chinese Prime Minster Wen Jiabao that China needed to take forceful measures to cool down its economy.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was around 110.17 as of 0600 GMT, a touch higher than late New York levels in quiet trade amid Japan's "Golden Week" holidays. Japanese financial markets will close again for the first three days of next week.   \par \par \uc2  "Signals of inflation picking up in China and the U.S., despite the overnight (GDP) disappointment, are weighing a little on the yen," said Naomi Fink, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas.   \par \par \uc2  Traders were also awaiting data from Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  due at 7 p.m. (1000 GMT) on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the month of April.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 4.7026 trillion yen ($42.93 billion) in March to help support the dollar and protect the country's export-led recovery, but analysts say the figure for April will likely be close to zero.   \par \par \uc2  "Most people think they have completely stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Yoshi Yanagisawa, vice president at State Street in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2  "If there is a positive number it may surprise the market and lead some to buy dollars."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar touched a one-and-a-half-month high of 111.04 yen on Thursday as the yen was hit by worries of a slowdown in the Chinese economy, which will soon replace the U.S. as Japan's biggest export market if the current pace of growth continues.   \par \par \uc2  Some said these worries were a little overblown.   \par \par \uc2  "Japanese exports are unlikely to disappear overnight and they should continue expanding despite a rate hike in the United States and even despite a rate hike in China, said Fink, who still expects the dollar to fell below 100 yen later this year.   \par \par \uc2  CHINA WORRIES   \par \par \uc2  The euro was almost unbudged from late New York levels at around $1.1967 compared with a nine-day high of $1.1983 hit after Thursday's U.S. GDP data.   \par \par \uc2  The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed little immediate reaction to German retail sales data, which showed a nominal decline of 0.5 percent month-on-month, compared with a consensus forecast of a 0.25 percent decline.   \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, worries about the Chinese economy and commodity prices also hit the Australian dollar, which fell 0.80 percent to around $0.7170 before springing back to around $0.7215. The Aussie fell two percent earlier this week.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was helped by views that U.S. gross domestic product data issued on Thursday, while below forecast, hardly dented the market's belief that the Federal Reserve will raise its overnight funds rate by August from a 46-year low of one percent.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. first-quarter growth figure was 4.2 percent instead of the 5.0 percent forecast, sending the dollar reeling on expectations the Fed might not be as quick to raise interest rates as the market had anticipated.   \par \par \uc2  But closer inspection of the GDP report showed the Fed's favoured inflation measure, the core personal consumption expenditures index, rose to 2.0 percent from 1.2 percent, reigniting dollar buying on chances of a rate increase.   \par \par \uc2  "If anything the GDP data endorsed expectations of higher U.S. interest rates in the future. Yesterday's data did not give us any reason to expect a major adjustment in the dollar," said Kikuko Takeda, a market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.   \par \par \uc2  The University of Michigan's consumer sentiment survey at 1345 GMT is expected to show that U.S. consumers regained some of their optimism over the course of April.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts are looking for a drop in the consumer sentiment index to 94.0 from a final reading in March of 95.8. (Additional reporting by Hideyuki Sano).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040430e04u000q9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
