\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Tanigaki says G7 did not debate  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 2004\par 04:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 27 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Tuesday that the Group of Seven countries had not discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy at their meeting in Washington last week.  \par \par \uc2 "There was no debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," Tanigaki said in response to questions at a parliamentary committee.  \par \par \uc2 He added that he had agreed with U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets were relatively stable.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has spent some 35 trillion yen ($322 billion) in the last 15 months in buying dollars to smooth what it said was excess volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said Japan's G7 partners were not necessarily critical of Tokyo's policies, adding that such views expressed in the media were "rather masochistic".  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki reiterated that the government had no problems in raising yen funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , despite extending an agreement for it to temporarily sell its foreign bond holdings to the Bank of Japan (BOJ) in return for such funding.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF and the BOJ agreed last December to set up a framework under which the government could sell its foreign bonds under repurchase agreements. The deal was set to end in March but was extended to the end of June.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said the agreement was extended to smooth funding, and not because there were problems in procuring funds.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ is holding some 6.1 trillion yen of foreign securities under that agreement.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF took the step to sell foreign bonds as a stop-gap measure as it approached a limit for issuing short-term government paper to fund its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The government normally borrows yen by issuing financing bills (FBs) to pay for its yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ temporarily takes on the FBs, which are then sold to the market. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stopped the routine underwriting of FBs.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui told the same parliamentary committee that temporarily holding FBs issued for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which he said amounted to about six trillion yen, would not hurt the BOJ's balance sheet.  \par \par \uc2 "The underwriting of FBs is not an obstacle for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's financial health," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The government, meanwhile, has raised its borrowing limit for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 140 trillion yen in the fiscal year that started this month. ($1=108.64 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040427e04r0006l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
