\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar to refocus on rates,G7 sings same  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  tune.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2004\par 17:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 25 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar will likely refocus on interest rates and the economy when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets open on Monday after top finance officials at the weekend stuck to old verbiage on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the fact that Group of Seven finance ministers and central bankers repeated verbatim their prior meeting's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  language was what the market had expected so it should not ruffle the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Finance officials from the world's leading economic powers met on Saturday in Washington to consider the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 The unchanged wording could leave the greenback's recovery path since February's G7 meeting in Boca Raton, Florida, intact, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The Washington G7 communique "is really status quo. We are basically in the same mode as in Boca," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Other analysts agreed.  \par \par \uc2 "The fact that the section on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  is completely unchanged shows that everyone is relatively pleased with what has happened since the last meeting," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with ABN Amro in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 DISORDER UNDESIRABLE  \par \par \uc2 The Washington G7 communique repeated that excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates were undesirable, while again urging more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in nations that did not have flexible systems.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts interpreted the volatility wording to mean that the G7 was still wary of any resurgence of euro strength, although the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed some 7 percent against the dollar since the February meeting.  \par \par \uc2 As for the yen, some traders had warned prior to Saturday's meeting of a slight risk \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be criticized for its hefty yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year.  \par \par \uc2 The absence of any direct G7 rebuke of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  for its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  could help the dollar move modestly higher against the yen when markets open on Monday, to the 109.30 yen area, said Kasumovich of Merrill Lynch.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late on Friday in New York, the dollar traded at 108.91 yen . The euro\par was down 0.53 percent at $1.1840 against the dollar, more than 10 cents\par below the euro's record highs above $1.19 hit in mid-February. The\par dollar traded at 1.3150 Swiss francs       . Sterling        fell to\par $1.7726.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategists agreed the G7 meeting has left near-term dollar sentiment fairly bullish.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The central catalyst for the greenback remains signs of robust U.S. economic growth and gathering anticipation that the Federal Reserve may be paving the way to raise key interest rates from 1958 lows of 1 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Higher U.S. interest rates would likely help the dollar by luring foreign investors into U.S. assets, analysts reckon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The trends in the market right now seems much more focused on U.S. interest rates and the conviction that rates will go up in the United States sooner than later (than on the G7)," said Carl Weinberg, chief economist of High Frequency Economics in Valhalla, New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The dollar will probably get a bounce up on Monday."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar gained on Friday as a robust U.S. durable goods report and comments from Federal Reserve officials strengthened the view that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would raise rates before year's end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think the G7 statement itself is going to do anything for euro/dollar but I think (the euro) will be moving towards $1.16 over the next few weeks," as hedging shifts by asset managers and European corporations weigh on the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said ABN Amro's Anderson.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040425e04p006px\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
