\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  says "smoothing"  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  justified.   \b0\par\par\uc2 394 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 April 2004\par 04:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 25 (Reuters) - Japanese Economics Minister Heizo Takenaka said on Sunday that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen sudden changes in exchange rates is necessary and Tokyo will continue to seek stability in the market.   \par \par \uc2 He denied the notion that Japan was trying to drive down the yen to gain an unfair trade advantage, stressing that its huge \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last year was aimed at countering excessive volatility.   \par \par \uc2 "The Finance Ministry has not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to push up or push down the dollar or the yen," Takenaka said in a talk show on public broadcaster NHK.   \par \par \uc2 "But because corporations make fixed investments and hire people, every businessman says he doesn't want a sudden change in the rates, and we must do some sort of smoothing. Our stance remains unchanged and this is something that is accepted around the world."   \par \par \uc2 The comments come a day after finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs from the Group of Seven met in Washington and issued a communique that repeated their previous statement that excess volatility and disorderly movements in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates were undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 The communique also called for more flexibility in exchange rates of countries that lacked such flexibility - a message sometimes interpreted in the market as a criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well as China's policy pegging the yuan to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Japan conducted a record 20 trillion yen ($183 billion) of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and has already thrown in a further 15 trillion yen in the first three months of this year to prevent the yen from rising out of control.   \par \par \uc2 The yen rose to four-year highs around 103.40 to the dollar earlier this month, rising some 10 percent since the G7 first introduced the "flexibility" wording in its communique in September.   \par \par \uc2 But since an apparent scaling back in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the last several weeks, the yen has retreated and was trading around 109 to the dollar last week.   \par \par \uc2 A pickup in the domestic sectors now contributed two-thirds of overall growth unlike a widespread perception that Japan's economic recovery was driven by rising exports, Takenaka said.   \par \par \uc2 The important policy for Japan was to further promote structural reform in the domestic sectors to enable sustained home-grown growth, he added. ($1=109.36 Yen).    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040425e04p000ei\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
