\par\par \b \uc2 Japan To Have First Easy G7 In 15 Yrs; Not Off Hook Yet  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Takeuchi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 991 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 April 2004\par 09:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Japan's top finance and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials will get a taste of something they haven't had in more than a decade when they attend a high-power meeting here this weekend - peace and quiet.  \par \par \uc2 Not since the bubble days of the late 1980s have Japan's finance chiefs escaped a chiding from their Group of Seven counterparts on their handling of the world's second-biggest economy. But their respite now doesn't mean they're off the hook completely. The Finance Ministry and Bank of Japan will still have plenty to worry about when they return to Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese economy increasingly shows signs it has embarked on its first durable recovery since 1989 and deflation has eased notably. But the threat that damaging price falls may return continues to hang over policy-makers' heads.  \par \par \uc2 For now, anyway, as they huddle with other G7 officials on the sidelines of the International Monetary Fund and World Bank annual meetings, Tokyo officials should have an easy time of it.  \par \par \uc2 "Carefree or not, at least we don't have to keep a low profile low this time, fearing some G7 colleague would make an issue of something about the Japanese economy and policies," a senior Japanese official close to the G7 preparations told Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials will have the easiest time since the G7 meeting in February 1989, said longtime Japan watcher Yasunari Ueno, chief market economist at Mizuho Securities. Ueno's entire write-up of that meeting was: "The G7 has effectively endorsed the current levels of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The other issue was the restructuring of Latin America's debts" - a problem that had only minor implications at the time for Japan's then-robust banks.  \par \par \uc2 In those days, the Japanese economic juggernaut was inspiring fear and envy worldwide, Tokyo's benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average had recently peaked at 38915 and the dollar was worth about Y130. The Nikkei closed Friday at 12120.66 and around 0700 GMT the dollar bought Y109.38.  \par \par \uc2 In the years between, the Nikkei sank to a two-decade low of 7603.76 in April last year while the dollar caromed between Y79 and Y140, roiled by seemingly endless bad news about the Japanese economy.  \par \par \uc2 After the stock and property bubble burst, Japan went through a series of recessions and fitful recoveries, punctuated by crises such as the collapse of several major banks and the government rescue of many others.  \par \par \uc2 In recent years, prices have fallen steadily, depressing economic activity and worsening the burden on Japan's legions of debtors.  \par \par \uc2 The upheavals always gave Japan's G7 counterparts - the U.S., Germany, the U.K., France, Italy and Canada - something to gripe about.  \par \par \uc2 Most recently it has been \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gigantic campaign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the strong yen from snuffing out an export-led recovery. The Finance Ministry sold Y35 trillion through the BOJ in the 15 months through March to prop up the dollar. This was meant to help exporters by keeping their products from getting too expensive abroad and to help fight deflation at home.  \par \par \uc2 But for the past month, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems abruptly to have stopped \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We hardly notice the hands of the BOJ in our daily dealings these days," said Minoru Shioiri, senior manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 And yet, although the dollar tumbled to a four-year low Y103.40 when the ministry withdrew from the market, it has drifted back up as traders have increasingly focused on the dollar-positive prospect of a U.S. interest rate hike by the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par               Deflation Not Banished \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But Japan's not out of the woods yet. The biggest danger remains the five-year drop in consumer prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consumer price index was unchanged in February from the previous month and from a year earlier, but analysts - and the BOJ itself - say deflation hasn't been banished.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Price declines have narrowed partly due to one-time factors including rises in government taxes and charges last year, and higher rice prices due to a poor harvest.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Underlying deflation is perhaps around 0.5%," David Burton, the IMF's Asia director, told Dow Jones Newswires this week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BOJ has been fighting deflation since 2001 by pinning short-term interest rates essentially at zero and flooding the banking system with excess cash. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is committed to maintaining this "quantitative easing" until on-year CPI changes are sustained at zero or higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recently BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui has gone further, saying it's too early to predict an end to the current policy. One person close to the BOJ told Dow Jones Newswires the bank wouldn't consider tightening policy as long as CPI rises stay below 1.0%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In October, BOJ policy board forecasts for CPI centered on a fall of 0.3%, with only one of the nine members predicting prices would turn up. The board will issue updated forecasts next week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former top Finance Ministry official Haruhiko Kuroda said this week that the BOJ must remain on guard. If \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were to decrease, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have to find some "innovative measures to further ease monetary conditions," he told reporters in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's recent past has also shown you can never rule out an economic shock, as DaimlerChrysler's surprise decision Thursday to stop pumping money into ailing Mitsubishi Motors Corp. shows.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's contingent at the G7 should enjoy their rare reprieve in Washington. They may not be able to make a habit of it.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com; 1-202-862-9285 \par    (Hiroshi Inoue in Tokyo and Tom Barkley in New York contributed to this item.) \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 23-04-04 0720GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040423e04n000b4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
