\par\par \b \uc2 Foreign central banks still buying U.S. debt - Fed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 204 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2004\par 22:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 22 (Reuters) - Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings of U.S. debt have hit another record high, the Federal Reserve reported on Thursday, which should ease concerns in the market about a slump in foreign demand for Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed said its total holdings of Treasury and agency debt kept for offshore central banks rose $4.715 billion to $1.186 trillion in the week ended April 21.  \par \par \uc2 The breakdown of the Fed's custody holdings showed overseas central banks bought a net $5.479 billion of Treasuries, taking their holdings to $957.442 billion.  \par \par \uc2 But they also sold a net $765 million in agency debt, taking those holdings to $229.331 billion.  \par \par \uc2 The increase came despite speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had sharply reined in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently, leaving it with fewer dollars to invest in U.S. debt.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ, and some other Asian central banks, have been huge buyers of Treasury and agency debt in the past year as they parked dollars bought in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The average of daily custody holdings rose by $5.757 billion from the average of the previous week, to stand at $1.186 trillion.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040422e04m002uv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
