\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Slips Vs Euro, Yen As Mkt Takes Breather  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 April 2004\par 07:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell against the euro and the yen in Asia Thursday as market players cooled off after an early bout of dollar buying and adjusted positions while they contemplated their next moves.  \par \par \uc2 The Tokyo session kicked off with a flurry of euro sales for dollars, coming after players concluded that U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's second day of Congressional testimony Wednesday didn't contradict the emerging view that the Fed is preparing markets for an interest rate hike in coming months.  \par \par \uc2 The euro sank to a five-month low of $1.1781, slipping below $1.1800 for first time since November. The dollar also rose briefly to a high of Y109.65.  \par \par \uc2 But the greenback reversed course due to players' lack of confidence to keep buying dollars.  \par \par \uc2 "The market seems to have decided to stop selling dollars. But it hasn't yet concluded that buying dollars should be the next big thing," said Minoru Shioiri, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Market players' neutralizing of short-dollar positions has been helping the dollar rebound against its major counterparts, but the lack of conviction among investors to build new positions has kept the greenback's gains in check.  \par \par \uc2 Despite increased focus on the outlook for U.S. interest rates, global terrorism fears continue to weigh on financial markets as a whole, traders said. They add that the dollar's strength against the yen is being offset to an extent by capital inflows into Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "It's hard to get a sense of the dollar's direction right now," said Masamichi Koike, a vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sumitomo-Mitsui Bank. "We're probably going to move higher, but it won't be easy going."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y109.17, below Y109.42 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.79 late Wednesday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1835, up from the session low of $1.1781, but still lower than $1.1839 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1834 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y129.21, down from Y129.56 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y128.78 in Tokyo late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.7714, down from $1.7742 in New York and lower than $1.7825 in Tokyo Wednesday. [ 22-04-04 0528GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Some Tokyo players said that with Greenspan's testimony out of the way, the next immediate focus would be the weekend meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 But many in the market expect the meeting to be something of a non-event, as \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely to be a major topic of discussion like they were at the previous two G7 gatherings, in Dubai in September, and Boca Raton in February.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, including Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki, have said that Tokyo's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also unlikely to come under scrutiny, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is already widely understood by the G7. And while Tokyo has never acknowledged a policy shift, it appears to have ended its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drive in late March.  \par \par \uc2 "Our policy is based on the consensus among G7 countries that exchange rates should move stably, in line with economic fundamentals," Tanigaki said Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein's Zahra Ward-Murphy in a research note said, "With the euro/dollar now lower and the Japanese appearing to have scaled back their \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts of late, the focus of the meeting may shift to growth. This could weigh on the euro/dollar."  \par \par \uc2 "The recent pick up in the U.S. economy has served to bring the poor performance of the Eurozone economy sharply back into focus. Any official criticism of the Eurozone and its policies from the G7 could add further downward pressure to the euro/dollar over the coming weeks," Ward-Murphy said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale and Kirsty Mackenzie  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribers can find this table:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX [ 22-04-04 0616GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040422e04m00063\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
