\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: IMF Urges Mkt-Based  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Rates For China, Japan  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Takeuchi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,010 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2004\par 07:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--China should consider altering the yuan's peg to the dollar, while Japan should stick to a market-oriented \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, the Asia head of International Monetary Fund said.  \par \par \uc2 A freer exchange rate for the yuan would help as Beijing steps up efforts to help tame an overheating economy, while Tokyo's case for massive yen-selling has weakened as the economy improves, David Burton, director of IMF's Asia and Pacific Department, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The case is stronger and perhaps the urgency is greater" for China to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, Burton said.  \par \par \uc2 He stressed the IMF hasn't changed its long-standing position of urging China to move toward a more flexible exchange rate at an early date - but also at a time of Beijing's choosing. Yet he recommended China act sooner rather than later because the yuan policy works against China's efforts to prevent its economy from overheating.  \par \par \uc2 Keeping the yuan, or renminbi, essentially pegged around 8.28 to the dollar requires the Chinese monetary authorities to buy the dollars earned by the nation's exporters, dumping a large volume of yuan in local markets and ramping up liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 As many analysts and even Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan have noted, this large liquidity provision makes it harder for the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to tighten policy to curb massive investment and lending to red-hot sectors such as property. The yuan peg highlights the difficulties in China's attempts to rewrite the economy textbooks by simultaneously controlling interest rates and the exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 China has long said that complete yuan liberalization is its ultimate aim but that it must move cautiously, for example by shoring up local banks first. Many analysts expect China will at least allow a slightly wider trading band for the yuan in coming quarters, and the market for nondeliverable yuan forwards is pricing in a 3.9% yuan rise over the next 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 Burton acknowledged there could be unfavorable side-effects from moving toward a more flexible regime, such as a temporary decline in foreign capital inflows. But if capital control measures are maintained at in the early stages of such changes, he said, this would pre-empt what the Chinese authorities regard as the biggest problem in freeing the exchange-rate system - a massive drainage of Chinese savings from domestic banks into foreign banks.  \par \par \uc2 On the overheating of the Chinese economy - which surged 9.7% in the first quarter from the same period last year - Burton warned of many unfavorable consequences, such as inflaming the problem of bad loans that plague the nation's banks.  \par \par \uc2 "There is a very considerable risk that the rapid credit growth that's fueling investment will result in a reversal in the downward trend of nonperforming loans," Burton said. "Not all this investment is viable in the medium run."  \par \par \uc2 Cleaning up banks' nonperforming loans, which built up through politically motivated lending to state-owned enterprises in the old days, is a priority task for Beijing ahead of its full opening of banking \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to foreign banks in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 To prevent overheating from developing into a hard landing for the economy, China needs further monetary squeezing steps now, the IMF director said. "More needs to be done" to cool down its excessive investment and credit growth, Burton said.  \par \par \uc2 "The authorities are well aware of the need," he said. "But we are not still there."  \par \par \uc2 To curb credit growth - especially to the property, auto, steel, cement and aluminum sectors - China has tightened prudential controls on lending and raised the reserve requirement for commercial banks three times in less than a year. Speculation is growing that Beijing might also raise interest rates.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Outlived Usefulness \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Burton also said \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's earlier record-setting \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had outlived their usefulness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo's massive sales of yen for dollars since last year made sense in fighting "dire" deflation, he said. But now that the Japanese economy apparently has gotten back on a sustained recovery track and deflation has eased a little, "the case for a large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as the recovery proceeds, becomes much weaker," Burton said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Finance Ministry sold an eye-popping Y35.5 trillion through the Bank of Japan to buy upwards of $300 billion in the 15 months through March to prop up the dollar and battle the yen's climb to four-year highs. But in the past three weeks, facing international criticism for its heavy-handed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  even as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's economic recovery accelerated, Tokyo has apparently abandoned its aggressive yen sales.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the early days of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  campaign, Burton said, "My personal view is that it was warranted" - not to control exchange rates but as a monetary tool to fight falling prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When Tokyo began \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  aggressively in the middle of last year, "Deflation was still severe and even long-term interests rate were very close to zero," he said. "None of the normal instruments of the monetary policies were \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the worst is over and the IMF thinks Japan is in "what appears to be a sustained recovery," Burton said. Economic acceleration was so strong around the end of last year that 2004 economic growth, with some of its carry-over effects, will likely be well "in excess of 3%," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remaining big problem for the Japanese economy is deflation, the director said. He acknowledged that the problem "has been ameliorating" as evidenced by the recent data on price indexes.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet, he said, "underlying deflation is perhaps around 0.5%" and in the IMF's annual discussions of the economy and policy with Japanese officials in May, the issue of how to further tackle the problem "will undoubtedly become one of the subjects," Burton said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takeshi Takeuchi, Dow Jones Newswires; Takeshi.Takeuchi@dowjones.com; 1-202-862-9285 \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 21-04-04 0547GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040421e04l0006x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
