\par\par \b \uc2 Global Growth Prospects Have Improved -German IMF Source  \b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 April 2004\par 14:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT (Dow Jones)--Global economic growth prospects have improved, with the U.S., China and Japan taking the lead and Europe lagging behind, a member of the German delegation for the International Monetary Fund spring meeting said Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The focus of the IMF spring meeting this weekend in the U.S. will be the sustainability of this economic upswing, the senior German IMF official, who declined to be named, said.  \par \par \uc2 The official said global growth in 2004 is likely to reach around 4.5%, mainly driven by strong growth in the U.S., China and Japan. The U.S. is likely to muster around 4.5% growth in 2004, while China and Japan will have growth rates above 8% and 3%, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 Growth rates for Europe, particularly for continental Europe, however, are expected to disappoint, the official said. He confirmed that German economic growth in 2004 is likely to be at the bottom of the 1.5%-2.0% range forecast by the German government.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF will present its World Economic Outlook with the latest projections for global and regional growth Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 But the senior official also said some risks to the sustainability of the global economic upswing remain.  \par \par \uc2 "The question about the sustainability of the current upswing continues to exist, and there are a number of risks which will be discussed at the upcoming IMF meeting," the senior delegation member said.  \par \par \uc2 The source mentioned geopolitical issues like terrorism, oil price developments, global imbalances between the major world regions as some of the main risks to the current scenario.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk stemming from oil price developments, in particular, previously has been underestimated," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 He said the euro zone has been spared the full impact of oil prices rising to over $30 a barrel due to the appreciation of the euro against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 However, "the appreciation of the euro hasn't fully compensated for the rise in the dollar-price for oil," the official said.  \par \par \uc2 He noted that a cut in the oil production quota, the U.S. topping up its oil reserves, and strong demand for raw materials like oil, particularly from China, are behind the rise in the oil price. [ 20-04-04 1229GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The continued global imbalances, particularly the U.S. current account deficit, "pose a risk as they might lead to an abrupt and drastic adjustment of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates," the German IMF delegate said.  \par \par \uc2 He said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate developments have calmed somewhat since finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrial nations last met in Boca Raton, Florida, in February.  \par \par \uc2 "Therefore, I don't expect any dramatic change in the discussion about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements at the G7 meeting," the senior official said.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 will hold a regular meeting Friday ahead of the spring IMF meeting over the weekend. In February, the G7 warned against "disorderly" movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The official said there are signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't continuing its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in the same way as the country did at the start of the year.  \par \par \uc2 He also said that he doesn't know of any recommendations by the IMF that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should lower interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "As far as I'm concerned, the IMF's position is that the ECB interest rates are appropriate," the senior official said.  \par \par \uc2 Room to maneuver interest rates lower "could come into existence, but whether this room needs to be used, is a different question," the senior IMF delegate said.  \par \par \uc2 The German IMF delegation member expressed some concern about excessive liquidity being available worldwide at the moment, stemming from low interest rates and an IMF policy which gives countries easy access to IMF credit.  \par \par \uc2 "We are pushing a massive wave of liquidity ahead of us, and we are concerned about that," the German IMF delegate said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Klaus Brune, Dow Jones Newswires; +49 69 29 725 500; klaus.brune@dowjones.com [ 20-04-04 1241GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040420e04k000ng\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
