\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Ex-MOF Kuroda Sees End To Large  \highlight22\uc2 Forex Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 2004\par 01:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Haruhiko Kuroda, a former \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, said Tuesday he expects "huge" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities to come to an end due to the rebound in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda, former vice minister of finance for international affairs and now adviser to the Japanese cabinet, said that with the dollar expected to recover by the middle of the year amid a strengthening U.S. economy, "large-scale \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may no longer be needed."  \par \par \uc2 While \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been less effective recently, it has also become less necessary due to increasing signs of a Japanese economic recovery and the communique issued at the February meeting of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations, which warned against "disorderly" markets.  \par \par \uc2 Still, Kuroda said that it is too early to end accommodative monetary conditions in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , because deflation has not disappeared. And if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to decrease, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to find some "innovative measures to further ease monetary conditions," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-20-04 1943ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking to reporters after a panel discussion cosponsored by Citigroup Inc. (C) and the Japan Society, Kuroda said that he didn't expect exchange rates to be a big topic of discussion at this weekend's G7 meeting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said that since \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets have stabilized following the February G7 meeting and the dollar has recovered, he doesn't expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy to be "a major issue" at the upcoming meeting in Washington.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Kuroda also said it might not be "productive" for G7 officials to press China to move toward a floating yuan at the meeting, since their stance is well understood by the Chinese government.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said that Chinese officials know that the current \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, in which the yuan is effectively pegged to the dollar, is not sustainable. But when and how it is changed and what form it will take is up to China, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Kuroda said that while he doesn't expect the large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese monetary authorities to be necessary because the dollar should continue to recover, he added that MOF's exchange rate and monetary policies haven't changed because of the Japan's persistent deflation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-20-04 2054ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040420e04k0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
