\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Apr 19  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,906 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2004\par 11:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: A weekend pledge by China's top central banker to create a market-driven trading system for the yuan heightened talk Monday that Beijing was on the verge of loosening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg and strengthening the yuan. Though a spokesman at the People's Bank of China told Dow Jones Newswires Monday no change was forthcoming in yuan policy and most local players in the tightly-managed spot market shrugged off the talk, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slight gain against the U.S. dollar Monday highlighted nervousness ahead of key meetings later this week among the Group of Seven leading industrial nations, the World Bank and the International Monetary Fund. ("=UPDATE: China Yuan Revaluation Talk Restarts Ahead Of G7," ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's retail sales swelled in the first quarter, helped by a surge in spending over the "Golden Week" holiday in January, while an increase in food costs drove a rise in the consumer price index, data from the National Bureau of Statistics published Monday show. ("China 1Q Retail Sales Up On Golden Week; Food Drives CPI," ran at 0501 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: All's well that ends well. At least that must be the hope of Japanese policy-makers who seem to have ended their prolonged, all-out campaign to weaken the yen without wreaking havoc on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Soul-Searching After \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Spree," first take ran at 0839 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: A good showing by India's ruling Bharatiya Janata Party in the monthlong elections starting Tuesday would finally give the party a mandate to push through a number of politically sensitive economic measures - and would also mean strong gains in the stock market. (Story will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong borrowers may not have to worry about an imminent rise in their interest expenses, despite market expectations of a tightening in U.S. monetary policy sometime this year. In the past, Hong Kong consumers have braced for higher interest rates when U.S. borrowing costs rise because of the territory's U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg. However, it may be different this time round because local banks haven't kept in step with several Federal Reserve easings in the past few years. ("=Hong Kong Banks Have Room Not To Follow A US Rate Hike," ran at 0658 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The South Korean government and ruling Uri Party agreed Monday that the government should keep up its "aggressive expansionary" stance in operating macroeconomic policy as domestic consumption is still sluggish. ("S Korea Govt, Ruling Party Agree On Expansionary Policy," ran at 0206 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The Taiwan government's plans to sell stakes in state-owned Chinese Petroleum Corp. and Taiwan Power Co. are facing further delay, due to obstacles from lawmakers and employees, Wu Fong-sheng, executive director at Commission of National Corporations, told Dow Jones Newswires. (INTERVIEW will run by around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD: Pakistan's securities regulator plans to set up a financial crime unit to stamp out insider trading and share manipulation, the chairman of the Securities and Exchange Commission of Pakistan told Dow Jones Newswires. ("=INTERVIEW: Pakistan To Set Up Unit To Check Market Abuse," ran at 0813 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong government Monday launched the retail portion of its HK$6 billion issue of securities backed by toll revenue from government-owned bridges and tunnels. It is the Hong Kong government's first-ever asset securitization. ("HK Launches Retail Portion Of HK$6 Bln Toll-Revenue Notes," first take ran at 0804 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Korea Electric Power Corp. has started to offer a $300 million 30-year global bond, with pricing expected as early as Tuesday in New York, a person familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires Monday. Kepco's decision put an end to weeks of waiting and speculation among investors over the exact timing and structure of the new offering by the state-owned utility. ("=S Korea's Kepco Offers $300M 30-Yr Global Bond - Source," ran at 0757 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTIONS VIEWS: Australian dollar-U.S. dollar options remain attractive even after spiking last week on huge moves in the spot market. In fact, the volatility implied by options prices is at a rare deep discount to actual market gyrations. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS:Rare Risk Discount In AUD/USD Volatility," ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra estimated the country's exports grew almost 19% in the first three months of this year compared with the same period last year. ("Thai PM Thaksin: 1Q Exports Up Almost 19% On Year," ran at 0112 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore Monday spelt out its supervisory approach in a new document, highlighting it is prepared to let financial institutions fail and that consumers are responsible for their investment decisions. ("=Singapore MAS: Don't Assume "Zero Failure" For Banks," ran at 0021 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand consumer prices rose at a slower-than-expected rate in the first quarter of 2004, increasing the probability that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave interest rates unchanged at its April 29 review. ("=UPDATE: Low NZ CPI Suggests Rates On Hold At Apr Review," ran at 2327 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The yen retreated against the dollar and the euro in Asia Monday as Japanese investors added more foreign assets to their portfolios and their overseas counterparts dumped faltering Tokyo shares. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Yen Slips Vs Majors As Nikkei Sell-Off Weighs," ran at 0535 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: UFJ Holdings Inc.'s shares plunged Monday after a local newspaper said the Japanese megabank may have Y1 trillion more in nonperforming loans than it has so far acknowledged. The report in the daily Tokyo Shimbun stirred concerns Japan's financial sector may still feel some more tremors before it gets back on solid ground. ("=UPDATE:UFJ Shrs Dive On Bad-Loan Worries; Other Bks Fall," ran at 0825 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Huaneng Power International Inc.'s planned purchase of stakes in five power plants from its parent should boost its generation capacity more than its earnings, but the low acquisition price justifies the deal, analysts said Monday. ("China Huaneng Pwr Gains On Asset Buys; Earnings Seen Up," ran at 0633 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: A tie up between the Philippines' largest cellular phone company Smart Communications Inc. and McDonald's Corp. will see patrons of the U.S. burger giant asked if they would like mobile call credit along with their Big Macs. ("=Philippines' Smart Sells Mobile Services With Big Macs," ran at 0133 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Prices of dynamic random access memory chips have fallen 13% over the past week, but does that mean good times are over for DRAM shares? No. Most analysts and industry players reckon the tight supply of DRAM could last throughout the year, facing its worst crunch in the second half as corporations begin to replace personal computers in earnest. (=ASIA DRAM REPORT will run by around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Foreign investors made a big bet on South Korean shares in the run up to last week's parliamentary election - a bet that appeared to pay off when the liberal Uri Party won a clear majority of seats in that election, heralding a new period of political stability. But now that foreigners own nearly 45% of the South Korean stock market, fund managers say foreign buying is bound to slow, depriving the market of its main engine. ("=ASIA FUND VIEW: S Korea Shrs' Momentum To Slow Post-Vote," ran at 0828 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial Co. said Monday it and Apple Computer Inc. have developed a low-cost editing system for high-definition television broadcasts as the firms aim to take on Sony Corp., the world's leading supplier of professional broadcasting systems. ("Matsushita, Apple Develop New Low-Cost TV Editing System," ran at 0605 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sanyo Electric Co. said Monday it plans to sell liquid crystal display televisions overseas in a bid to increase its LCD TV sales more than 10-fold and take a 5% share of the global market. The Japanese consumer electronics maker said it will also begin sales of DVD recorders in North America and Japan this year. ("Sanyo Elec Plans Overseas LCD TV Sales To Lift Mkt Share," ran at 0436 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian hearing aid maker Cochlear Ltd. said Monday an ongoing U.S. Department of Justice probe into the company's financial relationships with doctors isn't distracting management efforts to revive earnings. ("=INTERVIEW: Cochlear Says US Probe "Not Too Distracting"," ran at 0827 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 YOKKAICHI, Japan: Faster-than-expected growth in the global flash memory card market could help SanDisk Corp.'s annual sales reach $5 billion earlier than it anticipates, the firm's chief executive officer said. ("=INTERVIEW: SanDisk CEO Bullish On Flash Memory Growth," ran at 0833 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Liquid crystal display maker Varitronix International reports 2003 earnings. Thomson First Call's poll projects a net profit rise of more than 30%, while some analysts expect a special cash dividend to mark the company's 25th anniversary. (Story will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Yanzhou Coal Mining Co. said its 2003 net profit rose 13.5%, thanks to higher domestic sales offsetting falling export volume and lower coal prices. (UPDATE will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =NZ WATCH: Investors in New Zealand shares have reason to be pleased with the 6.5% gain in the benchmark NZSX-50 index so far this year. But the stock market could stall from here on, analysts say, as further rises are hampered by slowing domestic economic growth as well as likely interest rate rises in the U.S. ("=NZ WATCH: Shrs Gain So Far This Yr,But Outlook Uncertain," ran at 0145 GMT) [ 19-04-04 0906GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Foreign funds tracking Sri Lanka's volatile postelection stock market see little upside in the short term because of the island's political uncertainties and the view that its shares are overpriced compared with those of other emerging markets. ("=Asia Fund View: Little Upside For Costly Sri Lankan Stks," ran at 0812 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Military Bank PCL is likely to sustain its strong profitability in the coming quarters after posting a 49% growth on year in net profit in its first quarter, the bank's top executive said Monday. ("Thai Military Bk Expects Strong Profitability To Continue," ran at 0449 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Department store operator Daimaru Inc. said Monday its group net profit for the year ended Feb. 29 more than doubled, boosted by cost cuts and a newly-opened store in Sapporo, northern Japan. ("Japan's Daimaru Last FY Group Net Pft Y11.88B Vs Y5.17B," first take ran at 0646 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand casino operator, Sky City Entertainment Group Ltd., Monday said it is making a takeover offer for the balance of shares in cinema unit, Sky City Leisure Ltd., it doesn't already own. ("=UPDATE: NZ Sky City Makes Bid For Balance Of Cinema Unit," ran at 2245 GMT) [ 19-04-04 0906GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040419e04j000fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
