\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar dips as chance of U.S. rate hike seen ebbing.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kininmont  \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2004\par 05:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 19 (Reuters) - The dollar softened against major rivals on Monday, taking a breather from its recent rally as investors had second thoughts about whether the United States would raise interest rates in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said a disappointing batch of U.S. data on Friday as well as remarks by Richmond Federal Reserve President Alfred Broaddus that the Fed was still some way from seeing conditions for a rate hike led dealers to take profits on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Traders expect the dollar to drift ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington on Friday and Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 "We are in a bit of a rangy market at the moment," said Derek Kwok, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Barclays Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "Probably for the next week or two weeks we could well be stuck within a fairly broad 106-110 yen range. For the most part, even far tighter ranges."  \par \par \uc2 At 0231 GMT, the euro was trading at around $1.2028/32 up from $1.1995 in late New York trade on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was also up, fetching $1.8019/24 compared with $1.7970. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2896/01 francs down from 1.2950.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 108.04/09 yen up from 107.85 but well below a one-month peak of 109.28 yen scaled last Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "The two main pillars for the dollar's direction - the U.S. jobs market and monetary policy - have clearly stepped forward now," said Taisuke Tanaka, chief macro strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "But that doesn't mean the dollar will keep rising, as the Fed is likely to be more cautious about hiking rates than earlier expected, especially as the next jobs report is likely to disappoint after surprisingly robust numbers in the previous report."  \par \par \uc2 A rise in U.S. interest rates would enhance the allure of U.S. assets for global investors, helping to lift the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Renewed speculation about an interest rate cut in the euro zone helped support the dollar against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, the Financial Times newspaper reported that the International Monetary Fund would strengthen its calls for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider cutting interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 GREENSPAN, G7 AHEAD  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan also seemed some way away from boosting interest rates from near zero, despite a swell of recent data pointing to a strengthening of the Japanese economy.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Monday that domestic demand was recovering in Japan but it would take a while for consumer prices to begin rising on a year-on-year basis.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has committed itself to maintaining a super-loose monetary policy until it sees a sustained rise in prices.  \par \par \uc2 Although Fukui is due to speak again at 3 p.m. (0600 GMT), the market is focused on Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony in Congress. He appears on both Tuesday and Wednesday, with particular attention on what he will say about the economy on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't expect Greenspan to indicate inflation risks or a rate hike in the first half of this year just yet," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "But if he emphasises the Fed's 'patience'...then that could pour cold water on growing speculation about a rate hike as early as August," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki was quoted by local media as saying that Japan's efforts to hold the yen down were aimed at fighting deflation and would be understood by G7 officials.  \par \par \uc2 That comment was taken as yen-positive as it appeared to confirm what investors have been expecting, which is that Tanigaki will tell the G7 that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much as in the past as deflationary pressures are now ebbing.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think people are expecting too much fresh news out of the G7. It will probably focus on what they want China to do more than what Japan is doing with its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kwok of Barclays Bank.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040419e04j000as\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
