\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar dips as speculation on US rate hike eases.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 April 2004\par 03:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 19 (Reuters) - The dollar softened slightly against major rivals on Monday, taking a breather from its recent rally as investors had second thoughts about the chances of the United States tightening monetary policy in the near term.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts said that Friday's fairly disappointing batch of U.S. data as well as remarks from Richmond Federal Reserve President Alfred Broaddus that the Fed was still some way from seeing conditions for a rate hike, led dealers to take profits on the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  But market participants weren't expecting the dollar to fall much further.   \par \par \uc2  "The two main pillars for the dollar's direction - the U.S. jobs market and monetary policy - have clearly stepped forward now," said Taisuke Tanaka, chief macro strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston.   \par \par \uc2  "But that doesn't mean the dollar will keep rising, as the Fed is likely to be more cautious about hiking rates than earlier expected, especially as the next jobs report is likely to disappoint the market after the surprisingly robust number in the previous report," he said.   \par \par \uc2  A rise in U.S. interest rates would enhance the allure of U.S. assets for global investors, helping to lift the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Renewed speculation about an interest rate cut in the euro zone was helping the dollar's downside against the euro.   \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the Financial Times newspaper reported that the International Monetary Fund would strengthen its calls for the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to consider cutting interest rates.   \par \par \uc2  At 0053 GMT, the euro was trading at around $1.2004/09 up from $1.1995 in late New York trade on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  Sterling was also up against the dollar, fetching $1.8006/11 compared with $1.7970. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.2921/32 francs down from 1.2950.   \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the dollar was at 107.87/92 yen almost flat from 107.85 and well below a one-month peak of 109.28 yen scaled on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  GREENSPAN, G7   \par \par \uc2  In Japan, Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui and Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi were due to speak at 0600 GMT and 0800 GMT respectively, but the market's focus was on Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's testimony on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  "We don't expect Greenspan to indicate inflation risks or a rate hike in the first half of this year just yet," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.   \par \par \uc2  "But if he emphasizes the Fed's 'patience'...then that could put cold water on growing speculation about a rate hike as early as August," he said.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the dollar to trade in a limited band throughout this week ahead of a meeting of Group of Seven finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors in Washington at the end of next week.   \par \par \uc2  Over the weekend, Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki was quoted by local media as saying that Japan's efforts to hold the yen down were aimed at fighting deflation and would be understood by G7 officials.   \par \par \uc2  That comment was taken as a yen-positive factor as investors had expected Tanigaki to tell G7 nations that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not opt to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as much as had in the past as deflationary pressure was ebbing.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040419e04j00027\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
