\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's Kuroda sees dollar rising,oil price a risk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ritsuko Ando  \par \uc2 432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 April 2004\par 12:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 16 (Reuters) - The dollar will keep rising against the yen in the next few months, making Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  unnecessary, Haruhiko Kuroda, an adviser to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime minister and a former top \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar should keep on recovering through the summer, reflecting improvements in the U.S. economy," Kuroda said in an interview with Reuters.  \par \par \uc2 "Considering such a situation, massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not be needed."  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda also said \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely to be a main topic of discussion at a meeting of Group of Seven (G7) finance ministers and central bankers next week in Washington.  \par \par \uc2 "The February G7 meeting halted the dollar's decline," he said, referring to the group's previous meeting where members agreed that excessive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates was undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 That statement soothed market nerves after a meeting in Dubai last year triggered a surge in the yen as investors guessed - wrongly - that its call for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy would make it hard for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The market is stable now, so there is no particular reason for it to be discussed," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Rather, Kuroda said, a recent rise in oil prices posed a risk to the global economy and could be a topic of discussion at the Washington meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Members of the Organisation of Petroleum Exporting Countries recently agreed to cuts in output, prompting complaints from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow.  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda was in charge of the Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy as vice finance minister for international affairs from 1999 until early 2003.  \par \par \uc2 His successor, Zembei Mizoguchi, has led an aggressive yen-selling campaign over the past year. But the government is widely believed to have scaled back its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-March.  \par \par \uc2 Kuroda also said business conditions were showing more concrete signs of improvement, acknowledging recent data and growing views among economists that Japan's economic recovery is broadening.  \par \par \uc2 He also said Japan's deflationary trend of the past couple of years was finally showing signs of easing, but that more time and continued easing efforts by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  were needed for for it to be stamped out.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's economic recovery is now more apparent. And deflationary conditions are easing," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "But deflation hasn't been overcome at all. The current monetary easing should be continued."  \par \par \uc2 Despite repeated monetary easing measures that have pinned interest rates near zero, Japanese consumer prices have been falling for about five years. (Additional reporting by Mari Terawaki and Yuko Yoshikawa).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040416e04g001ep\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
