\par\par \b \uc2 G7 may mirror Boca  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  language at next meet-analysts.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 569 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 April 2004\par 20:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 14 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are betting the dollar's recovery in recent months will mean that the Group of Seven finance ministers will not need to change its policy on exchange rates at next week's meeting in Washington, D.C.  \par \par \uc2 At the last G7 meeting in Florida in February, finance ministers tried to stem the dollar's slide of the past two years by calling for less volatility in exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 Since that meeting, the dollar has gained about 7 percent against the euro and about 3 percent against the Japanese yen.  \par \par \uc2 In February, the G7 statement warned: "Excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts saw those words as a warning that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be tempted to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro, as the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  did over the past year.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the euro slipped to four-and-a-half month lows below $1.19, 10 cents below its February record highs.  \par \par \uc2 "In retrospect, the G7 statement was far more successful than either the G7 or markets believed at the time and did effectively stop the dollar's decline," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Under current market conditions, with the dollar having stabilized and in fact now rebounding, there is no reason to change the language of the statement," he added.  \par \par \uc2 STRONG YEN STILL A THREAT  \par \par \uc2 A strengthening Japanese economy could push the yen higher and the dollar lower, leading the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to resume its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the yen, which it recently unofficially suspended, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now is that we have done a lot, conditions in place are building for a sustained recovery in Japan and so long as moves are orderly, we will accommodate them," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy with Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading above 108 yen on Wednesday, just above the level hit ahead of the February G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts agree changes to the wording of the upcoming G7 statement on "excess volatility" are unlikely.  \par \par \uc2 Yet some note that while those words may have assisted the dollar's reprieve, economic growth and interest rate differentials are the biggest catalysts driving the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Strong recent U.S. economic data have stoked traders' expectations that the Federal Reserve will likely raise U.S. interest rates later this year for the first time in four years.  \par \par \uc2 "The interest rate story has switched. All of a sudden people are talking about rate hikes in the U.S. and rate cuts in the euro zone," said Lara Rhame, senior economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The United States is still running a trade deficit and current account deficit with the rest of the world that will weigh on the dollar, analysts noted.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar may also suffer from any revaluation of China's yuan. Were China to loosen its yuan-dollar peg, that could boost the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , renewing downward pressure on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Were the G7 to retain its prior statement urging "more flexibility in exchange rates," especially for major countries that lack it, that would amount to a new nudge at China to allow its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to trade more freely, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040414e04e002af\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
