\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL YIELD: Asia Plants Signposts For Global Economy  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,017 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 April 2004\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--After years of easy monetary policy, falling inflation if not outright deflation and massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the direction of the global economy is beginning to turn with Asia rather than the U.S. setting the new signposts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A turnaround in Asia has clear ramifications for the U.S. bond market, which has benefited enormously from Asia's efforts to battle economic malaise over the past few years.  \par \par \uc2 Stunning 7.3% growth in Singapore in the first quarter prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  there Monday to tap the brakes for the first time since 2000 in a pre-emptive strike against bubbling inflation. Surprising many analysts, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which targets the exchange rate as its key policy target, said it would allow a modest and gradual appreciation in the Singapore dollar, abandoning the formerly neutral stance on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's direction.  \par \par \uc2 While Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't necessarily viewed as a bellwether, the fact that it's the first Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to effectively tighten policy grabbed the attention of the financial markets, where belief in the Asian-led recovery is deepening amid continued strength in the economies of China and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 The People's Bank of China said late Sunday that it raised banks' required reserve ratio by half a percentage point to 7.5% in an effort to cool its red-hot economy that posted a 9.1% growth rate in 2003 and 9.9% in the fourth quarter. The increase, which applies to most banks from April 25, expands a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  directive last month that said the higher reserves would be required only by banks with the weakest balance sheets.  \par \par \uc2 Even Japan, a nation dogged by economic malaise for more than a decade, continues to report solid data, leading even the skeptics to admit that the recovery in that nation may be for real and its deflationary woes a soon-to-be distant memory.  \par \par \uc2 On Monday, Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said foreign investors bought a net Y2.69 trillion of Japanese stocks - a monthly record - in March, contributing to a net portfolio inflow overall of Y1.21 trillion in the month.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry also said Japan's current account surplus expanded 46.2% in February from the same month a year earlier, to Y2.157 trillion before seasonal adjustment, marking the eighth straight month of expansion as a booming export sector propels the country's economic revival. A 42.1% rise in the trade surplus to Y1.546 trillion and a 10.5% surge in exports powered February's expansion.  \par \par \uc2 The helped push the Nikkei back above 12,000, a psychological threshold for optimism on the economic outlook. The bellwether index is up 13% year-to-date.  \par \par \uc2 "That's a signal to me that this trend may be for real," said Mark Kiesel, executive vice president and portfolio manager at Newport, Calif-based PIMCO, the world's largest bond fund with $373.7 billion in assets under management.  \par \par \uc2 "Basically, as their economy continues to strengthen, there's going to be less need for the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, which means less dollars earmarked for U.S. Treasurys, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Beginning Of The End? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent years, Japan and other Asian nations have fought to keep theircurrencies from appreciating by buying dollars in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.The Bank of Japan has spent the most, attempting to stop its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengtheningin an effort to stimulate exports. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation also createsinflationary pressures, a welcome development for a nation in the grips ofcontracting consumer prices.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With its coffers full of dollars, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has gone on a shopping spree in the U.S. debt markets, gobbling up massive amounts of Treasury issuance at a time when the U.S. government is running ever widening budget deficits. Some bond analysts have estimated the purchases by the Bank of Japan and other central banks have left the benchmark 10-year Treasury yield around 50 basis points lower than it otherwise would be without such purchasing.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That not only helps the Treasurys market, it keeps U.S. interest rates low for homeowners and corporate borrowers, fueling the overall U.S. economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This mutually beneficial arrangement would be disrupted if the recovery in Japan and the rest of Asia proves sustainable. "The stronger the recovery is, the more tolerance there will be there for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strength," said Alan Ruskin, research director at financial consultancy 4Cast in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fallout on the Treasurys market could be even greater because a potential retreat by Asian central banks would come at a time that government bonds are under more pressure in any case from a sense that the Federal Reserve may raise interest rates sooner than many previously thought.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In fact, the Bank of Japan has been far less active in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets over the past few weeks, allowing the dollar to slide as low as Y103.44 this month from Y112.35 at its recent peak in March. Other Asian central banks, including South Korea's, have also been much less visible in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Michael Woolfolk, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York in New York, expects the Japanese authorities to slow their purchases of dollars further, allowing the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to slide to Y100 by mid-year from around Y105.36 now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Over time, it's likely that the Bank of Japan will be buying less Treasurys," said Kiesel, who estimates yield on the benchmark 10-year note will trend toward 4.80% over the next 12 months as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  buying dries up and positive economic news discourages domestic investors from purchasing government securities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane has covered U.S. credit markets for three and a half years).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Linda Lin in Shanghai and Jamie McGeever in New York contributed to this report.) [ 04-12-04 1645ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 4083  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020040412e04c00020\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
