\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan says Iraq kidnapping won't change  \highlight22\uc2 fx \highlight\uc2  stance.  \b0\par\par\uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 April 2004\par 10:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 9 (Reuters) - Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said on Friday that Japan's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not change in the wake of the kidnapping of three Japanese civilians in Iraq and the government would act to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market stability.  \par \par \uc2 "We will take appropriate steps if there are movements that deviate from fundamentals and are speculative. The situation in Iraq won't change that stance," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki told reporters after a cabinet meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The yen weakened slightly on Friday as traders worried that the kidnapping could damage Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's administration.  \par \par \uc2 A hitherto unknown Iraqi group, Saraya al-Mujahideen (Mujahideen Brigades), released a video of three Japanese hostages on Thursday and said it would "burn them alive" if Japanese troops did not leave Iraq in three days.  \par \par \uc2 At 0817 GMT the dollar was at 106.45 yen after rising to a high of around 106.75 - up about half a yen from late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki said the impact of the kidnappings on Japanese financial markets so far seemed relatively minor.  \par \par \uc2 He said the Finance Ministry was not considering any immediate steps regarding the hostage situation.  \par \par \uc2 Zembei Mizoguchi, vice minister of international affairs and the main architect of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, also said that Japan's stance was unchanged. He declined to comment on what effect the kidnapping might have on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to smooth volatility when it thought necessary, he added.  \par \par \uc2 On financing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tanigaki said the Finance Ministry and Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had extended an agreement under which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could temporarily buy government-held foreign bonds under repurchase agreements to provide funds.  \par \par \uc2 As a stop-gap measure for the government to bolster its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  war chest, the BOJ had agreed in December to temporarily buy up to about 10 trillion yen ($94 billion) of foreign bonds, namely U.S. Treasuries, as a way for the government to raise funds without issuing more debt.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Toshihiko Fukui told a separate news conference that the extension would run for three months. The original agreement ended at the end of the fiscal year in March.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ held some six trillion yen of foreign securities under the agreement as of March 31, according to BOJ data.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki emphasised that the government had no problem with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds at the moment, noting that the government had raised the ceiling on its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  special account to 140 trillion yen for the fiscal year that started this month.  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has sold some 35 trillion yen in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since January 2003 to curb the rise in the yen, which hit a four-year high of 103.40 yen per dollar last week. ($1=106.39 Yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040409e0490006c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
