\par\par \b \uc2 Weak yen gives Japanese autos big US advantage-GM.   \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 20:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, April 8 (Reuters) - Japan's efforts to depress the value of the yen gives Japanese automakers a $12,000 advantage in the price of luxury cars shipped to the United States, the head of General Motors Corp. (GM.N) said on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 A fair value for the yen, which is trading at about 106 to the U.S. dollar, would be about 90 yen to the dollar, GM Chief Executive Rick Wagoner told analysts, investors and reporters at a Morgan Stanley conference. But he said the Japanese government has repeatedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets to depress the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,   \par \par \uc2 "This is a bad deal. It's obviously a bad deal for us competitively," Wagoner said.   \par \par \uc2 According to Wagoner, if the yen were trading at 90, the price of a Japanese luxury car in the United States, when translated to yen, would be about $12,000 higher. For small cars Japanese automakers ship to the United States, the price difference would be about $2,500, Wagoner said.   \par \par \uc2 While the yen has strengthened over the past year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep it from strengthening further.   \par \par \uc2 Although companies can hedge against short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations, a stronger yen over the long term would hurt earnings from Japanese exports, including cars shipped to the United States, unless Japanese companies raised prices to compensate. But in the current U.S. price war and heavy sales incentives, Japanese automakers would find it difficult to raise prices.   \par \par \uc2 GM officials have repeatedly called on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, and in the past have asked the U.S. government to apply pressure on the Japanese.   \par \par \uc2 Japanese automakers have responded that many of them build a number of vehicles in North America, and that their market share gains have been because of attractive new cars and SUVs that consumers want, and not because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040408e048002pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
