\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Moody's Japan rating upgrade puzzles analysts.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tamawa Kadoya and Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 11:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 8 (Reuters) - A raising by Moody's Investors Service of its rating on Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt has puzzled some analysts as they view the reasons behind the move as contradictory.  \par \par \uc2 Markets generally viewed the upgrade positively, but analysts were perplexed when the rating firm attributed it to Japan's ballooning foreign reserves. Analysts say the reserves increase is hurting Japan's fiscal position - which is what Moody's seems most concerned about when assessing Japan's sovereign rating.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's left Japan's more important A2 sovereign rating unchanged because of the country's deteriorating domestic debt position.  \par \par \uc2 "The upgrade was based on a sharp rise in Japan's foreign reserves, but it's a strange argument because behind the giant reserves lies a bulging domestic debt," said Izuru Kato, chief economist at Totan Research Co.  \par \par \uc2 Japan, fearing the yen's strength could dent the country's exports, has sold some 35 trillion yen ($332.6 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets since January 2003, boosting the country's foreign reserves to a record $827 billion in March.  \par \par \uc2 But the twist is that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is basically funded by government debt through the issuance of short-term paper called financing bills (FBs). Analysts say this is straining Japan's already snowballing public debt.  \par \par \uc2 Official figures show that the outstanding amount of FBs issued by the government for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was 69 trillion yen as of the end of December.  \par \par \uc2 "The reasons for the two decisions on foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt and domestic debt seem contradictory," Kato said.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's on Wednesday upgraded its rating on Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  government bonds to Aaa from Aa1. It said the move reflected Japan's ballooning foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, which suggested Japan's strong ability to pay back foreign debts.  \par \par \uc2 But the decision, which affected only about $30 billion of rated securities, had little lasting impact on the markets as Japan, unlike many countries, has little foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt.  \par \par \uc2 GAP BETWEEN TWO "SOVEREIGNS"  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also noted that in contrast to rival agencies Standard & Poor's and Fitch, Moody's ratings of Japan's two sovereigns have now widened to five notches.  \par \par \uc2 "If they are worried about possible default risk, then they have to explain why real interest rates remain low in Japan because that reflects investor confidence in the government's solvency," a Japanese monetary source said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is an unusual case, having a domestic rating lower than its foreign rating, as many countries have a current account deficit and small foreign reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Chotaro Morita, senior economist at Deutsche Securities, said: "The wide gap between foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt and domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt ratings is already a contradiction."  \par \par \uc2 In an extreme case, domestic debt could be paid back if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  printed more money, while foreign debt could not once reserves dried up, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's and other rating firms repeatedly cut Japan's sovereign ratings in recent years, citing its deteriorating fiscal position as a decade of pump-priming left it with one of the worst fiscal deficits among leading industrial nations.  \par \par \uc2 "In contrast to overall strength and stability in Japan's external position, the government's domestic debt position has continued to deteriorate in accordance with Moody's expectation," the agency said in a press release on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Previously, the Japanese government had openly shown frustration towards the sovereign downgrades, firing off a letter to Moody's saying the agency's methodologies were unclear and that it had underestimated Japan's huge domestic savings. ($1=105.23 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040408e04800166\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
