\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Apr 8  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 11:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The Bank of Korea left its key call rate unchanged Thursday as widely expected, but raised its economic outlook, predicting gross domestic product could expand as fast as 6% on unusually strong export growth. (UPDATE will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: A surprisingly large rebound in employment took Australia's jobless rate to a 14-year low in March, renewing speculation the Reserve Bank will raise interest rates this year. ("=ASIA ECONOMY WATCH: Australia Jobless Hits 14-Year Low," ran at 0717 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Finance Minister said Thursday that Tokyo's massive campaign to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was effective in protecting the economy from excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility as it struggles to overcome deflation. ("=UPDATE: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Tanigaki: \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Curbed Volatility," ran at 0644 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's prime minister said Thursday he won't run for a third term when his current term expires in September 2006, indicating that after another two years he will have had enough. ("=UPDATE: Japan PM Koizumi Says Won't Seek A Third Term," ran at 0805 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: When the New Zealand dollar surged to seven-year highs against the U.S. dollar earlier this year, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could only stand by and idly watch. But after the Reserve Bank of New Zealand was granted the capacity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the local dollar reached "exceptional and unjustifiable" levels from Parliament Tuesday, the market is expecting the RBNZ to soon take a more active role. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Market Awaiting NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 0545 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The diverging paths of New Zealand's meager business confidence and surging capacity utilization are likely to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to stay the course of higher interest rates. ("=UPDATE: Confidence Slump Puts NZ Ctrl Bank In Tight Spot," ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The average delinquency ratio of South Korea's eight credit card companies climbed to 15.4% as of end February, a 0.2% increase from January, said the Financial Supervisory Service Thursday. ("S Korea Card Cos Feb Avg Delinquency Rate 15.4% Vs 15.2%," ran at 0305 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's exports of information-technology items reached a new monthly high in March thanks to robust overseas demand for semiconductors and rising prices of memory chips. ("S Korea March IT Exports Hit High Of $6.3B, +46.2% On Yr," ran at 0225 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: A senior World Bank official expressed confidence Thursday that China will be able to avoid or contain the damage of a potential meltdown in its state banking sector and current economic overheating. (INTERVIEW will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: Indian exports are expected to lag far behind an otherwise briskly growing economy, with revenue growth likely to slow for the second year in a row due to the strong rupee. ("=Indian Exports To Slow For 2nd Yr In A Row On Strong INR," ran at 0345 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: In its manifesto for the coming election, India's ruling coalition, National Democratic Alliance, or NDA, is targeting economic growth of 8%-10% over the next five years. ("=INDIA VOTE: Coalition Aims 8%-10% GDP Growth Next 5 Yrs," ran at 0816 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China has begun levying antidumping charges on chloroform imported from the U.S., the E.U., South Korea and India after a preliminary investigation by the Ministry of Commerce into complaints of illegal trading. ("China Levies Anti-Dumping Charges On Imported Chloroform," ran at 0623 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's industrial output rose a preliminary 11.1% on year in February, fueled by an 11.9% expansion in manufacturing, the Department of Statistics said Thursday. ("DATA SNAP: Malaysia Feb Indus Output Rises 11.1% On Yr," 0401 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's gross domestic product likely grew 5.8% in the first quarter compared with the same period last year, boosted by a strong performance in manufacturing as the economy continues on the recovery track, according to economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires. In the previous quarter, GDP rose 4.9% from the previous year. GDP data will be released at 0000 GMT on Monday. ("PREVIEW: Singapore 1Q GDP Likely +5.8% On Yr, Led By Mfg," ran at 0330 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar rose against the yen in Asian trade Thursday as Japanese investors continued to buy overseas assets at the start of their fiscal year. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Up As Japan Investors Buy Foreign Assets," ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Fair Trade Commission Thursday raided three offices of Intel Japan on suspicion that the company pressured Japan's PC manufacturers not to use its competitors' products., said an FTC official and Intel Corp.. (UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: An international consortium led by Australian Gas Light Thursday completed the long-delayed acquisition of Loy Yang A power station, continuing the exit of U.S. energy companies from Australia. AGL said the Great Energy Alliance Corp., which includes Tokyo Electric Power Co., paid A$3.48 billion for Loy Yang, one of Australia's biggest privately owned power stations. ("=UPDATE: Australia's AGL Completes Loy Yang Power Deal," ran at 0829 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shanghai Bailian (Group) Co. plans to merge its two listed retail companies, which will be a major step in restructuring China's biggest retail chain group. Both companies are units of Bailian Group, a massive retail group formed last year by the Shanghai government through the merger of several retail groups. ("Shanghai No.1 Dept Store To Absorb Shanghai Hua Lian," ran at 0047 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Ito-Yokado said Thursday its group net profit for the year to Feb. 29 climbed 15%, lifted by record earnings at its Seven-Eleven Japan unit. The Janese supermarket store operator posted a group net profit of Y53.63 billion for the fiscal year, up from Y46.62 billion a year earlier, and forecast continued improvement in earnings this fiscal year through February 2005, buoyed by efforts to improve customer services and cut costs. ("=UPDATE: Strong Group Units Push Ito-Yokado Profit Up 15%," ran at 0817 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: In a move that may prompt further realignment among Japanese sugar refiners, Shin Mitsui Sugar and two other Mitsui & Co. group sugar producers said Thursday they will merge in April 2005. ("=UPDATE: Shin Mitsui Sugar, Two Other Mitsui Cos To Merge," ran at 0209 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Kookmin Bank, South Korea's largest lender, is considering a strategic alliance with a foreign financial company to boost competitiveness of its credit card business and to offer more card services overseas, said a senior bank executive Thursday. ("=INTERVIEW: Kookmin Bank Mulls Foreign Tie-Up In Card Ops," ran at 0724 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Telecommunications equipment maker Lucent Technologies of the U.S. Thursday said it is looking for a successor to the fired president of its China \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The New Jersey-based company removed four executives from its China \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid a U.S. investigation into bribery allegations. ("Lucent In Search For New Head Of China \b \highlight22\uc2 Operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ran at 0634 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toshiba said Thursday it will form a second joint venture to make air conditioner compressors with Chinese home appliance giant GD Midea Holding this year. ("Toshiba Unit, China's GD Midea To Form 2nd Compressor JV," ran at 0141 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Solomon Systech shares ended 6% higher on their first day of trading Thursday at HK$1.86, from the HK$1.75 offer price, bucking the recent trend of weak IPO debuts. The Hong Kong-based chip designer raised HK$1.06 billion ahead of its mainboard listing. (UPDATE will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BRISBANE: Clearing a difficult hurdle that opens the way for a higher bid, wagering group UNiTAB Ltd. is now scheduling a meeting with target company TAB Ltd. to cross the finish line without animosity. ("=INTERVIEW: UNiTAB Clears Hurdle, Mulls Higher TAB Bid," ran at 0539 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: A former National Australia Bank employee and his brother Thursday pleaded guilty to laundering more than A$1.3 million. The two men were charged with structuring transactions to avoid reporting requirements under Australia's Financial Transactions Reporting laws. ("Former National Australia Staffer,Brother Face Sentencing," ran at 0740 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's vast rural population represents an untapped potential for satellite deployment that could eventually link the hundreds of millions of people living in the countryside to television and the Internet, said a chief executive at SES Global SA, the world's biggest satellite operator. (INTERVIEW will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The New Zealand arm of Rupert Murdoch's media empire, Independent Newspapers Ltd., said Wednesday it has won High Court approval for its plans to return NZ$340 million to shareholders. ("NZ's INL Wins High Court OK For NZ$340M Capital Repayment" ran at 2001 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Strong steel prices likely provided a big boost to Posco's earnings in the first quarter. Net profit at the world's fourth-largest steel maker is expected to have surged 66% on year to 779.4 billion won in the first three months of this year, analysts said. ("=Strong Steel Prices Seen Boosting Posco's 1Q Profits," ran at 0742 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's power utilities will accelerate cost-cutting as part of preparations to lower their electricity prices and survive tough price competition in the domestic retail market. ("=JAPAN POWER:Utilities,Facing Tough Competition,Cut Costs," ran at 0759 GMT) [ 08-04-04 0903GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: The price of steel and other industrial materials in China continued to rise in March, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said, among the latest indications that the country's voracious appetite for commodities shows little sign of waning. ("China Mar Steel Prices Up 2.6% Vs Feb, +35.9% On Yr -PBOC," ran at 0336 GMT) [ 08-04-04 0903GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040408e048000hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
