\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2 's Tanigaki:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Curbed Volatility  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 778 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 08:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister said Thursday that Tokyo's massive campaign to weaken the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was effective in protecting the economy from excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility as it struggles to overcome deflation.  \par \par \uc2 While a "considerable amount" of money has been spent on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sadakazu Tanigaki said that such action was necessary to keep excessive market fluctuations in check.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  instability is harmful when the economy is searching for an exit to deflation," he said. "There was a meaning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it eliminated the instability while the economy searched for a deflation exit."  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki's comments - made while answering questions at the Foreign Correspondents' Club of Japan - suggest the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 15-month, all-out mission to weaken the yen is effectively over.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the massive amount of funds involved in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market there is a limit to what \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can do against the larger market trend," Tanigaki said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government, fearing yen strength could undermine the export-led recovery, until mid-March conducted aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from rising sharply.  \par \par \uc2 Last year, the MOF ordered the Bank of Japan to sell an unprecedented Y20 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, while another Y15 trillion was spent in the first three months of this year.  \par \par \uc2 But recently, authorities seem to be relaxing their grip on exchange rates as Japan's economy shows increasing signs of what would be the first sustainable economic recovery in more than a decade.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market players say the government has thus become more tolerant of yen strength - although it's unlikely to have abandoned \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  altogether.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Tanigaki stressed that going forward, the MOF "will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary," as the government still believes that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should move stably in reflection of economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 "If movements go beyond fundamentals, we will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Policy Unrelated To U.S. Polls \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki said, however, there is no specific range that would inherently reflect economic fundamentals.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't have in mind any image of what would represent a limit when it comes to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels," Tanigaki said, when asked if there was a limit to how much dollar weakness Tokyo, or Washington, was willing to tolerate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki added there has been no discussion of tolerance limits or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  levels between himself and his counterparts in the U.S. or Europe.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's finance minister said he has also never discussed the issue of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in relation to the U.S. presidential contest in November. He had been asked if Tokyo was holding back on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - which would bring about a stronger dollar - due to its possible impact on the election.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I do exchange views with my counterparts on various occasions, but I have never talked to U.S. Treasury Secretary (John) Snow on the presidential election in relation to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  isn't decided by U.S. elections," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par       Not Keen On Japan Upping Gold Reserves \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the issue of Japan's massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, which rose to a record $826.58 billion at the end of March, Tanigaki acknowledged that a large portion of those reserves were held in dollar-denominated assets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In a sense, there may be a need to diversify our reserves," Tanigaki said. But he was quick to rule out that diversification would imply an increase in Japan's gold holdings.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said he was aware that some Japanese lawmakers have been calling for the government to boost its holdings of gold. "But I don't necessarily agree with the view that Japan should possess more gold."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 His comments were made in response to a question on whether Japan would consider boosting its gold reserves as some Diet members have recently suggested.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki also said the government would continue efforts to pare its fiscal deficit, which is currently in what he called "a worst-case scenario."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's deficit is the worst in the industrialized world, around 150% of gross domestic product, according to some estimates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tanigaki said, "for the time being we may see a continuation of a situation in which we will have to issue massive amounts of government bonds."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, he added the government was making progress toward achieving its goal of balancing its budget by the 2010s as it continues to cut down on wasteful spending and to pursue fiscal reform.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson [ 08-04-04 0644GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040408e048000dp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
