\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Up As Japan Investors Buy Foreign Assets  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takashi Nakamichi  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,109 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 April 2004\par 07:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar rose against the yen in Asian trade Thursday as Japanese investors continued to buy overseas assets at the start of their fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 In a thin market ahead of the long Easter weekend, the greenback began climbing against the yen early in the session following reports that explosions were heard near a camp for Japanese troops in southern Iraq, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Some Japanese exporters sold into the rally midmorning and pulled the dollar slightly lower. But helped by Japanese institutional investors seeking U.S. financial assets following the April 1 start of the fiscal year, the U.S. unit soon recovered ground and extended gains, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also rose against the yen on buying by Japanese investors looking for euro-denominated bonds, giving a further cross-trading boost to its U.S. rival, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar may stay supported by Japanese investors in coming days, but its short-term outlook against the yen remains grim amid growing violence in Iraq, said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "The Iraq situation has gotten stuck more deeply in the mud after the U.S. bombed a mosque overnight," Sasaki said. "That strengthens concern the U.S. budget deficits will keep expanding, and that's surely a bearish factor for the dollar."  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. bombed a mosque compound in Fallujah Wednesday, and witnesses said as many as 40 people were killed, prompting investors to sell dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Against the dollar Thursday, the euro built on its overnight gains partly because some players shifted funds into Europe amid terrorism concerns, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y105.71, above Y105.25 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y105.55 late Wednesday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2209, higher than $1.2175 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.2086 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y129.13, up from Y128.15 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y127.57 in Tokyo late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.8458, up from $1.8400 in New York and higher than $1.8399 in Tokyo Wednesday. [ 08-04-04 0540GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Adding to the dollar-bearish mood were comments by Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki which some market players said suggested Tokyo has halted its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign because of weakening deflation.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  instability is harmful when the economy is searching for an exit to deflation," Tanigaki told the Foreign Correspondents Club of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "In that sense, there was a meaning to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it eliminated the instability as the economy searched for a deflation exit."  \par \par \uc2 Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas in Tokyo, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may think it has done all it could to help soothe deflation and may have decided to stop selling the yen amid growing signs of easing price falls.  \par \par \uc2 "The work left is to pull the consumer price index out of the minus column, but the MOF perhaps thinks only the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quantitative easing policy or fiscal policy - not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - can do the remaining job," Fink said.  \par \par \uc2 Without \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar may fall as low as Y100 this month, given the growing appetite of overseas investors for Japanese stocks, continued turmoil in Iraq and the widening U.S. budget and current account deficits, she said.  \par \par \uc2 But the yen isn't without downside risk.  \par \par \uc2 Yen sales sparked by the explosions near the Japanese camp in Iraq showed the market hasn't factored in the risk to the Koizumi administration of troop dispatch, said Masamichi Koike, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  spot trading group at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Junichiro "Koizumi is even more vulnerable than Bush," he said. "Casualties in Iraq would shake Koizumi's government, and that would put the economic recovery at risk."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The chart of late Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates that accompanied Asia \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been replaced with an enhanced \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Subscribers can find this table:  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX [ 08-04-04 0702GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding to the dollar-bearish mood were comments by Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki which some market players said suggested Tokyo has halted its \f28 \b \