\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED - CORRECTED-Dollar up, yen fails to keep gains made on Moody's.  \b0\par\par\uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2004\par 09:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     In TOKYO story headlined "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 -Dollar up, yen fails to keep\par  gains made on Moody's", please read in first paragraph\par  ....four-month high.... instead of ....four-year high...\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (correcting time period).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 A corrected story follows.  \par \par \uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 7 (Reuters) - The dollar rose on Wednesday, nearing a four-month high against the euro, on the perception that the U.S. Federal Reserve could raise interest rates sooner rather than later following recent healthy U.S. jobs data.  \par \par \uc2 In face of the dollar's overall strength, the yen failed to keep gains after credit rating firm Moody's upgraded its rating on Japan's foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt to the highest level.  \par \par \uc2 At 0550 GMT, the dollar was around 105.95 yen up about 0.2 percent from late U.S. levels but still down from a two-week high of 107.25 hit on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar briefly fell to around 105.35 yen from above 106 yen in a knee-jerk reaction to Moody's announcement that it had raised its rating to Aaa from Aa1, which came during lunch-time in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounced back as traders concluded the ratings upgrade was merely anecdotal evidence of an improving economy in Japan.  \par \par \uc2 "The move is no more than an acknowledgement of improving Japanese economic fundamentals. I don't think it will have any influence in the market in the long term," said Junya Tanase, strategist at JPMorgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's cited Japan's ballooning foreign reserves as a reason behind its upgrade. The reserves hit a record high $826.577 billion at the end of March due to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 Moody's lifted only its foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating, which was of little importance to many traders since Japan does not have foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  denominated debt to speak of, and the rating will affect a mere $30 billion in government-guaranteed bonds.  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR KEEPS MOMENTUM  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar had slipped after hitting a four-month peak against the euro in choppy trade on Tuesday, it kept its strength on last week's upbeat reading for U.S. payrolls.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. payroll data had such a big impact," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 The euro eased about 0.6 percent to $1.2060 from around $1.2135 in late U.S. trade, edging towards the four-month trough of $1.1980.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's latest rally was sparked after the payroll figures showed more jobs were created in March than many in the market had expected.  \par \par \uc2 A sustained ithe allure of dollar-denominated assets for foreign investors.  \par \par \uc2 Hopes for higher U.S. rates were not dented by comments from St. Louis Federal Reserve Bank President William Poole, who said on Tuesday that job numbers beyond the March report were needed to confirm a positive trend in hiring.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations for rising U.S. rates contrast with lingering views in the market that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may cut rates in the near future to shore up the euro zone economy.  \par \par \uc2 But some market players worried about the latest uprisings in Iraq against U.S.-led troops.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm not entirely sure if one should buy the dollar with Iraq in such a mess," said Fumihiko Kawano, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Up to 12 U.S. Marines were killed on Tuesday in an attack by still unidentified assailants, while the battle between followers of a radical Islamic cleric and U.S.-led troops claimed more than 130 lives in three days. ($1=105.75 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040407e047000v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
