\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Apr 7  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 April 2004\par 11:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its official cash rate steady at 5.25% for the fourth straight month in April. The widely expected decision by the bank's nine-member board is in line with the emergence of a slowdown in the housing sector and signs of cooling in retail sales and credit growth, economists say. ("Australia's Reserve Bank Leaves Rates Unchanged In April," ran at 2330 GMT; "=INTERVIEW: RBA's McKibbin Ponders Fed Inaction," ran at 0610 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Moody's Investors Service Wednesday upgraded its ratings for foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euroyen debt issued by Japan's government and public-sector companies, but left unchanged Japan's domestic debt rating. While the upgrade of the foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  debt rating to Aaa, Moody's highest rating level, from Aa1 is a further sign of an improving outlook for Japan, the credit rating firm reiterated that the country is yet to conquer deflation and has a worsening fiscal problem. ("=UPDATE: Moody's Ups Rtg On Japan Govt Foreign-Curr Debt," ran at 0415 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Temasek Holdings has raised S$792 million by selling zero-coupon notes convertible into Keppel Corp. shares, a move that will allow the state investment agency to expand a war chest for regional acquisitions. It is the second - and won't be the last - time that Temasek has cut its stake in a government-linked company and at the same time raised cash to buy overseas assets. ("2nd UPDATE: Temasek Raises S$792M Via Keppel Notes," ran at 0643 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The news out of South Korea keeps getting better, but economic data reported Wednesday failed to convince government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials to cast aside their caution. Both money supply and services output for February swung to growth, while foreign companies pledged investments that were way higher than the same quarter in 2003. However, officials from both the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't confirm a local newspaper report Wednesday that gross domestic product hit 6% on year in the first quarter as exports surged over 30% in the period. ("=UPDATE: S Korea's 1Q GDP Likely Beat Forecast On Exports," will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Foreign direct investment in South Korea nearly tripled in the first quarter after more than a year's decline, thanks largely to Citigroup's purchase of KorAm Bank. But it's questionable whether Korea, competing with attractive investment destinations like China, can continue to attract such lucrative deals. ("=UPDATE: S Korea 1Q FDI Soars On Citibank; Trend In Doubt," ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose $49.72 billion in March from a month earlier to a record $826.58 billion, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday, reflecting the government's continued yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out during the month. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  March Foreign Reserves $826.58B; Up $49.72B On Mo," ran at 2357 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The Reserve Bank of New Zealand would take a medium- to long-term view when it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, potentially holding positions for five years or so, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Alan Bollard said Wednesday. Bollard was speaking on National Radio after Tuesday's vote in New Zealand's Parliament ratifying a new funding agreement between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor. The agreement, which provides balance sheet firepower for the RBNZ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, was passed by 84 votes to 35. ("Bollard Says RBNZ Could Hold \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Position 5 Yrs Or More," ran at 0014 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China has approved amendments to its Foreign Trade Law, effective July 1, which will help the country develop trade with foreign partners and adhere to World Trade Organization rules, the Ministry of Commerce said Wednesday. ("China Amends Foreign Trade Law, Effective July 1," ran at 0111 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's exports and imports surged to record high levels in March, the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Wednesday, noting that demand for Taiwan-made goods from Hong Kong, China, Southeast Asia and Europe were particularly strong. ("DATA SNAP: Taiwan Posts Record Exports, Imports In March," ran at 0821 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: The possibility of a U.K. interest rate hike this week while the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  considers easing policy in coming months pushed the euro to a 14-month low against the pound Wednesday, and further losses could be in the cards. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Euro-Sterling At 14-Mo Low;All Eyes On Old Lady," ran at 0556 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Asean finance ministers said Wednesday that a gradual correction of fiscal imbalances in major industrialized countries is necessary to avoid putting undue stress on global and regional economic prospects. ("Asean: Gradual Adjustment Of Fiscal Imbalances Necessary," first take ran at 0731 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The E.U.'s commissioner for research, Philippe Busquin, Wednesday said the adoption of common standards is important for the development of science and technology worldwide. The commissioner's remarks, on the sidelines of a workshop on E.U.-China cooperation in the space industry, appear to be a slap in the face for China, which is pushing for the adoption of homegrown technology standards as a way to protect domestic interests. ("=INTERVIEW: EU Commissioner Backs Global Tech Standards," ran at 0743 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and banking regulator are seeking public opinion on draft rules for loan use, which would ban using loans to invest in stocks, futures, and derivative products. ("China To Tighten Rules On Loan Use, Banning Sec. Invest," ran at 0010 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HANOI: Pledged foreign direct investment into Vietnam reached $420 million in the first quarter, up 11% from the same period last year, an official at the Ministry of Planning and Investment said Wednesday. In terms of the number of projects, however, Vietnam approved 120 foreign-invested projects in January to March, down 37.2% on year. ("Vietnam Jan-Mar Pledged FDI +11% On Yr At $420M -Official," ran at 0442 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The yen surged against the dollar and the euro in Asia Wednesday after Moody's Investors Service upgraded Japan's foreign debt rating, sending players rushing to buy the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Yen Surges As Moody's Ups Japan Foreign Debt," ran at 0524 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's Fujitsu said Wednesday it has filed several lawsuits against Samsung Corp. group companies over what Fujitsu says is infringement of patents on its plasma display panel technologies by the South Korean high-tech giant. The lawsuits come at a time when an increasing number of Japanese firms are taking the offensive in protecting their intellectual property rights on technologies that could earn them large profits from patents, as well as from sales of the hot gadgets these technologies help produce. ("UPDATE: Fujitsu Sues Samsung Group Over Plasma Panel Tech Patents," will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Telstra Corp. said Wednesday it will pay A$333 million for Kaz Group, strengthening its position in the Australian IT services market that is now dominated by U.S. computer giants. Currently ranked seventh in the A$12 billion-plus market, Australia's biggest telecom company said its combination with eighth-ranked Kaz puts it on track to reach the No. 3 position, behind IBM and Hewlett-Packard, within four years. ("Australia's Telstra To Buy Kaz Group For A$333M," first take ran at 2254 GMT;"REPEAT: Telstra Challenges On US Rivals With Kaz Deal," ran at 0559 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nippon Steel said Wednesday it has reached a basic accord with Rio Tinto on a new tie-up centering on purchases of iron ore and coal in Australia. For Japan's largest steelmaker, the deal will ensure a stable source of raw materials for steelmaking as robust steel demand in China keeps its global supply lines extremely tight. ("=UPDATE: Nippon Steel, Rio Tinto In Australian Mine Deal," ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Morgan Stanley Capital International and Standard & Poor's on Wednesday suggested that News Corp. may be able to retain its inclusion in regional and country equity market indexes, despite its planned shift in primary listing to the U.S. from Australia. ("=UPDATE: News Corp May Stay In MSCI, S&P/ASX Indexes," ran at 0451 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japanese supermarket store operator Aeon Co. Wednesday reported record high revenue and profits for the fiscal year ended Feb. 20, supported by the strong performance of its group companies. ("=UPDATE: Aeon Last FY Profits Up, Expects Gain On Mycal," ran at 0836 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The capital adequacy ratio of Bank of Yokohama may rise as holders of the lender's Y60 billion convertible bonds are likely to convert the bonds and pocket profits by selling the common stock they get in return. ("=UPDATE: Shr Rise Helps Lift Cap Adequacy For Yokohama Bk," ran at 0755 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The road to recovery for Mitsubishi Motors looks long and bumpy even if its major shareholders pump money and management expertise into the struggling Japanese auto maker, analysts say. Mitsubishi Motors, DaimlerChrysler's 37%-held affiliate, is drawing up a drastic restructuring plan with its parent and other large shareholders, and details are due April 30. ("=Mitsubishi Motors' Road To Recovery Long; Asia Focus Key," ran at 0848 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Airlines has no plans to start a low-cost airline in Australia and will remain focused on cutting costs by as much as S$1.6 billion annually, Chief Executive Chew Choon Seng said Wednesday. The comments come a day after Australian rival Qantas Airways unveiled plans for a Singapore-based low-cost airline that will compete with three other budget airlines, including SIA-affiliate Tiger Airways that will begin \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  late this year. ("Singapore Air: No Plans For Australian Budget Airline," first take ran at 0845 GMT) [ 07-04-04 0908GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: An European consortium led by France's construction group Vinci SA, Renault, UBS AG and others has proposed to buy Dongah Construction & Industrial Co., which went bankrupt in 2001, Dongah creditor Korea Exchange Bank said Wednesday. ("S. Korea Dongah Construction Targeted By Euro Consortium," ran at 0100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: The Thai Cabinet approved new privatization regulations Wednesday proposed by the Finance Ministry in a bid to fend off opposition to the sale of stakes in state enterprises by their employees. The regulations involve stock benefits for state enterprise employees and limits on foreign holdings in state agencies post-IPO. ("Thai Cabinet OKs New Privatization Rules To Ease Protests," ran at 0741 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Life Insurance said it will face no fines after a National Audit Office review uncovered illegal practices by its parent company prior to China Life's 2003 share listing. The parent, China Life Insurance (Group) Co., now faces CNY67.49 million in unpaid taxes and fines resulting from the audit office report. Although China Life faces no fines at home, it is confronted by a class-action lawsuit in the U.S. alleging "massive financial fraud." During a restructuring ahead of its US$3.46 billion IPO in December - the world's biggest share offering last year - less attractive assets at China Life's predecessor were transferred to its parent company and only its healthier assets retained. ("China Life Says Faces No Fines After Audit Office Report," ran at 2355 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Two debut stocks soared on the Bombay Stock Exchange Wednesday, defying the broader trend and indicating continued appetite for quality issues. The strong showing by Biocon Ltd. and Power Trading Corp. also showed the market's interest in new sectors, said analysts. ("UPDATE: India Biocon, Pwr Trading Surge Defy Mkt, Surge On Debut," will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: ICBC Asia said its controlling shareholder, the Industrial & Commercial Bank of China, increased its stake in the Hong Kong-listed bank to 63.2% from 53.2% by converting preference shares. ("China's ICBC Increases Stake In HK's ICBC Asia To 63.2%," ran at 2329 GMT; note "CORRECT: China ICBC Ups Stake In HK's ICBC Asia To 63.2%," ran at 0401 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Red-chip conglomerate China Resources Enterprise said Wednesday a loss in its retail \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  meant net profit rose just 4.3% in 2003 despite a surge in overall revenue. ("China Resources Enterp FY Net HK$1.46 Bln Vs HK$1.40 Bln," ran at 0829 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong-based chip designer Solomon Systech said Wednesday the retail tranche of its initial public offering was 1.65 times subscribed. The company raised HK$1.06 billion ahead of its listing on the Hong Kong stock exchange's main board Thursday. ("HK Solomon Systech IPO Retail Tranche 1.65 Times Covered," ran at 0209 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Technologies Engineering will release first-quarter earnings today. (results expected around 0900 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore Press Holdings said it is open to merging its loss making television \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  with its bitter rival, government-owned MediaCorp of Singapore. The comment came months after the city state's biggest publisher pledged it wouldn't abandon its broadcast business where it runs two free-to-air television channels.("Singapore Press Hldgs Says Open To TV Merger With Rival," ran at 0838 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Shares in main board-listed banking group Affin Holdings are testing important trendline support Wednesday, and any break would signal further losses of at least several percent. ("=TECHNICAL TALK: Affin Holdings Testing Trendline Support," ran at 0324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA ENERGY WATCH: China's runaway economic growth will remain heavily reliant on coal for many years to come, due to a shortage of domestic oil supplies and the inability of power generators to make a major switch to natural gas. ("=CHINA ENERGY WATCH:Coal In Spotlight As Power Needs Grow," ran at 0854 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China will launch an exchange for steel and steel products in Shanghai Friday, amid growing demand that has led to soaring prices in the past year. ("China To Launch Shanghai Steel Electronic Exchange Apr 9," ran at 0321 GMT; UPDATE will run around 1030 GMT; see also "China's Steel Sector More Than Doubles Profit In Jan-Feb," which ran at 0315 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Australia's federal government has taken steps to encourage more oil and gas exploration in high-risk, frontier areas, but more needs to be done, says the country's leading upstream lobby group. ("=INTERVIEW: Australia Lobby Urges Action On Energy Crunch," ran at 0551 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Prices for New Zealand's main commodity exports rose to their highest level in 17 years, driven mainly by a continuing recovery in dairy prices, the Australia and New Zealand Banking Group said Wednesday. ("Spot Prices For NZ Commodities Rise To 17-Year High -ANZ," ran at 1940 GMT) [ 07-04-04 0908GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040407e047000cs\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
