\par\par \b \uc2 Japan govt source says strong yen has benefits.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Takeshi Yoshiike  \par \uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2004\par 06:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 5 (Reuters) - There is a growing view among Japanese officials that the merits of a stronger yen now outweigh the disadvantages, a government source said on Monday, adding to speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may scale back its campaign of massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 The source, who has regular contact with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which runs \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, said it was his impression that many in the government recognised that a stronger yen helped companies deal with high oil and raw material prices.  \par \par \uc2 He said that as a result, the government was likely to halt its campaign of massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "I think they might stop," the source said, adding that this was his personal opinion. "They will wait and see what happens at least until the U.S. presidential election."  \par \par \uc2 The election will be held in November.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold more than 15 trillion yen ($144 billion) in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the first three months of this year, following on from a record 20 trillion yen in all of 2003.  \par \par \uc2 But the policy, aimed at smoothing the yen's rise to protect exporter profits and keep Japan's economic recovery on track, has come under fire from Japan's trading partners, including the United States, as well as from opposition politicians concerned about the huge amounts of money being spent.  \par \par \uc2 Also, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not stopped the yen's rise.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose to around 103.50 yen to the dollar last week, its highest level in in four years.  \par \par \uc2 It was quoted around 104.40 yen to the dollar on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's recent rise has worried some, however.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Policy Board member Hidehiko Haru said in an interview with Kyodo news agency on Saturday that Japan's recovery could slow if the yen appreciated sharply.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ acts in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the MOF's behest.  \par \par \uc2 But the government source said there was now a view among authorities that a strong yen would help ease the problem of rising costs of raw materials, most of which are imported.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's recovering economy and China's rapid economic growth have pushed up prices of such key industrial commodities as copper, aluminium and other metals as well as oil and coal, adding to the cost of doing business for Japanese companies.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to have been scaled back in recent weeks, although officials have repeatedly said that there has been no change in policy.  \par \par \uc2 Economics and Financial Services Minister Heizo Takenaka told a Sunday television programme that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent undesirable sudden moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. ($1=104.15 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040405e045000ed\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
