\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. Treasuries extend losses after payroll shock.  \b0\par\par\uc2 500 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 April 2004\par 06:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 5 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasuries slid in Asia on Monday in a market still suffering aftershocks from surprisingly strong U.S. payroll data, which abruptly ended the market's seven-month bull run.  \par \par \uc2 Treasuries had their steepest fall in more than five years on Friday after data showed U.S. nonfarm payrolls jumped 308,000 last month, the largest rise in four months and sharply above economists' average forecast of around 103,000.  \par \par \uc2 "Friday's figures have completely changed the market's image about payrolls," said Etsuko Yamashita, chief economist at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corp (SMBC).  \par \par \uc2 The data suddenly brought into question a scenario many market players had in mind - that U.S. labour conditions would remain sluggish, making the Fed keep its benchmark rate at one percent this year.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0345 GMT, the benchmark 10-year U.S. Treasury yield was around 4.17 percent, up from Friday's U.S. close around 4.15 percent and from 3.88 percent on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Yields on two-year notes , the most sensitive to thinking on official interest rates, climbed to 1.88 percent from 1.85 percent at the end of U.S. trade last week and from 1.63 percent on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 In Asia, mortgage-related selling pushed down Treasuries, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 "I think position adjustment will go on," said Manabi Miyake, a senior manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "Buying by foreign central banks also seems to have slowed recently," he said.  \par \par \uc2 During the past year, the Bank of Japan has bought a huge amount of U.S. Treasuries with dollars obtained as a result of massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , supporting U.S. Treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have sharply scaled down its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last few weeks.  \par \par \uc2 SIGN OF STABILITY?  \par \par \uc2 Still, some market players said Treasury yields might have risen enough to entice yield-hungry investors and that a further rise in yields is unlikely, at least for now.  \par \par \uc2 "The market will now try to see whether strong payroll figures will continue or if Friday's numbers were abnormal," said SMBC's Yamashita.  \par \par \uc2 "A further rise in Treasury yields is unlikely until the next payroll data and perhaps the Fed's policy meeting early next month," she said, noting that the Fed would want to see a few more months of solid recovery in jobs before taking any action.  \par \par \uc2 Eurodollar futures , which extended Friday's sharp losses in Monday Asian trade, have already priced in a Fed rate increase by September.  \par \par \uc2 A weak reading in future payroll data, or a suggestion by Fed officials that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not be in any hurry to raise rates, could prompt short-covering, traders also said.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't see the 10-year yield rising above 4.25 percent for now," said a trader at a major Japanese bank. "Tomorrow's auction of five-year notes will become the selling climax," he added.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040405e0450009e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
