\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar on firmer ground ahead of U.S. jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2004\par 14:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 2 (Reuters) - The dollar rose broadly ahead of an eagerly-awaited U.S. employment report on Friday, recovering almost a yen from four-year lows against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  set earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Monthly U.S. jobs figures have disappointed recently, sending the dollar reeling, but a strong employment component in Thursday's ISM manufacturing report has raised expectations that March payrolls data at 1330 GMT could be better.  \par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters last week expected 103,000 new jobs to have been created last month compared with 21,000 in February. However, dealers said the consensus was now closer to 120,000.  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations for the payrolls are flying quite high at the moment," said Carsten Fritsch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Commerzbank in Frankfurt.  \par \par \uc2 By the European midsession, the dollar was up more than half a percent at 104.22 yen . The euro also succumbed to the dollar's firmer tone, shedding almost half a percent on the day to $1.2302 .  \par \par \uc2 Job creation has been the weak spot in the U.S. economic recovery and investors believe the Fed is unlikely to raise interest rates until the employment market has perked up.  \par \par \uc2 Low rates of return on dollar deposits have played a key role in the dollar's recent downtrend as investors have sought higher-yielding assets elsewhere.  \par \par \uc2 YEN LOSES STEAM  \par \par \uc2 The yen paused on Friday after sharp gains earlier in the week, prompting speculation Japanese investors were buying foreign assets at the start of the fiscal year which began on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, against a backdrop of an improving economy, large foreign buying of Japanese stocks, and an apparent scaling back of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said it was only a matter of time before the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  resumed its climb.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen is just pausing for breath," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered. "We still see it going higher in the coming months."  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials were upbeat about the country's economic recovery on Friday but denied speculation that the improvement had led to a change in their policy of resisting a rise in the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The country's finance and economics ministers said the Bank of Japan's "tankan" business survey, released on Thursday, suggested Japan's export-led rebound was spreading to the lagging domestic sector.  \par \par \uc2 But Japan's top financial diplomat, Zembei Mizoguchi, said Tokyo's stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unchanged and that it would continue to act as needed in the market.  \par \par \uc2 In the euro zone, industry producer prices rose 0.1 percent on the month and were unchanged on the year in February.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  received a boost on Thursday after European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet indicated the bank was in no hurry to cut rates, wrong-footing many traders who had expected him to signal a rate cut in coming months.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040402e0420021v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
