\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Rising yen shatters myth of seasonal weakness.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Vidya Ranganathan  \par \uc2 884 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2004\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 2 (Reuters) - The yen has begun Japan's financial year at nearly four-year highs against the dollar, contradicting market talk of seasonal weakness and showing little effect from pressure of fresh Japanese investment overseas.  \par \par \uc2 Its rise last month had already defied conventional wisdom that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MOF) would be sure to keep it down ahead of Japan's financial year-end on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 It rose 4.7 percent in March to near 104 yen to the dollar, a big chunk of that rise being on the last day of the month, shattering traditional beliefs about Japanese flows and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 On Friday in Tokyo it was hovering just above 104 yen, having hit 103.40 yen last Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Unsurprised, analysts say the year-end factor that markets chatter about is a myth. That the yen has risen in April for nine out of the past 14 years is testimony to that.  \par \par \uc2 "The theory was that in April and October, the yen would weaken because local funds would be putting all their money back offshore after new asset allocations," said Michael Jansen, a strategist with National Australia Bank in Sydney.  \par \par \uc2 The March hypothesis was that companies and funds had to bring back money invested overseas to bolster balance sheets because yields at home were so low, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Exporters and funds would revalue their assets and liabilities at the year-end, and the view was that the authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling the yen to weaken it, and so help Japan Inc inflate its books.  \par \par \uc2 "A lot of seasonality is no longer apparent. It's a bit of an excuse to some extent to justify price action," Jansen said.  \par \par \uc2 "This year-end theory is a bit of a fiction in that sense."  \par \par \uc2 Most of Japan Inc, analysts say, now subscribes to global accounting rules that allow fund managers and corporations to consolidate their overseas and domestic books.  \par \par \uc2 Besides, their books are revalued almost everyday, not just at the financial year-end, and notional losses due to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations do not require a transfer of funds.  \par \par \uc2 "The Bank of Japan has traditionally adopted a role making sure that dollar/yen is supported into these repatriation of portfolio flows so that they don't undermine corporate balance sheets in Japan," said David Simmonds, a strategist with the Royal Bank of Scotland in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF determines \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, but \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are conducted by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ).  \par \par \uc2 "This whole game has been moving around a bit anyway and the idea that it all happens in March has changed," Simmonds said.  \par \par \uc2 INCONCLUSIVE EVIDENCE  \par \par \uc2 First, the numbers do not support the argument that the Japanese bring their money home in March and re-invest in April.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese invested a record net 1.58 trillion yen ($15.2 billion) in foreign bonds in the week ended March 26, although analysts say they brought home a net 1.8 trillion yen in the whole month.  \par \par \uc2 In March 2001 there was an outflow of 2.3 trillion followed by a small net inflow in April. Net inflows in March 2002 totalled 2.4 trillion and April saw an outflow of 2.8 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 In 2003, an outflow of 2.4 trillion in March continued into April, when outflows were a net 3.5 trillion.  \par \par \uc2 Some new theories suggest the repatriation now takes place in January, which some say explains the record \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that month, when the BOJ sold 7.1 trillion yen.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become volatile, making it more difficult to predict any yen seasonality. Markets suspect most of the 4.7 trillion yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March was done early in the month.  \par \par \uc2 "What does it matter what March and April normally look like? The overwhelming dominant fundamental is that volatility in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels has gone up enormously," Simmonds said.  \par \par \uc2 According to Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo, there have been only five occasions from 1992 to 2003 when the MOF has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in March. Average \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounts have been largest in September and October.  \par \par \uc2 The only fact that lends some credence to the fiscal year-end theory about the yen is that it rose only five times in the month of March between 1990 and 2003, Sasaki said. There has been no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the final day of March in the last six years.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts said the BOJ's motive for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be a rule that requires firms to revalue their foreign investment portfolios if the yen has moved over 15 percent in a year. The yen rose 13 percent in the last 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 Others said the authorities' only motive in weakening the yen at the year-end would be to help businesses in a struggling economy make more ambitious plans for the following year.  \par \par \uc2 "The theory was that the BOJ wanted to keep the dollar/yen rate as high as possible so that corporates looking to do their business planning for the new fiscal year will have a slightly cheaper yen level," said NAB's Jansen.  \par \par \uc2 "That will allow them to look at more financing for capital investment and employment and other stuff."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040402e042000so\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
