\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen buoyed by upbeat business survey, euro eyes ECB.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 10:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 1 (Reuters) - The yen pushed back towards this week's four-year high against the dollar and four-month high against the euro on Thursday after a survey showed Japanese businesses more upbeat than at any time in almost seven years.  \par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, slipped from the previous session's one-week peak against the dollar as caution set in ahead of an interest rate decision by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey showed large manufacturers felt business was better than at any time since June 1997. It also showed improved confidence among small firms and non-manufacturers, confirming the recovery is extending beyond exporters.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen has already seen a big rise but the Tankan suggests that there should be more gains to come," said Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein. In early European trade, the yen was up a quarter-percent against the dollar at 104.05 and a third of a percent against the euro at 127.92.  \par \par \uc2 The yen staged a stunning rally on Wednesday as investors sensed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was scaling back yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  going into the new fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB will announce its decision on interest rates at 1145 GMT and hold a news conference at 1230 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 ECB VERDICT AWAITED  \par \par \uc2 A warning on consumer spending from ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet last week hurt the euro by fanning speculation of an interest rate cut.  \par \par \uc2 But 58 of 60 analysts polled by Reuters this week said they expected rates to be left at 2.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "Very few people are expecting an ECB rate cut today but the press conference may reinforce expectations of a cut in May or June," said Dannesboe.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.15 percent from the U.S. close at $1.2292 at 0810 GMT, having hit a one-week high above $1.2320 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pared early losses after data on Thursday showed euro zone manufacturers put in their best performance in over three years.  \par \par \uc2 The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers Index, which measures business activity in manufacturing, rose to 53.3 last month from 52.5 in February, wrong-footing economists who predicted a fall to 52.3.  \par \par \uc2 That pushed the index to its highest level since December 2000 and kept it above the 50 line dividing growth from contraction for a seventh month.  \par \par \uc2 "It's calming fears of a surprise rate cut," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  economist at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 TANKAN ENCOURAGING  \par \par \uc2 The Tankan's main diffusion index for big manufacturers came in at plus 12, the highest since June 1997 and the fourth straight quarter of improvement.  \par \par \uc2 Many dealers were also struck by the average dollar/yen rate that Japanese companies expected for the new financial year that kicked off on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Large manufacturers forecast an average dollar rate of 108.43 yen in fiscal 2004/05, versus the current level around 104.00.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ said roughly 40 percent of respondents submitted their results by March 9, when the dollar was at 111.21 yen, and 90 percent of responses arrived by March 22, when the dollar closed at 106.97 yen.  \par \par \uc2 But many analysts said that Japanese companies were capable of enduring a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 "In the second half of the previous financial year, most companies revised down the dollar/yen rate but revised up their earnings forecasts, suggesting they are much more resilient to a strong yen," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040402e041000ws\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
