\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Euro extends gains after ECB no-change decision.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 14:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, April 1 (Reuters) - The euro extended gains to fresh one-week highs against the dollar on Wednesday after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left interest rates unchanged, as expected.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has in recent days clawed back losses made last week on dovish comments by ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet, and 58 of 60 analysts polled by Reuters this week saw no change in current rates of 2.0 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Investors will focus on Trichet's comments at the ECB's 1230 GMT news conference, after he warned on sluggish consumer spending early last week.  \par \par \uc2 "The rate decision was largely anticipated," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank.  \par \par \uc2 "The press conference is going to be interesting to see if he (Trichet) does take a more dovish line and starts opening the door for a rate move in the future. Our view is they will probably go in June."   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro was trading at $1.2324  at 1152 GMT, steady from the U.S.\par close and off post-ECB highs of $1.2356. The euro was down 0.40 percent\par against the broadly strong yen at 127.88 yen          , compared with\par four-month lows set on Wednesday of 126.60.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was down 0.50 percent against the yen at 103.82 yen , compared with four-year lows set on Wednesday of 103.38.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen staged a stunning rally on Wednesday as investors sensed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was scaling back yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  going into the new fiscal year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It remained buoyant on Thursday after the quarterly "tankan" survey showed Japanese businesses more upbeat than at any time in almost seven years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. initial jobless claims at 1330 GMT are forecast at 340,000, compared with 339,000 the previous week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Institute of Supply Management manufacturing index at 1500 GMT is expected to dip to 60.0 in March from 61.4 in February, still firmly in above-50 expansionary territory.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve governors Ben Bernanke and Donald Kohn and Chicago Fed President Michael Moskow speak later.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow tours institutions in New Mexico and Arizona on Thursday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EURO ZONE, JAPAN BUSINESS UP  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Upbeat euro zone manufacturing data earlier on Thursday started a rise in the euro and removed lingering talk of a euro zone rate cut.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Reuters Eurozone Purchasing Managers Index, which measures business activity in manufacturing, rose to 53.3 last month from 52.5 in February, wrong-footing economists who predicted a fall to 52.3.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That pushed the index to its highest level since December 2000 and kept it above the 50 line dividing growth from contraction for a seventh month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The PMI fuelled a move higher in the euro and threw cold water on any expectations of the ECB cutting," said Jane Foley, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We had a very good tankan overnight which added to a move that had already taken place in the yen."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey, the main diffusion index for big manufacturers came in at plus 12, the highest since June 1997 and the fourth straight quarter of improvement.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The survey also showed improved confidence among small firms and non-manufacturers, confirming the recovery is extending beyond exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese officials kept markets guessing about the extent of yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese authorities, after more than a year of heavy official action to curb potentially export-damaging yen strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi repeated in European trading hours that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will continue \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when exchange rates move out of line with fundamentals.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi said an excessively appreciating yen was undesirable given current conditions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Elsewhere, the Swedish crown strengthened half a percent to one-week highs against the euro after Sweden's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  cut interest rates by 50 basis points to historic lows of 2.0 percent, matching euro zone rates, but said the easing cycle was coming to an end.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040401e041002a9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
