\par\par \b \uc2 Yen upbeat on tankan but caution sets in on US jobs.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Mariko Hayashibara  \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 08:23\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, April 1 (Reuters) - The yen was buoyant on Thursday, briefly rising near a four-year peak against the dollar on a broad improvement in Japanese corporate sentiment.  \par \par \uc2 The closely watched quarterly "tankan" survey by the Bank of Japan (BOJ) showed that the main diffusion index for big manufacturers came in at plus 12, the highest since June 1997 and the fourth straight quarter of improvement.  \par \par \uc2 But dealers trod carefully at the start of the new financial year in Japan as they awaited Friday's key U.S. jobs data and, to some extent, the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate decision later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 "A round of tankan-inspired yen buying cooled down and people started preparing for any surprise from U.S. payrolls data like what happened last month," said Kota Kimura, assistant manager at Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Disappointing U.S. non-farm payroll figures released in early March sent the dollar reeling as the data was seen lessening the chances of the U.S. Federal Reserve hiking interest rates sooner than later.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  then \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to help push the dollar above 112 yen.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the dollar slipped to a low of 103.85 yen after the tankan survey was released but recovered to around 104.15 yen by 0611 GMT as the key Nikkei stock average trimmed gains.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a four-year low of 103.40 yen on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.6 percent at 127.55 yen.  \par \par \uc2 TANKAN ENCOURAGING  \par \par \uc2 Still, many analysts were confident that it was a matter of time before the yen breaks 100 per dollar and felt that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not be a serious hurdle to pushing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Japan's economic fundamentals are obviously good with the yen looking set to move towards the 100 per dollar level, brushing aside any yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Kouki Muroi, deputy manager at Aozora Bank's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading group.  \par \par \uc2 Of the tankan, yen bulls were heartened to see that brightening corporate sentiment in Japan was spreading to non-manufacturing and smaller firms.  \par \par \uc2 Many dealers were also struck by the average dollar/yen rate that Japanese companies expected for the new financial year that kicked off on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Large manufacturers forecast an average dollar rate of 108.43 yen in fiscal 2004/05, versus the current level around 104.15.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that given recent yen strength most companies must have revised down the level and were concerned how it affected their earnings outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The BOJ said roughly 40 percent of respondents submitted their results by March 9, when the dollar was at 111.21 yen and 90 percent of responses arrived by March 22, when the dollar closed at 106.97 yen.  \par \par \uc2 But many analysts said that Japanese companies were capable of enduring a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 "In the second half of the previous financial year, most companies revised down the dollar/yen rate but revised up their earnings forecasts, suggesting they are much more resilient to a strong yen," said Junya Tanase, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "Japanese companies would have no problem with the dollar/yen at the 105 level, or even 100 to 95 level," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Such a view is underscored by foreign investors' persistence in pouring capital into Japan's assets.  \par \par \uc2 Latest data from the Finance Ministry on weekly capital flows suggests that net Japan stock purchases by foreigners from March 1 to March 26 totalled 2.58 trillion yen ($24.75 billion).  \par \par \uc2 The monthly volume would be the highest since the ministry started keeping data in March 1998, so long as foreigners did not sell stocks heavily in the remaining three business days of the month.  \par \par \uc2 The market is also looking to the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy meeting. Its decision on interest rates is due at 1145 GMT, followed by a news conference with ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet at 1230 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was down 0.3 percent on the day at $1.2270 on caution over the ECB's rate decision. ($1=104.23 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040401e041001ap\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
