\par\par \b \uc2 Yen up as tankan confirms Japan economy improving.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 02:54\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, April 1 (Reuters) - The yen rose near a four-year peak against the dollar on Thursday after a closely watched survey showed broad improvement in Japanese corporate sentiment.   \par \par \uc2  The main diffusion index (DI) for big manufacturers in the Bank of Japan's quarterly "tankan" survey was plus 12 in March, the highest since June 1997.   \par \par \uc2  "Taking into account the change in the way the tankan survey is done, the big manufacturers' DI of plus 12 is the highest since the bursting of the bubble. So the data is very positive," said Hiroshi Yokotani, economist at Tokio Marine Asset Management.   \par \par \uc2  Beginning with the latest survey, the BOJ has revised its polling method, adding more industries and rejigging some categories.   \par \par \uc2  As of 0015 GMT, the dollar was around 104.05 yen, down about 0.2 percent from late U.S. levels.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit a four-year low of 103.40 yen on Wednesday, pushed down by speculation that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be scaling back its massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well growing optimism about the Japanese economy.   \par \par \uc2  The euro also slipped to around 128.00 yen from around 128.40 yen in late New York trade.   \par \par \uc2  The common European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed versus the dollar, parking slightly above $1.23 The euro gained more than one percent to a one-week high of $1.2323 on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  A short-lived rumour that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan had suffered a heart attack, a downbeat report on U.S. Midwestern business and dimming expectations of a rate cut from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday all helped the euro.   \par \par \uc2  A Fed spokeswoman said Greenspan had not suffered a heart attack and was fine.   \par \par \uc2  Still, the effect on the dollar was lasting, thanks to dollar-bearish sentiment generated after the National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago's business barometer showed slower-than-expected expansion in March and contraction in the jobs component of the index.   \par \par \uc2  The overall index indicated expansion for an 11th straight month, coming in at 57.6 in March versus 63.6 in February. Economists had expected it to decline only to 61.5.   \par \par \uc2  More worrying for those banking on a pick-up in hiring that has so far eluded the broader economy, the employment component of the index dropped sharply to 49.2 from 54.8   \par \par \uc2  The market is now bracing for weekly first-time jobless claims, due at 1330 GMT, keenly watched for a clue about Friday's important U.S. payrolls data.   \par \par \uc2  The market is also looking to the ECB's policy meeting. Its decision is due at 1145 GMT, followed by a news conference with ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet at 1230 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040401e04100023\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
