\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Caution Rules Markets  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,151 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 07:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY:  Little Incentive to Buy Stocks \par  \par DJIA          10357.70  loss 24.00      dn 0.2% \par NASDAQ        1994.22   loss 5.41       dn 0.3% \par S&P 500       1126.21   loss 0.79       dn 0.1% \par Dow Future    10344.00  loss 6.000      dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1141.50   unchanged \par S&P Future    1125.50   gain 0.50       up 0.0% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 3.84% down 0.06 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street edged modestly lower Wednesday, closing a turbulent quarter on a down note as the government reported weaker-than-expected growth in the manufacturing sector and OPEC signaled it would move ahead with a planned production cut. Treasurys gained in price.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Investors sought safer positions after two days of robust gains, and ahead of a much-anticipated jobs report due Friday. In addition, the Institute for Supply Management was set to release its report on manufacturing activity on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "People are a little afraid to step out ahead of those two numbers, especially after the magnitude of these two days of gains," said Russ Koesterich, U.S. equity strategist with State Street Corp. in Boston. "You've had a nice run-up since last week's lows, so it's time to take a little bit off the table."  \par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department reported a 0.3% rise in factory orders for February, a welcome bounce after January's 0.9% drop. The rebound wasn't as strong as the 1.5% increase economists had forecast, however.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for durable goods - manufactured products such as automobiles, household appliances and computers - rose by 2.5% in February. But nondurable goods, such as food and clothing, fell 2%.  \par \par \uc2 OPEC signaled Thursday that it would go ahead with a scheduled 4% production cut, despite surging fuel costs. Analysts said the move could drive crude oil prices above $40 a barrel, which would mean higher costs at the pump for U.S. drivers.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys snapped a four-session losing streak Wednesday after a report showing softer-than-expected regional manufacturing activity established a solid bid in the market early in the day.  \par \par \uc2 Buying related to month-end index matching emerged later in the afternoon, which helped actively traded Treasurys close trade at session highs.  \par \par \uc2 The weakening of the dollar versus the yen, meanwhile, caused little commotion on trading desks despite the greenback's influence on the bonds in recent months.  \par \par \uc2 The jobs picture continued to play a big role in trading Wednesday, with the Labor Department scheduled to release its employment situation report for March on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The most important indicator is the monthly employment report," said Gary Pollack, managing director of fixed income at Deutsche Bank Private Wealth Management in New York. "That's been setting the tone in the bond market every month."  \par \par \uc2 "Whisper" forecasts in the 150,000 area for payrolls have already begun to circulate around trading desks, but few are willing to boldly position or talk loudly about a much stronger number, considering the stubborn sluggishness in hiring reported so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 "I think people are embarrassed about being optimistic on the job market," Pollack said. "But in the back of everyone's mind, they're wondering if this is going to be the breakout report," he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY:  Tokyo Stocks Directionless \par  \par USD-Yen             104.36       gain  0.11    up 0.1% \par AUD-USD             0.7620       loss  0.0042  dn 0.6% \par Nikkei 225          11704.18     loss  11.21   dn 0.1% \par Hang Seng           12654.97     loss  26.70   dn 0.2% \par JGB Yield           1.4250% down 0.0250 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo's key stock index meandered Thursday morning as investors welcomed an upbeat \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  business survey released on the first day of Japan's new fiscal year. The dollar was up against the Japanese yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the stock market, prices edged up in early trading on the Bank of Japan's better-than-expected tankan survey of business sentiment for March, released earlier Wednesday, the start of Japan's new fiscal year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The survey showed Japanese corporate sentiment improved in all sectors in the three months through March, in another indication of a Japanese economic recovery.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tankan index for big manufacturers for the latest quarter stood at plus 12, up from 7 in the revised previous reading in December and above expectations of plus 10 by analysts surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and Nikkei News.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tankan indexes measure the percentage of companies saying business conditions are better, minus the percentage saying they are worse.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Nikkei index, however, narrowed its early gains as investors grew cautious about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements and sold technology and other exporter issues. Electronics giants Matsushita Electric and Sony, as well as automakers Honda Motor, Nissan and Toyota were among losers Thursday morning.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A weaker dollar means Japanese exporters earn fewer yen from their sales overseas.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trading, the dollar remained just above the new four-year-low it hit Wednesday as investors tried to figure out whether Japanese monetary authorities would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK:  Stocks Have Little Supoort \par  \par Euro-USD            1.2278      loss  0.0040  dn 0.3% \par Stlg-USD            1.8415      loss  0.0045  dn 0.3% \par USD-Franc           1.2690      gain  0.0045  up 0.3% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares, government bonds and the euro are destined this session to hang in narrow ranges pending the ECB's rate decision.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are unlikely to make much headway this session given the touchy issues of ECB interest rates and U.S. payrolls yet to be resolved.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were lower Wednesday on the final trading day of the financial quarter as disappointing U.S. data pulled bourses off opening gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. manufacturing data rekindled fears of a slowing recovery in Europe's largest exporting market. Both the March U.S. Chicago Purchasing Managers Index and February Factory orders data fell short of economists' forecasts.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strategists at Barclays Private Clients said the market remains in a wait-and-see mode ahead of the ECB interest rate verdict Thursday and U.S. employment figures Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While rates in the euro zone are likely to fall at some stage, I don't think it will be" Thursday, said Peter Dixon, economist at Commerzbank.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be forced to rethink its position in May, at which point first-quarter gross domestic product data will provide insight into the pace of euro-zone growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil stocks were in strong focus Wednesday after OPEC, which pumps about a third of the world's oil, said it will reduce its output ceiling by 1 million barrels per day starting in April.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bonds start a flat session ahead of the ECB's rate decision, which could cause some turmoil if it comes in against expectations of unchanged rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds spiked higher late Wednesday, after the U.S. March Chicago purchasing managers' index came in below expectations. The index fell to 57.6 in March, against expectations of a decline to 61 from 63.6 in February.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The employment component fell to 49.2 in March from 54.8 in February. This suggested that the market might have to wait a bit longer for U.S. non-farm payrolls figures to point to a turnaround in the labor market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The non-farm payrolls release, due Friday, is one of the most closely watched indicators for gauging the health of the U.S. labor market and how close the Federal Reserve might be to a rate hike.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market's been concerned that the mostly positive labor picture in recent survey data hasn't been reflected yet in payrolls.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We've been seeing general improvement in all the labor market indicators except payrolls," said Monique Wong, fixed-income analyst at UBS in London. "People are looking for confirmation that the payrolls number will likely be big."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro begins the session somewhat lower against the dollar ahead of lingering hopes that the ECB will cut rates. But players are increasingly wary of testing the euro's downside much more in case the ECB leaves rates unchanged. The dollar slid across the board Wednesday, hitting a four-year low against the yen as investors tested the resolve of Japanese monetary authorities to limit yen strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , would step back from the market at the end of Japan's fiscal year had been fueled by several recent news reports citing unnamed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sources as saying the policy of large-scale \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was over. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's fiscal year runs to March 31.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When that speculation appeared to ring true, investors took advantage of the apparent lack of official bids early in Tokyo trade, pushing the dollar down over two yen to Y103.44, a level not seen since the spring of 2000.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They just let it go, and it went," said Greg Anderson, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at ABN Amro Bank in Chicago. "If you pull out your bids and the impetus is lower, (the yen) is going to surge until you put your bids back in."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were also encouraged by less-than-convincing saber-rattling by Japanese officials. Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki acknowledged that the yen's surge "isn't small," and warned that the government would "continue to watch the market and market trends carefully."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was also tripped up by trader speculation about Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan's health, but the rumors were quickly quashed by the Fed, which said the chairman is "fine."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had been gaining in recent sessions on speculation that the ECB would cut interest rates when it meets Thursday, but those expectations have also died down. [ 01-04-04 0503GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Only two out of 26 banks in a recent Dow Jones Newswires survey expected the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to lower rates this week, though views were split on the outlook for rates into the end of the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Thursday, April 1: Manufacturing Data \par GMT \par 0600  GER  Feb     Retail Sales \par 0645  FRA  Mar     Consumer Sentiment Survey \par 0700  GER          ECB Governing Council Meeting \par 0750  FRA  Mar     Mfg PMI \par 0755  GER  Mar     Mfg PMI \par 0830  UK   Mar     Manufacturing Purchasing Managers Index \par 0900  FRA          QAT-Auction \par 1000  FRA  March   New Vehicle Registrations \par 1145  GER          ECB Interest Rate Decision \par 1330  US   Mar 27  Initial Jobless Claims \par 1330  US   Feb     Construction Spending \par 1500  US   Mar     ISM Manufacturing Index \par 1500  US   Mar 20  DJ-BTM Business Barometer \par 1530  US           Fed Gov Bernanke \par 1700  US           Chicago Fed Pres Moskow \par 1730  US           Fed Gov Kohn speaks on monetary policy   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 01-04-04 0503GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Scor (SCO): FY Earnings \par Consensus net loss (JCF, 11 analysts): EUR297M (EUR455M loss) \par Note: FY unlikely to yield negative surprises as company made EUR241M new provisions against US policy losses in 3Q. Earnings boosted by investment income from bond and real estate capital gains, analysts say. Eyes also on whether combined ratio comes in under 100%, which would benefit shares. \par  \par Gucci (GUC): 4Q Earnings \par Consensus 4Q net profit (JCF, 20 analysts): EUR122M (EUR95M) \par Consensus 4Q operating profit before goodwill, amort: EUR125M (EUR115M) \par Consensus revenue: EUR793M (EUR715M) \par Note:  Bear Stearns expects high store and advertising costs to hit operating profits at core Gucci and Yves St Laurent brands. \par  \par Barry Callebaut (BARN.EB): 1H Earnings \par Consensus net profit (DJ, 4 analysts): CHF88.5M (CHF75.9M) \par Consensus sales: CHF2.23B (CHF1.91B) \par Note:  The acquisitions of Brachs and Luijckx are consolidated for first time in half year figures, and company experiences positive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effects. \par  \par Alizyme (AZM.LN): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP11M (GBP10.5M loss) \par Note:  Analysts want to hear about progress of ATL-962, a drug for the ever expanding obesity market. \par  \par OTHER SCHEDULED EVENTS: \par  \par UCM Group (UCM.LN): FY Earnings \par Note: Jan 27, UCM said it expects profits for the year to Dec. 31, 2003 will be in the region of GBP1.6M pre-exceptional items and tax. \par  \par Carphone Warehouse (CPW.LN): 4Q Revenue \par  \par Christie Group (CTG.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par IFG Group (IFG.DB): FY Earnings \par  \par Raiffeisen (RZO.YY): FY Earnings \par  \par Elma (ELMA): FY Earnings \par  \par COS (CSS.EB): FY Earnings \par  \par Hugo Boss (BOS.XE): FY Earnings, Press Conference \par  \par Aareal Bank (ARL.XE): FY Earnings \par  \par ING's Diba Bank (INK.YY): FY Earnings, Press Conference \par  \par Austrian Air (AUA.VI): FY Earnings \par  \par Gruner & Jahr (BRT.YY): FY Earnings, Press Conference \par  \par ATU: FY Earnings, Press Conference \par  \par FHB (FHB.BU): Fotex included in benchmark BUX index basket \par  \par Hagemeyer (35547.AE): Annual Report \par  \par Haslemere (28933.AE): AGM \par  \par Svenska Cellulosa Aktiebolaget (SCA-B.SK): AGM \par  \par Cargill (CAG.YY): EU Antitrust Decision on Cargill's planned buy of Bank Cargill Agriculture Holdings Limited. \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 01-04-04 0503GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020040401e0410004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
