\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips on Greenspan rumor, Chicago data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 April 2004\par 00:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  CHICAGO, March 31 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Wednesday, hit by a short-lived rumor that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan had suffered a heart attack and by news of a weaker than expected report on U.S. Midwestern business.   \par \par \uc2  "The (euro) market rallied after the rumors of Greenspan came around," said Bob Priore, desk manager at Carr Futures in Chicago and the Midwest business data only added steam to that rally of the euro against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  The Greenspan talk sent the euro as high as $1.2323, a gain of more than 1 percent. The dollar fell more than 1.1 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.2654 francs in the immediate aftermath of the Greenspan rumor.   \par \par \uc2  A Fed spokeswoman first said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not comment on the rumor, then later said Greenspan had not suffered a heart attack and was fine. Nevertheless, the effect on the dollar was lasting, thanks to dollar-bearish sentiment generated by the unexpectedly weak Midwest business data.   \par \par \uc2  Investors view the euro and the Swiss franc as safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when news from the United States creates uncertainty.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers said fading expectations of an interest rate cut from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday also supported the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Early in New York trade, the dollar weakened on news the National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago's business barometer showed slower-than-expected expansion in March and contraction in the jobs component of the index.   \par \par \uc2  The overall index indicated expansion for an 11th straight month, to 57.6 in March from 63.6 in February, although economists had expected it to decline only to 61.5.   \par \par \uc2  Separately, the government said U.S. factory orders rose 0.3 percent in February after falling a revised 0.9 percent in January. Wall Street had expected a 1.5 percent gain.   \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, traders will watch for data on weekly first-time jobless claims, February producer prices and U.S. manufacturing.   \par \par \uc2  In late New York trade, the euro was up 1.2 percent at $1.2308. The dollar was down 1.24 percent at 1.2654 Swiss francs.   \par \par \uc2  JAPAN'S STRENGTH   \par \par \uc2  The dollar plummeted against the yen, shattering a key psychological barrier around 105 yen to hit a four-year low as investors tested a reduction in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it entered its new fiscal year.   \par \par \uc2  The 105 level had been considered the tolerance threshold for Japanese policy-makers aiming to prevent a strengthening yen from jeopardizing the country's export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2  Comments from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki that Japan had grown resilient to the stronger yen gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  another boost in the European session, lifting it to 103.45 yen to the dollar, its highest point since April 2000.   \par \par \uc2  In late New York trade, the dollar was down 1.3 percent to 104.27 yen, above session lows of 103.45 yen, which was a four-year low and marked its sharpest one-day fall against the yen this year.   \par \par \uc2  The euro also succumbed to broad-based yen strength, falling to session lows at 126.63, its lowest since last November. The euro ended Wednesday down 0.19 percent at 128.34.   \par \par \uc2  "It certainly appears to be a decline, at least a reduction in BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is favoring a stronger yen," Lynch said. "From here we look at the tankan survey coming out tonight and see if it concurs with other indicators that show that Japan's economy is improving."   \par \par \uc2  Expectations of continuing improvement in Japan's economic outlook also supported the yen, as investors positioned for a robust quarterly "tankan" business survey from the Bank of Japan at 6:50 p.m. EST (2350 GMT). (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v002xa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
