\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar dips on Greenspan rumor, Chicago data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 868 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 21:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, dateline; previous NEW YORK)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, March 31 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Wednesday under the weight of a quickly denied rumor that Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan had suffered a heart attack and news of a weaker-than-expected report on U.S. Midwest business.  \par \par \uc2 A Fed spokeswoman soon said Greenspan had not suffered a heart attack and was fine.  \par \par \uc2 "The (euro) market rallied after the rumors of Greenspan came around," said Bob Priore, desk manager at Carr Futures in Chicago, noting that the Midwest business data only added steam to a euro rally already in progress.  \par \par \uc2 The Greenspan talk sent the euro as high as $1.2323 , a gain of more than 1 percent. The dollar fell more than 1.1 percent against the Swiss franc to 1.2654 francs in the immediate aftermath of the Greenspan rumor. But the greenback soon recovered to 1.2675 francs.  \par \par \uc2 Investors view the euro and particularly the Swiss franc as safe-haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  any time news from the United States creates uncertainty.  \par \par \uc2 Also in early New York trade, the dollar weakened on news that the National Association of Purchasing Management-Chicago's business barometer showed slower-than-expected expansion in March. The index indicated expansion for an 11th straight month, but at a much slower pace than expected. The Chicago purchasing managers' index fell to 57.6 in March from 63.6 in February. Economists had expected the index to decline only to 61.5.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. factory orders rose 0.3 percent in February after falling a revised 0.9 percent in January. Wall Street had expected a 1.5 percent gain.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the dollar gave a muted reaction to data showing expanding business activity in New York City for the seventh straight month in March. The National Association of Purchasing Management-New York's business conditions index rose to 271.8 in March from 267.2 the month before and 257.3 in January.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN'S STRENGTH  \par \par \uc2 The dollar plummeted against the yen on Wednesday, shattering a key psychological barrier around 105 yen to hit a four-year low as investors tested a reduction in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  going into the new fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 The 105 level had previously been considered the tolerance threshold for Japanese policymakers wishing to prevent a strengthening yen from jeopardizing the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Comments from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki that Japan had grown resilient to the stronger yen gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  another boost in the European session, lifting it to 103.45 yen to the dollar, its highest point since April 2000.  \par \par \uc2 "The comments from officials overnight also fed into sentiment from the market's perspective that there is perhaps more opportunity to sell dollar/yen at these levels," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.  \par \par \uc2 At midday in New York, the dollar was down 1.31 percent against the yen at 104.23 above session lows of 103.45 yen, which was a four-year low and marked its sharpest one-day fall against the yen this year.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also succumbed to broad-based yen strength, falling to session lows at 126.63 , its lowest since last November.  \par \par \uc2 "It certainly appears to be a decline, at least a reduction in BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is favoring a stronger yen," Lynch said. "From here we look at the tankan survey coming out tonight and see if it concurs with other indicators that show that Japan's economy is improving."  \par \par \uc2 Expectations of continuing improvement in Japan's economic outlook also supported the yen, as investors positioned for a robust quarterly "tankan" business survey from the Bank of Japan at 6:50 p.m. EST (2350 GMT).  \par \par \uc2 Data on Wednesday showed Japan threw more than 4.7 trillion yen at the foreign exchanges in March, bringing its year-to-date \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to more than 15 trillion yen. (Additional reporting by Manuela Badawy in New York).   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 1829 GMT. All data taken from Reuters\par calculated from the levels at 4:30 p.m. (2230 GMT) in the\par previous New York session.\par                       Last  US Close    %      YTD     2003\par                             30 March  Change %Change    Close\par -------------------------------------------------------------\par Euro/dlr             1.2297   1.2162   +1.11   -2.23   1.2578\par Dlr/yen              104.17   105.64   -1.39   -2.93   107.31\par Euro/yen             128.13   128.58   -0.35   -5.12   135.05\par Dlr/swiss            1.2662   1.2813   -1.18   +2.09   1.2403\par Stg/dlr              1.8415   1.8261   +0.84   +3.17   1.7850\par Dlr/cad              1.3136   1.3060   +0.58   +1.32   1.2965\par Aus/dlr              0.7636   0.7532   +1.38   +1.60   0.7516\par Euro/swiss           1.5571   1.5593   -0.14   -0.19   1.5601\par Euro/stg             0.6674   0.6660   +0.21   -5.29   0.7047\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All spots  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo spots  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Europe spots  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volatilities  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\u