\par\par \b \uc2 LME aluminium ends firmer, targets $1,750/T.  \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 19:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     LONDON, March 31 (Reuters) - London Metal Exchange (LME) aluminium\par ended firmer in trading on Wednesday because of a surge in Japanese buying after the yen strengthened\par against the dollar, traders said.\par     "It's been the ally (aluminium) story all day," an LME trader said\par     "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movement was the main factor, prompting Japanese buying and Far East and European\par consumer buying in general. Once the market broke through $1,700 a tonne it triggered some stop-loss\par buying, and now, in a technical sense, the market is targeting the recent high of $1,750."\par     The yen smashed through a key psychological barrier to hit a four-year high against the dollar\par on Wednesday as investors sensed \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was scaling back yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  going into the\par new fiscal year.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Comments from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki that Japan had grown resilient\par to the stronger yen gave the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  an added boost in the European session, lifting it to 103.45\par versus the dollar -- its highest since April 2000.\par     Aluminium built on its successful break of $1,685 a tonne resistance on Tuesday and $1,700\par earlier in the day. Three months futures ended at  $1,728 versus $1,685 at Tuesday's kerb close.\par The market  hit a four-year high of $1,775 a tonne in February.\par     Analysts said that a close above $1,690 could spark renewed fund interest, while a predicted\par 500,000 tonne supply deficit and eastern European stockpiling was eating into exchange inventories.\par     Copper        ended the rings at $2,980 a tonne, up from $2,952 at Tuesday's kerb.\par     "Dollar weakness encouraged some buying and there's been some short-covering," the trader\par said.\par     In the short term, copper's failure to consolidate a break above $3,000 on Tuesday could\par see the metal sticking within its recent broad $2,900 to $3,000 range.\par     The market would look to non-farm payroll data on Friday for fresh direction and a decision\par on interest rates by the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (ECB) on Thursday, analysts said.\par     "The ECB decision is not a big deal, there is much more emphasis on the U.S. data," the trader\par said.\par     The market remained characterised by dwindling warehouse stocks and tightness in nearby spreads,\par although the cash to threes had eased to $93.5 backwardation from above $100 on Tuesday.\par     Tin        was firmer at $8,475 from $8,320 but was off Tuesday's fresh 14-1/2-year high\par of $8,480. Lead  was at $822, up $5, and zinc        rose $7 to $1,110.\par     Nickel        added $125 to $14,000 after dropping earlier on bank selling, traders said.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v002bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
