\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen storms higher, hits four-year peak vs dollar.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 669 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2004\par 14:36\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, March 31 (Reuters) - The yen smashed through a key psychological barrier to hit a four-year high against the dollar on Wednesday as investors sensed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was scaling back yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  going into the new fiscal year.  \par \par \uc2 The yen rose at breakneck speed after hurdling 105 per dollar, previously considered the tolerance threshold for Japanese policymakers keen to prevent a strengthening yen jeopardising the country's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Comments from Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki that Japan had grown resilient to the stronger yen gave the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  another boost in the European session, lifting it to 103.45 to the dollar - its highest since April 2000.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's break through 105 yen implies more acceptance of yen strength from Japanese policymakers," said Bilal Hafeez, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Economic fundamentals are pointing to further yen strength and it will be harder for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 By 1215 GMT, the yen was off session peaks at 103.85 per dollar but still on track for its sharpest one-day rise against the greenback this year.  \par \par \uc2 Data on Wednesday showed Japan threw more than 4.7 trillion yen at the foreign exchanges in March, bringing its year-to-date \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to more than 15 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro outperformed the dollar but also succumbed to broad-based yen strength, falling more than one percent to 126.63, its lowest since last November.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN OPTIMISM  \par \par \uc2 Expectation of continuing improvement in Japan's economic outlook also supported the yen as investors positioned for a robust quarterly "tankan" business survey from the Bank of Japan at 2350 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll forecast the survey would reveal heightened optimism among big manufacturers who are benefiting from robust exports and high-tech demand.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of optimism about the Japanese economy. The Nikkei had a good run over the last 12 months and we have the tankan overnight which is expected to show further improvement," said Ryan Shea, senior international economist at Bank One in London.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, he said it was "premature" to expect the Bank of Japan to step out of the market completely.  \par \par \uc2 The 105 level in dollar/yen had long been seen as crucial for Japanese officials, worried a stronger yen could derail the export-led recovery that is taking hold in Japan after a decade of stagnation.  \par \par \uc2 Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank agreed the threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not disappeared.  \par \par \uc2 "The line in the sand at 105 has gone, clearly, but I wouldn't be surprised to see another one above 100," he said.  \par \par \uc2 EURO RECOVERS FOOTING VS DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 The euro was up half a percent against the dollar at $1.2225, slightly below earlier one-week highs but up almost two cents from this year's low hit on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 News of a fire at a BP Plc refinery in Texas bolstered the euro against the dollar in early trade, particularly after the FBI last week warned of a security threat to refiners in that area.  \par \par \uc2 BP said the cause of the fire was unknown but added it did not appear to have been started intentionally.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said fading expectations of an interest rate cut from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday were also supporting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations for an ECB rate cut are now much reduced, and euro/dollar has benefited from that," said Adam Cole, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 The Chicago purchasing managers' index, due at 1500 GMT, is forecast to show a fall to 61.5 in March from 63.6 in February. U.S. factory orders, due at the same time, are expected to to show a rise of 1.5 percent in February after a 0.5 percent decline in the prior month.  \par \par \uc2 However, reaction to both sets of data may be muted ahead of Friday's key U.S. payroll report, dealers said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020040331e03v001jv\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\cl